      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0001" n="art0001">
        <p>IHE GRANDEST FETE IN GREAT BRITA N.</p>
        <p>SHREWSBURY</p>
        <p>GREAT FLORAL FETE</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY &amp; THURSDAY, AUG. 18th &amp; 19tb,1909.</p>
        <p>IzIE MOST MAGNIFICENT FLOWER SHOW rel. held in the United Kingdom. OVER £1,200 IN I,4IZES 3,000 Entries staged in 10 Monstre Marquees. Itraordinary Musical Treat by the CELEBRATED REGIMENTAL BANDS of H.M. COLDSTREANI GUARDS, 56 performers, H.M. IRISH GUARDS, 50 performers, AND THE /</p>
        <p>-ROPSHIRE YEOMANRY CAVALRY. THE GRANDEST SERIES OF SENSATIONAL PERFORMANCES IN EUROPE. 40 SEPARATE PERFORMANCES BY THE GREATEST ARTISTES OF TIIE DAY. DOUBLEI BALLOON ASCENTS.</p>
        <p>°APTIVE BALLOON, 500 to 1,000 Feet, by Spencer Bros, Aeronauts to the Crystal Palace. 11248 R LEAPING COMPETITION £llO in PRIZES. 44ESSRS. WILDER'S MAGNIFICENT FIREWORK DISPLAY.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL EXCURSION TRAINS FROM WARRINGTON, WIGAN and all parts.</p>
        <p>4f,_4l:3lissioN : Wednesday, One o'clock, 2s. 6d.; Five, Is.; Thursday, all day fronitleven, ONE I`III,LING ONLY.</p>
        <p>H. W. ADNITT, 1 Hon. W. W. NALINTON, JSecs. The Square, Shrewsbury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0002" n="art0002">
        <p>DUBLIN ALSO ENGLISH LAKES</p>
        <p>TEDCASTLE LINE: S.S. " BLACKROCK," "CUMBRIA," "EBLANA,' and "ADELA," from SOUTH Side, NELSON DOCK, Liverpool. Every Tuesday, Wednesday, Friday and Saturday, to DUBLIN. Return Saloon, 10s. • Steerage, ss. with Cheap Excursion Tickets EVERY 'SAILING DAY, to Bray, Wicklow Avoca, &amp;c., and on Fridays. and Saturdays to Killarney, Cork, Bundoran, Warre4oint, &amp;o. Fast S.S. " BLACKROCK " every Tuesday and Friday from Liverpool, and every Monday and Wednesday from Dublin. SPECIAL ROUND SEA CRUISE, per "Cumbria." Every Saturday, to Wbitehaven, giving 3 days for English Lakes,thence to Dublin,and beck to Liverpool (6 days, or within 2 months). Saloon, 205.. or including 14 meals, 425. Apply TEDCASTLE, McCORMICK &amp; CO., LTD, Liverpool. Telegrams, "Tedcastle." relephone 975.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0003" n="art0003">
        <head>H. N. HOUGHTON,</head>
        <p>H. N. HOUGHTON,</p>
        <p>For Ha o Bakes &amp; Forks, Machine Files, Potato Forks &amp;c,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>33, BRIDGE STREET, Warrington. Established over 100 years.</head>
        <p>33, BRIDGE STREET, Warrington. Established over 100 years.</p>
        <p>Telephone 385 x.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>IRONMONGERY, &amp;c.</head>
        <p>IRONMONGERY, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Tel. No. 123. ESTABLISHED 60 YEARS (in Warrington). Tel. No. 123. E. Forshaw &amp; Son, Ltd., ENGINEERS, BOILERMAKERS, IRON &amp; BRASS FOUNDERS, Mersey Boiler Works,</p>
        <p>Mersey Street, Warrington. Makers of IRON CASTINGS from the smallest size up to 20 tons weight. Clean Well-moulded and of the Highest Quality. To buy the best finished castings is to frequently save 50 per cent. in labour.</p>
        <p>BRASS CASTINGS (any weight) ; Gunmetal, Copper, Aluminium, Phosphor Bronze, Damaxine, Acid Proof Metal and all other Alloys.</p>
        <p>CONSTRUCTIONAL IRON AND STEEL WORK, Corrugated Iron Roofs and Buildings, Farmers' Hay Sheds, Barns, Store Sheds, &amp;c., Iron and Steel Tanks, Cisterns, Stoem Jacketted Pans, Boilers, &amp;e. Repairs by Experienced and Skilled Workmen to all kinds of Machinery and to Steam Boilers, Gas Engines, Traction Engines, Motor Cars, Dynamos, Steam Engines, Oil Engines, Road Rollers, Motor Waggons, Electric Motors, ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING. Speciality Work. Drawings and expert advice submitted free.</p>
        <p>Heavy Machining and Forging done for the trade. Turning up to 15 feet diameter. Planing up to 5 feet square. Slotting up to 18-inch stroke. Milling, Punching, Shearing and Bending, Pressing, Dishing, Fangling and Rivetting by hydraulic pressure. Unlimited Tackle and Special Appliances for efficiently dealing with Repairs and Breakdown work, accumulated during an establilisment of 60 years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>2 McDOUGALL'S Patetit SELF—RAISING FLOUR for delicious Yorkshire puddings.</head>
        <p>2 McDOUGALL'S Patetit SELF—RAISING FLOUR for delicious Yorkshire puddings.</p>
        <p>CALL RIGIIT3 RE3ERVED.j WHOSE HAND?</p>
        <p>EY ANNIE 0. TIIII3ITS.</p>
        <p>Mithor of "The Love that Won," "Hunted Down," "Hidden Hands," "The Finger of Fate," "Robes of Shame," &amp;e,</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: As a vessel is going down Olga Murray tells Dr. DaCre that, six years since, she murdered Stephen Henderson, of Greatbridge Hall, for which crime suspicion fell on Richard Sterry. If possible, the statement which she signs is to be kept from her lover, Sir Geoffrey Verriker. A bottle containing the document is flung into the sea. Sir Geoffrey, who rescues Olga from the wreck, insists on taking her to his mother at Kernseoto Hall, of which he has just come into possession by the death of his uncle.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dunston was to have married the uncle, and she plots with her daughter Julia to make the latter Lady Verriker by a marriage with Geoffrey or Geoffrey's cousin Edward. Dick Sterry owns the neighbouring • estate to the Verrikers, and is in love with Geoffrey's sister. As Geoffrey and Olga are nearing home Dick gets into the railway carriage. He lives near Greatbridge. He does not recognise Olga, but when Ethel Verriker introduces . him next day Olga faints. The Verrikers believe Olga's nerves -were upset by the ce4periences of the wreck. Sterry -Ekes not seem to know her. .He explains te Ethel Verriker, in order afterwards to de- clare his love, the fact that he - was tried for the, Greatbridge murder. -Henderson, wild character he had learnt, was to have mar vied Sterry's sister. The latter threatened him. and placed his own revolver on the ' table. He left it there. He repaired again to the Hall, at Lis sister's request, and only saw her again through prison bars. Ix.</p>
        <p>whose</p>
        <p>CHAPTER</p>
        <p>Ethel looked up, with her face white and strained in the growing dusk. A white mist was beginning now to creep over the garden. A dz.impn?.is stole over everything, over the hanging roses, over the yew hedge, over the green gram The chair grew clammy under her hands. The air felt cold upon her</p>
        <p>• face. "Through prison bars !" shs whispered. 4' What do you mean?" _ _</p>
        <p>Dick Sterry was not looking at her face. lie stared cut from under drawn brows across the garden. The scent of roses seemed to bang heavy in the air. "It is a wonder you have not heard about this before," he said, abruptly. "It is a wonder no one told you, but at the- time most people sympathised with me, I think, and perhaps that is why no one has said any- thing to you. Anyhow, let me go on." He came a little closer, and then suddenly sat down opposite to her, so .that she could see his face even in the growing dusk. "For Ruth's sake, I went back .to Great- bridge -Hall that night," he went on. "Dr. Collins spoke gravely to me about her. There was a hint of consumption, and worse—of heart diseae, which he had net suspected. It would be well, he said, to let her have every- thing she wished, if that were possible. "Well, her sole wi:;11 was. to sea Stephen Henderson, and perhaps you can imagine what it meant to me to go and fetch him.</p>
        <p>"But I went. When I reached the Hall it was almost dark, and to this day I don't know why, instead of knocking as usual at the front door, I went round the grass and *looked in at the billiard room window. Some ghastly fate sent me, I think—some cruel chance. Do you know that there are tim when one's luck runs badly, do what you ? When, in spite of everything you can do, all things work for ill for awhile. Well, every- thing went wrong with lal3 then. I lost everything that made life • worth living, anc; I came near to losing my life too." He paused, and drew a deep breath. For a moment it seemed hard work to go on. "I walked straight round the corner of the house, to the window," he said. "It was a French window, and opened on to the grass. It was open now, and as I turned the corner it seemed to me that a little dark figure slipped quickly out, and disappeared beLind the bushes on the lawn close by. "I couldn't be sure, for it was very dark at that corner of the lawn, but I otood for several minutes staring - curiously at the Lushes. It seemed to me that it was a woml.i. and she ran quickly, as though she was afraid. but nothing stirred, no one moved, and turned to look in at the window.</p>
        <p>"The room was only dimly . lighted. The gasses were only half turned on under the green globes over the billiard table, and the great room was half in shadow, and ampty. "Then swiftly my eyes grew accustomed to the dim light, and then I saw that the balls and cues were lying still on the table where Stephen had laft them earlier in the afte:- noon, and that his pipe and pouch were on a chair close by. "I went in, and then stood to listen. No- body seemed about, but suddenly. as I stoud, I fancied I heard a slow and titealthy rootstap, and then tha creaking of a door. "I. glanced across at the door, which opened into the smoking room, and went towards it. My first, thought was that ha was there, that he had seen me come,' and was sneaking away, but when I reach:A the door • I fouri the room in total, darkness, and then suddenly heard something panting close by. " I shall never forget that sound. I sto(Al quite still, scarcely breathing. It was some- where closa to me, faint and irregular, like a cat choking. - I stood listening for perhaps half a minute, and then a sudden horror of something I know not whae seized me, and .1 plunged my hand into my pocket, and stru a match.</p>
        <p>"It flared up sharply. I held. it high, aed looked round, and then my heart kaped. "Lying at full length. on the ground was Stephen Henderson, his face turned up to the ceiling, his eyes wide open. • ." The light( did down, and I sprang to. him. It was quite dark, as I said, but once the match had shewn me where he lay I could manage to distinguish him, even in the darkness, and I dropped to my knees bestie him, and b2gan unloosening his collar. " I had scarcely touched hint before he sprang upon inc. Whether he thought I was going to attack him or whether he thought I was the man who had already attacked him I don't know, but he sprang at my throat, and for half a minute fought like a madman. Then he gave a cry, and the next instant the butler was in the room with a light, and the :an Perry at his heels. " You can guess 'What followed. I had beet catight red-handed, as it was thought. MY own pistol was on the floor beside me, and Stephen was dying of a wound in his chest "The rest is all confusion. I was, of course, arrested at once. The police we" call!td in, and a doctor sent for, but before ne -could come Stephen Henderson was dead. He died without •a word, and I was charged with his murder, and nothing in the world would ever have got me off but one strange thing. When the doctor examined him finally for the cause of death- he found that he had two wounds—one an almost trifling one, in the shakier, from my pistol, and another, a deeper one, caused by ,a knife. It was the knife wound which had killed him, wad it was that which eventually got me off at ihe•trial. For that knife was found outside the window c‘n the lawn, and Stint Perry and the butler between them proved that I had not had time to stir frcm the body, let alone walk into the billiard room, which I must have done, in order to fling it to the spot „where it lay. But, anyhow, it was touch and go with Everything was against me—everything except the knife, and it was only because of that that I got off at last—at last." He stopped, and passed his baud across his , face. It was very grey. "I got off—l was set free after many wmks, wishing that they had hanged Me in- stead. -The shock of it killed my sister before my trial. It broke my heart. It turned me from a young man into an old one. It brought grey to my hair, and heaven JAnows what bate u?ss to my heart*. . . . They set me free with my name blackened and may life' ruined. They let me loose in Greatbridge, to be stared at, wondered at, doubted. To this day ha;l the peopl3 who remember the trial believe me guilty—manslaughter, perhaps, if they are kind, but guilty Of' a man's death all the same. Ask anyone you see.' There is only one answer. Thera was not enough evidence</p>
        <p>against me.- And there are some who think me lucky !" The word broke from him almost with a groan. Ethel got up suddenly, and held out her hand. ".Thank you for telling me," she said. "Oh, how dnadfully 1 feel for vou !" He caught her hand, and held it in a tight clutch. "Do you know why I have told you?" he cried, fiercely. "Do you know why I have spoken -of it? It is six years since Stephen Henderson's name passed my lips, six pars since I have spoken to anyone of my poor Ruth. Can you guess why I have spoken to you now?" , She lnsitated a moment, then quickly lifted her head and looked at him with her wide, open, honest grey 'eyes. You want me to believe .in you," she said. "Well, I do."</p>
        <p>He drew a quick breath, and for a mo- ment something that rose in his throat threat- ened to choke him.</p>
        <p>"I have spoken," he said, because I love you. I know 1 ought not to tell you so, but you are the first woman I have aver loved, and lam going to prove my innocence, and then —and then try to win you. These six years have gone by me like a dream. I have just lived, and that is all, until you came hare. At my trial, when Ruth was 'dying. I didn't care whether- I was hanged or not. I had nothing to live for, and nothing to care for, and I had helped to kill Ruth." "No, no, no ! " cried Ethel. "You can't say that ! Oh, you can't say that ! " " It seamed to me that I had," Dick said, slowly, " and the thought of it nanny sent me mad. I tried at first to live in Greatbridge,. and to live it down, but things were too strong for me, and at last I went abroad. "Twelve mouths ago I came back—not to Greatbridge, but to the house in which I am now living. Why I did it I do not know, but I had a f aeling while I was travelling that some day I might • like to try to prove my innocence, and that it would be good for me to be on the spot, if I could. "So that was why I came here—to wait and see if anything happened. I looked up my old sarvant and his wife, and they were glad to come to me, whether I was guilty or not guilty. So . here I am. and now, after to- night, all my work in life will be to prove my innocence of that crime. "When I have done it I—l—shall speak to you again." Ha turned away abruptly, and she sud- denly caught his arm. Her face seemed close to his in the dusk, and his heart throbbed heavily as he felt her close against him. Ho look ad- down.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Sterry—Dick—let me help you," she cried. He turned to her, as if he would have taken her in his arms, and only checked himself with an effort.</p>
        <p>"Heaven bless • you!" he cried, hoarsely, and then suddenly her arms were about his neck her face premed close to, his.</p>
        <p>; ';</p>
        <p>He helped Olga up into the dog-cart, and then sprang to his seat beside her, and took the reins. The horse leaped forward. The groom fell away, and then they were off. They bowled out along the drive, and across the park towards the road. Geoffrey twirled the whip with a light on his face and a smile on. his lip. He stopped to speak to the boy at the lodge, who had ,been ill, and who was sitting in a chair in front of the cottage wrapped in blankets, and he stopped again to speak to his mother, as she came to Jp,...a the gates for them.. "The boy going on all right, Mrs. Green?" he cried. Yes? Well, fie shall have some jelly clown from the Hall. I'll tell the cook to make it the moment I get back. And if the doctor orders port, or anything like that, he can have it, you know, Mrs. Green." The woman curteseyed as he started the horse again. Her face had flushed, and she looked after her new master with a sudden light li her eyes. Geoffrey would be popular. M- ready the small children on the estate knew him, and the women looked at him indul-: gently. He had come to them from the .sea come by a miracle straight from the jaws of death; and he was good to look at, good to speak to, the kind of master they would be proud of. • Olga lifted her eyes to his as they—sw-Tt round into the road. If only things had heea different ! If only she had met him before —this good man who loved her so, whpse heart she would have to break.</p>
        <p>She set her lips, and stifled a sigh as they swept along the broad road; and then, with a little leap at her heart, she found that they were not going towards Greatbridge atix all.</p>
        <p>"Where are we going this afternoon, Geoffrey?" she asked.</p>
        <p>He twirled his whip, and smiled clown at her. •</p>
        <p>" Guess," ha said.</p>
        <p>"To the common?" she asked, but he turned his face from her again, and gem-3y flicked the horse's ears.</p>
        <p>"Perhaps," he said; "but I'm going to do a little business • first. Hera's Kenaseote Church. Doesn't it look a jolly- little -pla-.h.) —out. of C...a way, qt3'.et, smothered in ivy—tne very church for a wedding, Olga." Her heart gave a quick leap. Her Nes whitened, and under the rug her hands clasped suddenly together. "A wedding?" she said, in a whisper. He had turned into the little lane on the opposite side of the church, and the horse was walking up the slight incline. He turned to look at her for a minute, and his browB. contracted a little. She had no business to look as white as that, he thought. She ought to be getting back her colour by now. Well, he would make her his very own, and take her away again for a little while, and get rid for ever of that pallor that so easily came to her cheeks and lips. He stopped the horse outside an iron gate which led through a small paddock to a white house under trees.</p>
        <p>T 1</p>
