      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>Olga gave a startled cry and half rose from her seat,</head>
        <p>Olga gave a startled cry and half rose from her seat,</p>
        <p>"Oh ! I love you—l love you," she whys- pered; "and I'll help you with. all my heart."</p>
        <p>CIIAPTER X.</p>
        <p>"Sir Geoffrey is ready, Miss Murray." Olga turned with a start-. She was stand- ing looking out of her window across the wid2 Hall gardens. The sunshine lay full upon them, yellow, golden, selming to wake up a flood of colour wherever it fell, and under the willows to the left the little lake gleamNi like a diamond in the light. Olga turned to the housemaid. , _</p>
        <p>." Tell Sir Geoffrey I will come down et once," • she said. Yet she lingered, looking out with mis?rable eyes at the long sweep of grass, the far-away meadows, and the blue say beyond them all. Sir Geoffrey was nailing ! Downstairs, as she knew, the brown horse pawed the gravA with a groom at his head, and under the porch Sir Gaoffrey waited for her to come.</p>
        <p>Where would he drive her to this after- noon? She asked the question with a white, wan face. Twice he had taken her out, an.d each time 5h3 had had a difficulty in avoiding Greatbridge. Now, this afternoon, he was sure to go somewhere in that direction. She fumbled with her gloves, and gave a last touch- to her hair and veil. Her face looked white as it met its reflection in the glass, and her eyes seemed to have large dark rims round them. Yet sh 3 had never been so happy in her life as she was now— never so happy, even though she knew it was only a dream, from which she, must awake sooner or later—even though the end might Conic at any moment. Every morning she told herself that she must go that day. Every morning, as she searched the newspapers with her heart in h3r mouth for news of the confession she had made to Dr. Mier? on board the ship, she felt that it would be ber last day there, the end was bound to come, and soon.</p>
        <p>She turned slowly at last, and w nit down- stairs. Geoffrey looked round • impatiently when he saw her.</p>
        <p>"I thought you were never coming," said, holding out his hand to her. 'We are losing the best of the afternoon, and you know what the doctor said? Fresh air, and plenty of it."</p>
        <p>Olga stared at the prancing horse. " I could sit in the gardan," she bega.), but he stopped her.</p>
        <p>"Don't;You ever want me?" be asked, under his breath. "Olga, sweetest, I think you want to be away from rac, butt you don't, do you? You don't want to be rid of me?" Her eyes answered him.</p>
        <p>" Want? " she cried, huskily. -" Oh I wish I ineed never be separated from you—even fi;r an hour."</p>
        <p>He had no chance to mak3 any reply,. for the groom appeared, stiff -and immaculate La his livery, and touched_ his 'hat.</p>
        <p>" Yes, we are ready,' said Geoffrey, "and you need, not come. We will go by ours3lvea this nftornnAn '?</p>
        <p>" Will you hold tho rs-.qns for a moment, dear," h 3 asked, " while I open the gate?" She looked round with vague surprise. "Why are we going here?" she asked. "This is the rectory, isn't it?" He did not reply for a moment. He seeired to have a little difficulty with .the gate. For an instant it hung resisting on its hinges, almost as if it was trying to prevent his en- trance, but the nr?xt, it had swung sharply back, and he had turned the horse's. head and was leading it in. "Why are we going here to-day?" Olga asked again. He looked up at her. "Children should be seen and not heard," he answered, as he climlied back into the trap. "1 hav3 a little business with Mr.- Pendlebury. It will not take us long—only a minute Or two. _</p>
        <p>He V,olc the reins from her. and it ieemod to her that his fingers .clung to hers curiously for an instant. Then he turned his head again', and drOv: smartly on. At the Vicarap a gankner tame orward, and Geoffrey, springing to the grouf'd, held out his arms to 'Olga.</p>
        <p>"Come along," he said. "I want you to • come too." .</p>
        <p>A maid stood at the open door. Mr. Pen- dlebury was in, she said, and what naml should she give? When Geoffrey told her she looked at him with some curiosity, and there was a new air of dignity about her as she rapped smartly at a. door, and then flung it open. " Sir Geoffrey Verriker and Miss Murray," she said, with enthusiasm, and than they Went in.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pendlcbury was a little old znan, half blind. He was b,-n:t double • over his desk when they entered, and he turned quickly and cable to in.-2et them.</p>
        <p>"Sir Geoffrey !" he cried. "I am glad to meet you—glad to welcome you after your terrible experiences. And, Miss—Miss— " Ile, was peering into Olga's fac?, l•olding her hand. " Th'' is not your sister?" he addej. abruptly, a bewildered, and Geoffrey laughed. " No, not my sister," he said, " but e, bit of wreckage which I brought back from the sea—Miss Murray, who is to be my " Indeed lucked !" The old man wa3 shakily' her hand,- and had put his other on her shoulder, peering still into her face, in his half-blind way." " - _</p>
        <p>"I 'congratulate you both," he said. "Yon have 'come through a terrible experience, and I am glad to see yeti both ber.l safe and w;AI." He turned to put chairs for them, and Olga sat down. But Ceoffrey stood with his back to the window, and his eyes on her face. Somehow his own had grown a little white. He -fidgeted uneasily. watching as the old clergyman and Olga talked. He had come on impulse—without thinking except of the thing he himself so longed for, and now, there, in the quiet study, with Olga looking so calm and white, he felt unaasy. Yet, after wto• not? He loved ;tor. and she 10-,-RO</p>
        <p>nim—ana he knew it. He plunged sun- denly.</p>
        <p>" Pendlebury, I've come on a little matter of business," he said. "I have told you that Miss Murray is my—my—fiancée. I have come this afternoon to ask you to put up our banns."</p>
        <p>Olga gave a startled cry. She met Geoffrey's eyes, and half rose from her seat. thrusting out her hands in a queer, nervous way. as if thrusting back something that was coming upon her. _ _</p>
        <p>-The old man was too blind to se the ex- pression on those two faces looking at each other—too blind, too unsuspecting—too uncon- scious to see.</p>
        <p>But Geoffrey started back at the look in Olga's eyes. His face paled, and ho took a step towards her, with questions bursting from his lips. Surely—surely she couldn't fear marriage with him? Surely, surely she loved him? Yet what did that look in. her eyes mean? Why did she look afraid? Why did she shrink? Perhaps he had not ben fair to her. He had sprung it upon her too sud- denly, and, after all, he ought not to have brought her there without telling her why he had done so. Yet, what harm was thera in putting up the banns? They need not marry unless they chose. And ,she .loved him—he had been so sure that she loved him !</p>
        <p>He had spoken no word. The old clergy- man was profuse in his congratulations, and was busy getting out hooks. and papers in which to make notes of the particulars. - Geoffrey went up to Olga, and stood at her side. He put out, his hand to her. "I am so anxious to make sure of :her," he said. "She nearly escaped me in the sea, and I shall feel when the banns are up that I am a little nearer getting her, even though— even though the banns don't mean anything, after all. We might alter our minds yet— eh,.olga, in spite of the banns?" _</p>
        <p>She —made no reply. Her throat was parched. The blood that - had rushed to her face had died away again, leaving it paler than ever. Ha watched her closely. Mr. Pendle- bury had found his papers, and had his pen in hand.</p>
        <p>"Marriage between—between Sir Geoffrey Verriker," he muttered, "and Miss—Miss Murray. What Christian name did you say, Sir G?.offrey? " Geoffrey turned to him.</p>
        <p>"Miss -Olga Murray," he said. He felt her fingers tighten over his. He heard her draw her breath in a queer, sobbing way, and then the old chrgyman looked up. Olga sat quite still while they spoke. "Then that is all right, Sir Geoffrey," Mr. Pendlebury said. "That is all I require just now. And I congratulate you both." His old, half-blind eyas were smiling :into Olga's again, and her hand was trembling in his. She stood up. There was a low boom- ing in her ears—a booming like the far-off sound of the sea. She almost felt again the rocking, of the boat under-foot, almcst heard her own harsh voic3.• almost saw again the hard, stern face of Dr. Dacre, as she niade her confession to him. •</p>
        <p>Write—write, that I, Olga, murdered Stephen Henderson, in Greatbridge Hall, six years _ago</p>
        <p>- Had he really said those words to Dr. Dacre? Had she really seen hini write .them down? Had she really signed her. name to them, and then seen him ferce the paper into a soda-water, bottle and fling it out, high above their heads, or was it all only a ghastly dream? The little study and the face of the old clergyinan faded away for a moment, and she saw only Geoffrry—Geoffrey, as she had seen him under the sky as the boat sank. Oh !if she had'only died then in his arms !. If only she had had strength to leave" him before this!</p>
        <p>But every day where hb was concern.Nl she grew weaker. She could not look in his face now without yielding. How could she help it? All things ugly lay outside .his arms—all the happiness in life lay within them. How could sh?. leave him? •</p>
        <p>She became aware that Geoffrey wa 3 speaking.</p>
        <p>" are • you ready, Olga ? " ho was asking. She looked up at him, and her head reeled. She could never leave. him. She could never go away from him. She could never face a day and know that she would never see him again. She could not. Her pulses began to beat as she turned and walked out through the little - square hall to the porch before which the dog-cart stood. file sun poured down upon the rector's gar- den on the gleaming patches of geraniums in the beds, on the, green grass of the paddock beyond. It caught the shining harness of Geoffrey's horse, and glittered on the polished sides of the cart. It seemed to em- phasise all the beauty in the world, all she would lose if she left Geoffrey now, and ter heart beat up, her blood ran quickly through her veins, and her eyes brightened. Why should she? ~Why should she ever go? Stephen Henderson deserved his death. She had only taken justice into her own hands when she had killed him six years ago, and no' one knew—ul one except one dead map, lying Jeep under the sea—a dead man who could let rise to accuse her now, and whose hard, 4-.ern face Would never look into Lers again. No., no, she would not look back. She would go on. She had nothing to fear— tothing. Yet, on the threshold as she walkcd towards .the cart she stopped. The ' thought ran cold through her. 'What of her confession in the soda-water bottle, that had danced green and "gold on the waves?. What of that? She was cold as Geoffrey helped her up in- to the trap. lite .:mbs shook under her, but her face was sot- She would go on. She could not give aeoffrey up. She clasped her hands in her 107, and watched him hungrily as he walked to the other side and mounted to the seat beside her.</p>
        <p>Heaven help her, and heaven save him! He Was a good man, and never in all her life had a good man loved her. Never in all her life had she had a chance of happiness until now. What. drowning man would turn from the plank • that would save him? Geoffrey 'would save her. He would marry her, and no one would ever know that it wae she who six years ago had stolen into Greatbridge Hall and murdered; Stephen Henderson. No one would know. And Richard Sterry had forgotten now the ugly suspicion that had en- wrapped him, she thought. He must have forgotten, and when he married Ethel he would forgive, too. Yes, he would forgive and forget in time, and she— She put out her hand to Geoffrey as they turned into the road again. "'Geoffrey, I will be _ good to you," she whispered'. - He turned to her abruptly. "Darling, for- give me for acting like that," he cried. " I am so afraid of lesing you—l want you so badly, my sweetheart.". _</p>
        <p>Her eyes. were on his, and he saw them suddenly fill with tears. He stopped the horse and put his arm round her. " darling ! " he whispered, "Are you quite happy? Am I doing. every- thing you want to make you happy? Is there anything 'else—anything else in the world, my darling, that I could do ? " Anything more ? " she whispered, huskily. "Anything more than the most? Oh, Geoffrey, I can't tell you what I feel; but I pray—l pray that you will he happy. Oh, Geoffrey, I'd give my life for your happiness. My life—my life? It would be nothing, if your happiness depended on it. Geoffrey, when anything happens—if anything doet happen, you'll know—you'll know that I have loved you too much—that I could not tear myself away—because—Geoffrey, because my heart and my life and my soul and my strength are in your hands." He held her close against him, under the shadow of the hedge. His face had paled a</p>
        <p>'Olga, my darling—Olga, my sweetheart— mine! You talk as if you yourself were nothing, whereas—Olga darling, if you knew all I think of you, you'd be vainer than any peacock. , There is do woman in the world like you." He bent his head, and raising her hand held it against his lips. • Then he loosed it again and started the horse afresh. "I'm- the luckiest man he said.</p>
        <p>She stared ahead with white lips and wild eyes, seeing across the smiling country a vision that mocked her, a haunting dread, a fearful doubt. If that thing should rise again? If that bottle should be washed ashore? If the</p>
        <p>shadow of a dead man should rise to drag no: from Geoffrey's arms?</p>
        <p>°She lifted her face to the sky. Somewhere in her brain again was the dull boom of the sea, and now it seemed to be repeating some old familiar words. They .beat up, keeping time to the clack, .clack of the horse's hoofs on the dry road: " The punishnient of sin umishment of sin is death ' "</p>
        <p>i 3 death —th3</p>
        <p>(''o le continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>- _ -------.'''';''A--------- '4'...-; _</p>
        <p>[By Redcap.]</p>
        <p>DISEASES OF POULTRY AND THEIR CURE. Diseases and ailments in Poultry are generally speaking, the result of neglect. Therefore, before dealing with such let me point out that if properly housed and fed on wholesome food, disease is to a great extent avoided. The first principles in poultry farming should be : perfect cleanliness, good ventila- tion and sound food. Of course, misfortunes do happen on the best regulated poultry farms. Disease has played havoc in many a well kept establishment. And because of its existence we have it to fight. ROUI'.</p>
        <p>This is a very common disease among poultry. It is first detected by a swelling of the face and eyes. If not attended to in the first stages it- develops into a worse form called diphtheritic roup. The latter is most difficult to cure, in fact unless they are valuable birds it is advisable to kill and bury at once in quick-lime. Roup in any stage is highly infectious, especially in its worst form. Treatment :—Make a decoction of camomile flowers and bathe the head and face while quite hot. When you have dried it,disinfect the mouth by painting with a feather that has been dipped in strong camphor- water. Bathing the head and face in Cony's Fluid is also a useful remedy. A piece of camphor about the size qf a small hazel-nut should be placed in the drinking water in all cases. Roup powder can also be obtained and should be mixed in the soft food. Need I remind you that separating the sick from the rest of the stock is necessary. _ _</p>
        <p>Roup is caused by fowls experiencing a sudden change. After being in a hot show, for instance. Sometimes fowls that have been in the habit of roost- ing in trees, when made to roost under artificial con- ditions easily contract roup. It was in this way that I lost a very fine specimen of the Old English Game, in spite of a well ventilated house. From a chick he had been quartered in the open, finally lodging in a damson tree. But the change was too drastic and he " shuffled off," a victim to roup. GAPES.</p>
        <p>This terrible disease is dreaded by the poultry farmer when raising young stock. It is caused by a worm in the trachea. Sometimes the whole course of the windpipe isfull of these parasites. It is easy to detect when chickens are suffering from this dinase. They open their mouths ...wide. and, gasp for breath, shaking the head and sneezing, reaching the neck in the attempt to swallow. These are all sure signs of gapes, Although the chicks have an enormous appetite while thus suffering, they lose flesh rapidly. They seem to instinctively gobble their food as if to assist in removing obstructions. Treatment : By careful manipulation these worms can be removed by the introduction of a feather in the wind-pipe. Strip a feather of its web except for about half an inch of the top. Thus by passing it over the tongue into the wind-pipe gently turning it round you can extract the worm. It is a very difficult process and if great care is not taken death vill follow as a result. There are other remedies of special value in -the form of liquids and powder. Fumigation is another method and a convenient one, because where a number of birds are affected at one time time they can be treated en bloc. This is done by blowing the powder into w i coops With a bellow-s. s. The powder dust s inhaled by the chicks after which they may be given a drink, the water having been diluted with Liquid cure according to directions sent out with these remedies. I would' advise my readers to make themselves acquainted with poult2y medicines and keep a stock in hand. To prevent the spreqing Of gapes, disinfect all utensils and coops. Sprtrkle the runs with powdered lime and lime-wash the houses frequently with hot-lime. By this you will destroy any worms that may have been passed up by coughing and sneezing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>rn.tflflh{(Et.</head>
        <p>rn.tflflh{(Et.</p>
        <p>This disease is one which soon racks the constitution of fowls. It is noticeable by frequent voidings -of liquid matter, which if allowed to remain in the runs is most dangerous. Fowls therefore, thus affected, should be isolated at once. The complaint is caused by sudden change of food, or of food unwholesome in character and any indigestible diet;' such. as for instance unripe corn, and soft food which has become tainted and sour. Treatment—Whatever may have been the primary cau,e it is absolutely "necessary to clear it away. A mild laxative is essential for this purpose and could be given in the form of a teaspoon- ful of salad oil. Feed on boiled rice to which has been added a little poultry spice. Give them an occasional tonic and use care in the diet for a week or so and they will soon revive. Fowls taken with this disease should have immediate attention. Or if not the more aggravated form of diSrrheea called dysentry will follow.</p>
        <p>CROP-BOUND.</p>
        <p>The crop is the first receptacle into which the food is stored, before being passed into the stomach or gizzard. Sometimes it becomes bound through eating , food which unduly swells.. I have seen fowls crop- bound through eating cut grass—grass that has been plucked long and thrown down. Fowls that scarcely get any green food greedily swallow it, and so become crop-bound. Another cause is by swallowing a large piece of food which sticks and accumulates other food round it in the crop. Birds irregularly fed are genet4 ally the victims, through a keen sense of hunger. The symptoms are noticeable by visible enlargement ,of the crop, unusual thirst and the moping attitude of the fowl. Upon examination you will find that the crop is very hard, and appearing as though it'would burst. Treatment.—When the 'case is not a bad one, the introduction of an oily liquid sometimes proves effectual. Take one table-spoonful of olive oil, and two of hot milk and mix well together. Pour a little ia the mouth of the bird and work it down the throat into the crop. Then gently manipulate with the fingers and endeavour to get some of the contents of the crop to pass. Give the fowl a rest, then try a second time. Should you fail in both attempts an operation will have to he performed . as. follows : Remove a few feathers from the crop, and make an incision with a very sharp pen- knife (that has been dipped in tondy's fluid) through the skin and then through the wall of the crop. Then take a small bone egg ,spogn, dip it in olive oil and gently remove the contents. When the crop is quite empty pour in a few drops of Coneys Fluid.. Very often the food has become offensive and the fluid will purify the crop. Afterwards stitch both the wall of the crop and the outer skin with pure silk ' thread. Each must be stitched with four separate stitches and quite independantly of the other. After such an ordeal the fowl must be placed in a coop in some quiet corner free from draught. • Feed on hot bread and milk for about four or five days. During this period no water is required as sufficient liquid is obtained from the diet. - Then feed on, boiled rice and oat-meal until quite strong. During the whole time no stimulant should be given as it is very dangerous after crop-binding.</p>
      </div>
