      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS,</p>
        <p>The New Relieving Officers. EXTRAORDINARY POSITION.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Roarci. of Guardians was held at the Workh-mse on WednesdeY” Mr. G. Mather presiding. There were also present : Mrs. Simpson Mrs. Coll;ngvt ood, Rev. M. Ryan, Messrs. J. Billinge, %V. Gill, A. J.. Jolley, R. Forshaw, V. Houghton, W.C. Brooks, A. Brooks, W. Flunnag •n, A.. Bottomley (clerk), R. W. Peacock (assistant clers), J. F. Awhery (master), and A Wright (superintendent of the Cottage Homes). NON-RESIDENT OFFICERS' TELEPHONES.. At a meeting of the Finance Committee the asslstant clerk read a letter from the Rev. W. Bracecamp, date 28th July, in respect of the Guardians paying the rent of his telephone until June, 1910, and the Committee after due consideration recommended that the date for the termination of the Board's responsibility for the telephones of non-resident officers be June 21t.b, 19:0. Mr. JOLLEY, in moving the adoption of the minutes, pointed out that the resoluticn would put all the non— resident officers on an equal footing in the matter. Mr. BILLINGE seconded and the n.inutes were. approvei.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>NEW BUILDING.</head>
        <p>THE NEW BUILDING.</p>
        <p>'rho CLERK reported that he 11;17eceived, a letter from the Loral Government Board, dated July 7th. I9o9,stating that they had approveri, under their seal,. the plans relating to the proposed new building for aged and infirm inmates at the Workbouse.Preliminary to,the issue of an Orderauthorising the Board to pro- ceed with the building, it was necessary that certain- resolutions should be passed. Mr. JOLLEY moved :—" That the Guardians herehy consent (1), To the erection at the Workhouse of the Union of a building for the accommodation of the aged; and infirm inmates, in accordance with plans to which the Local Government Board have affixed their Seal, subject nevertheless to such modifications thereof as, that Board my hereafter direct (2), To the provision of furniture for the said building. (3), To the. expenditure for the above purposes of a sum not exceed- ing £6,657. (4), To the borrowing of the said sum of £6,657 or such part thereof as may be necessary for the purposes aforesaid. Further that the sum of £6,657 be borrowed for the purposes aforesaid. That the sum so borrowed be repaid within a pericd not exceeding. thirty years."</p>
        <p>Mr-. f3ILLINGE in seconding asked if they would have to apply for an additional loan in respect of the fur- nishing of the building. The CLERK said he understood the furnitling was included in the £6,657. The resolutions were agreed to. Mr. Jor.LEY said a very strong opinion was expresse,' some time ago that it was desirable that there should be no further delay in carrying out the work. The probabilities were that the issuing of the Order by the .Local Government Board would be a comparatively - formal matter and would not he delayed. He proposed that the present Building Committee be authorised to take any necessary action to give the speediest possible effect to the resolutions just agreed to. The majority of the Board would. probably agree that the celay which had happened since it was first proposed to carry- out a scheme on those lines had been highly injurious to the best interests of the Union. Mr. BILLE:CGE seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>TILE NEW RELIEVING OFFICERS.—CCRIOUS POSITION, The CLERK read the following letter from the Local Governmrnt Board :—" I am directed by the Local Government Board to acknowledge the receipt on the - 15th ult. of the form of queries containing particif.ars of the proposed appointments of Messrs. T. Gidnian. and E. H. Cottorill as relieving officers in the War— rington Union. The Board cAserve that Messrs.• Gidnian and Cotterill have had no previous experience. of Poor Law or similar work, and on present information it appears to them doubtful whether they are competent to perform eliciently the responsible duties of the office of relieving officer. They regard it as of great importance that persons appointed t.) this office should have had experience in duties of a similar nature. Before further considering the question, the Board are desirous of being informed of the grounds (Nl' %filch the appointments have been made by the Guardians and the evidence of qualification on which they have relied." -</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON : It is an extraordialry letter, ia my view.</p>
        <p>The Rev. M. RYAN : It is not in mina. Mrs. SIMP.iON : Nor in-mine. lhe CLERK: I think it would be well to • consider- the matter in committee, and draft a proper reply. Mr. HOUGHTON: It is a most extraordinary letter, T repeat. It is too conservative for anything, an idea of that kind. How are we to get new blood in Poor filo officialism if we are to be tied with our hands behind our backs in this manner?</p>
        <p>l'he CLERK : I reported to the Local Government Board that the Guardians were fully satisfied th it the, selected candidates were quite able and competent to. perform efficiently all the duties of the office of reliev- ing officer. Mr. W. C. BROOKS: Seeing that it was a specially convened meeting of the °metal Pu:pose3 Committee which selected the candidates, I move that another special meeting be convened for the purpcsa of discuss— ing the subject. Mr. HOUGHTON seconded. _</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY : It is surely of great importance that the matter should be settled as quickly as poisible. Tbe-admTnistrution of relief is in a somewhat unsettle&amp; state. 1 should suppose that after the long discussion that took place the majo. i,y of the members were satisfied that the persons selected were competent. We were all satisfied that we hArl a number of excellent candidates and we selected two who were as good RS' any others in the list. I propose th it tits Board no W resolve itself into co.ntnitteo to consider the prActiza-- bility of replying this morning. Mr. FORSHAW seconded.</p>
        <p>The Rev. M. RYAN: We ought to have special ultice to give every Guardian tbe opportunity of considering the matter. It is a matter the like of which has 110 D cropped up before. Mr. A. BIIOOKS : The matter has certainly not come suddenly to me, after the Clerk's warning when we first; advertised. We ought to have been prepared for it. After further discussion the Board resolved itself into Committee and a suitable reply to the Local Government Beard was agreed upon.</p>
        <p>COTTAGE 11031 ES APPOINTMENT. The followiog salected candidates for the position of foster mother ;A the Cottage Homes appeared before the Board :—Miss Bessie Sutton, Warrington ; hliss Mary Turton, Warrington ; bliss Edith Swinton' Orford. hliss Sutton was appinted at a 'salary of £2' a year with the usual allowances.</p>
      </div>
