      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>215 BOWLING FAMILIES.</head>
        <p>215 BOWLING FAMILIES.</p>
        <p>A NOVEL GAME AT \V-AiItRING rON.</p>
        <p>A crowd of COO assembled at the Bowling Green, Bank Q.mv, on Aloaday to witness the first of a couple of home sod home bowling matches to decide which is the crack bowling f gmily of the Connty Palatine. The sides are composed of Joseph Harrison, of Moseley Hill, and his five s &gt;ns, and William Gieave, of Warrington, and five of h:a sons.</p>
        <p>, - '1 he retura takes pia s 3 at Moseley Hll, on August 14th. SoLno wry close garne3 were witnessed, and eve ltually the Gleavas 'won by N. Details : Will Gleave, sen. E. Gleave 2l Joseph ll:wizen, jun. 8 Joe Gleave Walter Cleave l2 Har )1d Harriboa 2l Jack Gleave.. W. Gleave, jun.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Will Warrington win the League championship? It is a difficult question to answer, but Warrington enthusiasts are hopeful that the honours will come to the local eleven this season. The victory would be a popular one, for the Warringtonians have worked hard although they have had much bad luck in the tourney. The present position is deeply interesting. If War- rington beat St. Helens Recreation on Saturday next, the cup will be theirs for the ensuing twelve month. A. draw will place them on level terms with the Prescot club and in that event there will be a (Treat struggle between Warrington and Prescot to decide the leadership.</p>
        <p>The following are the League tables up-to-date : FlasT DIVISION. Club. P. W. L. D. Pts. Prescot Warrington St. Helens Recs. St. Helens 8 ... 2 ... 3 ... $ ... 7 Eqrlestown *Newton *Two points deducted for placing ineligible player. SECOND DIVISION. -</p>
        <p>Prescot St. Helens Rees Warrington Earlestown Newton St. Helens 8 ... 2 ... 5 ... 1 ... 3</p>
        <p>Warrington made a bold bid for victory at Earles- town on Saturday, but although they gave a creditable exhibition they had to be satisfied with a draw. Batting first, the visitors declared their innings after amassing the substantial total of 165 for 5 wickets,and they had the hardest of luck in not being able to dis- miss the Earlestown eleven, the total when time arrived being 96 for 9 wickets. It was indeed a most tantalising position.</p>
        <p>W. H. Podmore distinguished himself with a brilliant display, his 13 including five fours. He also</p>
        <p>came out w.ith the fine bowling analysis of three wickets for seven rut's. S. Partridge continues to show very promising form, and he had the satisfaction of carrying oat his bat with 35 to his</p>
        <p>For Earlestown F. N. Clare b-atted in clasiiinglZ his innings realizing 33.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd entertained the second a) I:.ngent from Earlestown, but after the visitors had completed their innings rain fell so heavily that play was abandoned. The Earlestown total was only 79, so that the Warringtonians had reasonable expectations of victory. W. Bennett took fiv.e wickets for 33 runs and J. Waywell five for 40.</p>
        <p>lii the Warrington and District League St. James's continue their winning way, Latchford Baptists being their latest victims. Dismissing the Baptists for 78, the saints piled on 106 with only 1 wickets down. J. H. Constable, who has done splendid service for the team, played finely for his not out innings of 68.</p>
        <p>14lands' Bros. met Wilderspeol Brewery, bUt the rain stoppad the match when. the Brewery were all out for 41 and the wire men had made 12 for 2 wickets. McGowan bowled wi.h great effect for Ryland.s, taking six wickets for 12 runs.</p>
        <p>Whitecross angered defeat at the hands of the " Guardian " office, to the extent of 42 runs. Riley was the top scorer with 31. D. Clare for the winners took four wickets for tllree runs and 11. Wright for Whitecrcss five for 42.</p>
        <p>On Monday the " Guardian " eleven failed to account for Rylands's who triumphed by a .majority of 16. T. Newton (12) was the most successful bats, man. G. McGowan, for the winners, took seven wickets for nine runs and performed the hat trick. C. Woodhead, for the " Guardian," captured seven wickets for four runs.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Eng:neers entertained Sankey Wire Mills and gained the victory by 12 runs.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington Junior League, Rylands could only put up eight men against Sankey and suffered a reyerso by. 29. Daintith (17) batted well for the</p>
        <p>winners.</p>
        <p>Orford were triumphant over Padgate,Leigh taking five wickets for six runs and Allen five for eight.</p>
        <p>Ditchford Baptists englged in a friendly match with Marbury and had no difficulty in winning. Dixon (19) and G. Cox (20 not out) batted creditably and Chile carried off the bowling honours with eight wickets for 11 runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington Municipal Officers' Guild accounted for Helsby, D. Holland (15) being the highest scorer. Holland also did well with the ball, taking six wickets for 14, while on the losing side Cartwright (the Cheshire County man) captured six wickets for 11 runs.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists went to ltuncorn on Monday and proved more than a match for the M.F.C. The game was chiefly notable for the batting of A. Albion who knocked up 22.</p>
        <p>Lymm inflicted a severe drubbing on Sale Moor, IL Saville distinguishing himself with a brilliant not out innings of 62.</p>
        <p>At Oughtrington Park an inter estlit match between the Northern Nomads and Mr. Tomkinson's eleven resulted in a draw. J. Leslie was in great batting form, compiling 27 in his first innings and 54 in his second.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>The great event in local boWling circ!es to-day is the fmll of the Warrington works Competition, which will take place on the Bowling Green, Bank Quay. A record gate is anticipated and there should be a keen struggle between Whitecross and Rylands' No. 1 for the Finlayson trophy. The teams will be selected from the following :—WhiteCross : T. Ellison .(capt.), A. Gleave, Ted Gleave, John Gleave, E. Gleave, W. Gleave, A. Warburton, G. Warburton, J. Appleton, A. Shelmerdine, J. Slavin, J. Horton, W. Jones, G. Shaw, J. Gleave. Rylands' : It. Savory (capt.), G. Shaw, J. Shaw, R. Knight, A. Stringer, T. Humphries, I. Newton, W. Daniels, W. Roughley, H. Eyes, H. Haslam, J. Woods, F. Miller, E. Blaekledge, G. Holbrook. Mr. J. Willett will be the referee.</p>
        <p>Mrs. A.. 11. CrosGeld has kindly consented to present the cup and medals to the winners and runners-up after the match, on the green. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., will also be present. Crosfield's prize band will play selections during the afternoon. Ticket- holders are requested to note that the gates will be open to them at 2 o'clock prompt, and will be reserved for them until 2.30, after which time money will be taken at. the gates. Seats will be provided around the green at a charge of Id. extra. .</p>
        <p>The "Examiner " photographer will take photo- graphs on the green, and these, together with a full account of thejnatch, will appelr in our nest issue.</p>
        <p>Bowlers are reminded that to-day is the last day for the receipt of entries for the annual handicap in connection with the Association.</p>
        <p>In view of to-day's final a brief outline of the history of the Warrington Works Bowling Association will probably be of interest. The Association was founded at a meeting held at the King and Queen Hotel, Padgate, at the beginning of 1903. Captain George ,Crosfield was elected president, Mr. J. Willett chairman of the committee, Mr. G. Holbrook treasurer, and Mr. F. M. Scarisbrick seeretarv. No fewer than 30 teams entered the first year. The winners of the Finlayson Cup in 1903 were L. and N.-W. and the runners-up Pearson and Knowles. Ont of 28 teams in 1904, Pearson and Knowles were declared the winners with Cheshire Lines as runners-up. in 1995, 23 teams entered and Pearson and Knowles again carried off the cup, Fletcher, Russell and Co. being their con- testants in the final. Thirty-one teams entered in 1906, and Rylands' No. 1 were the winners. Messrs. Fletcher's were the runners up. In 1907 the teams entering created a record,the number being 33. White- cross were the winners and Sankey Wire Mills the runners up. In 1908 out of 31 teams entered, White- cross won again and the other finalists were L.&amp; N.-W.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting held on March 16th 1909, Mr. W. Patrick was elected secretary. Mr. Pattick modestly said at the time that he felt he was not qualified for such a position, but he yielded to the pressure of the members to accept. lie has fully justified their high expectations and the increasing success of the association has been due in no small measure to his untiring efforts.</p>
        <p>" Having put my shoulder to the wheel " Mr. Patrick said in a chat with our representative the other day" I was determined to do my best, and was soon aware that something was required to keep the ball rolling.' Smaller works said that unless something were done for them they would retire from the compett- tion as they had no chance of winniug, having only 100 men to pick from and there were very often few bpwlers among them, whereas the larger works had seores of bowlers—and good ones too. Take for instance Messrs. Greenhigs. They never got beyond the second round. What encouragement was there to keep entering year after year ? Some of the larger works kept saying Be sportsmen and enter for the love of the game?' But all true sportsmen like to have a chance. The Committee for the year 190 S had many ideas brought before them and after many meetings decided to recommend to the annual meeting the rather novel idea of handicapping the teams, which turned out to be very successful. '</p>
        <p>The Association has succeeded in raising 5.,15 for the Infirmary and the Nursing -• Association. At the annual meeting of 1900 Mr. Holbrook retired from his post as treasurer, having done good service for the Association since its formation. Mr. W. Campbell was then elected in his stead. Almost unanimously the meeting decided to • handicap the teams, only one works objecting, and they had won the cup twice in succession. As a result of the handicapping 35 teams from 25 works entered this year and it is con- fidently believed that more will be the case next year. It is claimed with some pride that the Association is the first of its kind in the country.</p>
        <p>The Association owes a great deal to the encourage- ment of Mr. and Mrs. grampbell. Mr. Campbell's green is an old one, having been used as far back as 1772. The organization is doing a great deal to make the life of the workingmen a little brighter by pro- viding them with means of wholesome recreation. The Association have to monrn the loss of the late Councillors J. J. Neild and W. B. Roberts, who were warm supporters of the game.</p>
        <p>Warrington Cemetery are to be congratulated upon again finding themselves in the final of the Lancashire</p>
        <p>and Cheshire Club Championship. List year they achieved that distinction,but in the final they had to bow the knee to their loeal rivals of the Bull'sHead.lt is hoped that this time their persistence will be rewarded a victory which will be hailed with satisfaction by tll local lovers of the game. Last Saturday's semi- final between Warrington Cemetery and Chorlton Bowling Green was played under miserable conditions rain falling most of the time.</p>
        <p>The Chorlton captain, J. Fairhurst, bowled a remark- ably good game, and won by 14 chalks. All Scott, who is at present tight on the top of his form; bowled finely, and scored at the expense of Paxton.</p>
        <p>G Ball, who is now bordering on 70 years of age, skippei about the green like a youth in his teens, and on a short length fulling mark bowled some real beauties, to win easily 21-'2B. •</p>
        <p>A. J. Jones, and J. D. Johnstone, who is the Chorlton president, played a splendid game, neither of them being able to shake the other off. Only one point separated them at the 25th end, and they peeled at 17, 18, and 29, Johnstone winning a most exciting tie 21-20.</p>
        <p>The other semi-finalists, Leigh Railway and Newton le-Willows, will have to meet again. The green on which they should have played, Radcliffe Wahine, was absolutely unfit, being under water in places.</p>
        <p>The annual matert. to</p>
        <p>-- There was a novel match on the Bowling Green, Bank-Quay, on Moadhy between .the Gleave family of Warrington and the Harrison family, -of Moseley Hill. The opportunity of witnessing such a battle royal is not often afforded and there was an attendance of quite 690. The Weaves had t'..e satisfactioa. of wuming by 16 points.</p>
        <p>_ _ _ decide the championship of the Bull's Head Club took place on Saturday. Albert ("leave conceded . , . W. Andrews 2 points ..,.,•,„ • and defeated him by the handsome margin of 21-4. '-' 11 •.:•,. Ile thus becomes *the holder for twelvemonths ',.'.. -.*•0,......--4„.1.i..„ of the handsome ••:::;:,—;-...--: '"'" challenge trophy which • 4:.c.'' ' he also had the honour , sv°4:7#• .-"'"; '••"..- - of winnin,-,i, in 1907. A. -.•:•••:..- - Gleave is a member of ~,-7, :'.- . the well-known bowling F. :-' -41 r. 1,i,.,.. 'f %, . , ,e4'4U'' '.... family of that name and is undoubtedly one of the finett players in the A. GLEAVE. district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>Warrington F.C. hold their first practice match to-day, on the ground behind St. James' Church. This field had been secured for the Club use at odd times, and should relieve the football ground from excessive wea`r during the season.</p>
        <p>I understand that Frawley, the Australian three- quarter, has arrived in London and is expected in Warrington next:yeek. He was playing football in New South Wales early in June so that he should not need a great deal of training to be fit for the first Saturday in September. O'Malley has not yet arrived, but is said to be on his way.</p>
        <p>On entering the Football Field on Monday I nas surprised to see the playing" pitch" looking so well and as the hoardingi around the field have had a new coat of lime wash everything looks bright and at its best—All that is required is for the team to secure a win in the opening engagement against Leigh for a rush on season tickets, which are expected to be ready before the opening match. '</p>
        <p>Patrons of the Warrington Rugby Club will be agreeably surprised to find that Fletcher-street and the back passages approaching the football ground have been paved. These approaches to the ground have long been in a wretched condition and a perfect nuisance to everyone of the thousands of spectators in wet weather. Many were the ugly comments by visitors, &amp;e,, but there is now no room for complaint as there is a good road right up to the admission gates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>IN CAMP.</head>
        <p>IN CAMP.</p>
        <p>LOCAL TERRITORIALS' MINTAIII?ES CN THE '6II.7LSH HILLS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Territorials - to give them their full description, .the 4th Battalion Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) are at present undergoing their annual 'training at Caerwys, Flint- shire, where they have this week participated in realistic manoeuvres on a large scale. The Weather conditions when the men entered camp on Sunday were dismal and depressing, but Monday saw a welcome iMprovem.nt, and for the remainder ,of the week there has been bright sunshine, thus enabling the plan of, campaign to be carried out in its entirety. Thqe was a large concourse of people to witness the deplirture of the Warrington Territorials on Sunday afternoon, the route to Bank Quay Station being well lined. The first section of the battalion left for Holywell at 1.45 and the second detachment followed about forty minutes later. Notwithstanding the fact that a drizzling rain fell persistently, there was much enthnsiasaa on the part of both Territorials and spectatov and the heartiness of the" send-off" was calculated to, put the Men in good spirits for the seven miles march along muddy roads which con- fronted them on leaving the train at Holywell. The battalion was under the command of Lieutenant- Colonel J. D. FairclO-agh and the other officers on parade were :—Major B. Fairclongh, Major F. M. Appleton, V.D. Captain G. R. . Mott, adjutant; Captains G. It. oresfield, R. Smith,C. F. Avery, 11 G-. Roberts, C. L. Williamson, A. B. Layton and H. Owen; Lieutenants F. Pb Monks, E. Fairclough, M. Woods, W. McClure, 0. L. Whittle, and L. S. Ilenshall ; 2nd Lieutenants H. 0. Adams, B. S. Townroe, C. Collingwood and A. Skinner. The regimental band under the direction of 4andmaster Richardson, and the bugle band in charge of Sergeant- Drummer Plinston, headed the procession. Among those on the platform to sed the men off were .Captain and Quartermaster A. F. G. Potter and ex-Colour- Sergeant Woods, who this year miss attending the Camp for the first time, the former for 15 years and the latter fer 22 years. . lhe .Warrington battalion forms part of the West Lancashire Division the Territarial Army. and altogether there are 12,000 Territorials unthr canvas at, Caerwys. The troops ore under the command of‘llajor- General Bethune, and consist of two brigades of artillery, one regiment of Royal Engineers, twelve tattaliens of intintry with Maxim., three field ambulance columns, and one transport and supply column. The territory from which they are drawn includes practically the whole of West Lancashire, from the Mersey to the lakes and from the coast to Bolton. General Bethune intends during the fort. nights training to give his large commani as much experience of the varied work to he done in warfare as can he mauaged without actual fightirig. Tactical schemes are being worked out over a large area of country, much of it enclosed and cultivated, EO that the conditions will closely resemble those under which the home defence army would have to fight in the event of an invasion. The ancient town of Caerwys is situated on the verge of the upland country, lying between the river Dee and the narrow valley—it might be called a ravine—through , which the Mold and Denbigh line runs, one of the stations being named "Caerwys." This ravine ends a few miles westward in the Vale of Clwyd, and it is often referred to as the Pass of Varies, for the reason that the Roman commander of that name entered the Vale of Clwyd by that gateway, and has left his name. at least behind him in Bodfari, the "abode of Varies," which is the hamlet situated just where the Pass ends in the famous Clwydian Vale. Bodfnri is also the name of the station between Caerwys and Denbigh. The maln camp of the West Lancashire Division is not far from the town of Caerwys on the uplant plateau and a smaller camp for the artillery and engineers is down in the ravine, neirer the station. Caerwys can be approached by good roads from Treformrand Holy- well, ,er Greenfield„ and in order to give sufficient statita accommodation for so large a body of troops three or four units detrained at each of these places,and the remainder at Caerwys, having thence a long and stiff march to their quarters. Trains filled with eager territorials continued to arrive at frequent intervals from quite ervly in the r orning, and the three stations, usually so quiet on the Sunday, were scenes of well-regulated bustle. In all 33 trains arrived at the tbree detraining points. The weather for the most part was wretchedly wet,and the men suffered much discomfort at the very outset of their camp life.</p>
        <p>The plan of the campaign involves the supposition that war has broken out in Europe and Great Britain is involved. An enemy 20,000 strong has landed at Llan- dudno and Barmouth, has captured Bettws-y-coed, and is advancing to Barmouth, Corwen, and Bala. On Monday morning operations were commenced in which the South Lancashire Brigade vk ith a South Lawashire</p>
        <p>company, Ist Brigade M.F.A., one field company, and the L. Divisional Engineers and the 3rd Field Ambulance took part. The skeleton enemy consisted of one capt .in, six mounted men, the 4th Howitzer, Brigade R.F.A., and three captains, three lieutenants, and twenty-five men of the Liverpool Brigade. The menceevres were cenducted on the bill over- looking the lower camp, neer the railway station. After the scouts had returned the army moved slowly in extended order and took possession of the commanding positions. Everything was done as in war, the flanks and rear being guarded, and altogether the officers had a good lesson in tactics. The men, too, worked hard, Carrying out their officers' orders with intelligence and precision. As showing the spirit in which the men are taking the work, the following incident is worthy of mention. One of the Maxim guns was without a horse, but the men hauled it up themselves. When a particularly steep portion of the path came in view someone muttered something about broken legs, but the men went up with a run. The gun party resolutely refused all assistance, and were loudly cheered by the other Territorials as they ascended the hill. • The operations lasted from 9 a.m. until after 5 p.m. Opportunities for recreation are not to numerous as could be desired. Creerwys village itself is but a tiny place with few attractions, while—at all events in the main camp—space is 5) limited that there is scarcely room to swine a cat, much less to indulge in a game of crieeet, football or rounders. One result is that the Y.M.C.A. tents—there are three altogether on the ground —are literally crowded out. The water supply is very far from being satisfactory. Some of the advance parties were entirely without water for several days, except for what they were able to beg from neighbouring farms; and even now, after tbe heavy rainfall of the week-end, the supply is far from being abundant. Captain and Mrs. Potter visited the South Lancashire camp on Mowery. Having regard to the heavy work which fell to the lot of the South Lancashires on Monday, the demand upon them on Tuesday was not of a very heavy nature. those • in command being satisfied with patting them through the skirmishing and other drills. It was the tare of the Liverpools to be tested. Under the personal direction of General Bethune, they were ordered to make the ascent of the huge range of hills which towers for hundreds of feet above the Caerwys Station, and on the side of the railway line from tbo main camp. It is surmised that those of the enemy who have landed at Barmouth and are making their way to Caermys via Co/.wen, will reaeh the camp from this direction. Accerdingly the-Liverpoels in full strength, were pushed out at that point, strongly sup- ported by the Artillery, which took the main road in the direction of Denbigh. General Bethune was early on the scene, and at the outset of the day's work for a considerable time the commanding officer was seen to be amongst his staff explaining the details of his scheme of operations to them. Tne men again did their work spleudidly. The day was very warm, but they never slackened. They were in the field from 8.30 a.m. till 4 p.m. with only such refreshment as could be contained in their water bottles and their knapsacks. On Wednesday no operations in connection with the main war scheme was carried on at all, and although all the various hrig tdes, under their respective com- manders, were exceedingly busy, their operations were confined to themeelves, without any reference to any of the other brigades in camp. The chief event of the day, however, was the departure at an early hour in the morning of the 4th Artillery Brigade under the com- mand 0:Colonel Molloy, for Flint. The brigade con- gets of 350 rteo and twelve guns. In concoction with this movement the men had received orders to be away for two days, the scheme itself being a forty-eight hours one, during which both officers and men were led to feel that they were on active service by having to sleep alongside of the guns as best they could. 'Urn the part of the infantry dnritg the ciq, f!r!!1a, zi;outinz and skirmishing was going on incessantly, and one a the most tacewing tied imposing sights of the campaign was the departure from camp for the country of the whole of the Liverpool Brigade. Thursday was oee of the hardest days of the weeleand the menceuvres proved most interesting. Again the weather was gloriously fine, and at times, in spite of the cooling breezes Of Caerwysethe sun was broiling hot, but, notwithstanding that, the troops are standing up to the work in splendid fashion. The tactical idea of Thursday's mancenvres was to teach the the officers and men how to reinforce a firing line with a brigade,. and how to withdraw the brigade under htavy the. Durieg the afternoon a party of six French gentle- men, including General Langtois who are inspect- ing the soldiers of this country, paid a visit to the camp and watchel the operations. The North Lancashire Brigade, with an A.S.C. Gem- pany and one battery rine ammunition column of the 4th Howitzer Brigade R.F.A., assembled at the head- quarters, Bryngwyn hull, very early. It was found that the invadors. bad come up from the direction of Denbigh St. Aeaph, and Prestatyn, and had captured a hill called Moe Eta. Not satisfied with this they had made a strong attack on Mount Cefudu, and took this also. From Mod l Efa the enemy were able to- fire on the defending army and do considerable damage.• General Bethune moved out the whole of the North Lancashire Brieade and the Ist Royal Field Artillery tinder Colonel-I'4loft° retake this hill. Covered by the artillery and inachiao gun?, the General v.as able to regain postession of the mountain, and the men rushed up in splendid fashion, finishing at the doreete. [laving been foiled in this direction' the enemy trjed again, and about noon the advance ofa mixed column from the direction of Denbigh began to make itself felt on the centre of No. 2 section of the line of resistance. This, at 3.39, developed into a grand attrck, and by 4.30 the defenders were dri,en in; but, mean- while the North Lancashire Brigade under the personal direction and commend of General Bethune, took up a position in Bryngwyu Park, in order to cover the retirement. It Vf as not until then, amidst the din and the clamour of a real battle, that the counter-attack was delivered, the big guns keeping up a continual roar for upwards of an hour until the last position was recaptured and the enemy drew off in the direction of Denbigh.</p>
        <p>3 he-other brigades in camp were engaged in outpost and covering operations, and were preparing themselves for the great thy which was expected on the mortoT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Lillie Soutter is a comedienne with a way of her , own, and it is a " way" that takes the fancy of h6r audience. There is something breezy and mirthful about her singing which is irresistible to the mind on pleasure bent. An uncommon turn this week is that of the Tintos in African gambols. The scene depicts very realistically a Zulu campfire, and the Tintos are made up into horribly real negro warriors. Their gambols are quite in keeping with the part and pro- voke laughter all over the house. The Sisters St. Julian show excellent taste and brightness in their duets and dances, and Jessie Starr strikes a new line as a female Harry Lauder. Her songs have a catchy chorus, and go with a swing. Tom Gott, the confiding comedian, takes the audience into his confidence with some very funny stories and songs in Yorkshire dialect. Judging from the frequent shrieks of laughter, Tom hit the mark every time. Leo proved a very smart. ventriloquist; Manson and West present a comedy interlude which depicts one of the funny sides of life when the husband conies home with the milk, and has to make ingenious explanations. The Palace pictures are an entertainment in themselves, and the story of the porous plaster is one of the most realistic and laughable things we have ever seen thrown on the screen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>APPLETON RIFLE CLUE.</head>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUE.</p>
