      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>S XTURDAY, AUGUST 7th. 1909.</p>
        <p>Parliament resumes its THE BUDGET consideration of the VICTORY. Budget proposals on Monday, under changed Itild happier circumstances, for the battle has eeil Won. The Opposition played their part with bitter persistence so long as the struggle i'thin the House continued, but during the tief holiday interval they have collapsed 'With a dramatic completeness which leaves 110. room to doubt that the Government is 110"9? free to pursue its legislative course illichainpe,red and unembarrassed. The speeches the PRIME MINISTER in London, of the ~'l-IA-NcELLoR at Limehouse, and Mr. 'RURCHILL in Edinburgh riddled the armour 'l)fthe Opposition,and coming on the top of the 17-election rebuffs they convinced theßudget- •s''eakers that they were playing losing a game. h° far the Opposition leaders in the House save given no sign of defeat,but the white flag • as been conspicuously hoisted by the- ‘,l'irnes," the "Morning Post" and the ;311)aily leaving little ground for Mr. I,..4Ll'ouit and his friends to stand upon unless ~"Y too are prepared to capitulate. The Mail"Jaily indeed, with characteristic 'ersPicacity has made a complete volte face ,41-Icl now blesses the Budget which it formerly ill,rseci• Unquestionably, the tide has turned. 'le Liberal Party never stood higher in the • stirnation of the people than it does to-day, •altd the vindication of our courageous and 1/Nressive Government is complete. the eve of entering upon the last • lj nases of the Budget struggle, let us briefly • 11:ev1ew the position of the Bill at the present • ',l,°/nent. There afe three Land Values aes proposed in the Budget Bill—the Verernent Tax, the Reversion Tax, and the -I,4cleveloped Land Tax. The Increment aC Proposes to take one-fifth of the incre- nt value that is createdon land after April, tYth, 1909. The tax is not to be retrospec- it,lve, and the site value of the land in April, 1.°09, is to lip taken as the normal or basal ,14e. The land is to be .franked up to that alue, and increment is to be charged only mi3on 'increases that take .place owing to Z)cial causes. lithe improvement is due to owner's own industry he will not be 114)ced• If it is due to agricultural causes e will not be taxed. He will only be taxed if it is due to the increased value 'of the ,ta. d Produced by the growth of and the demand for building sites. 'hat tax has now passed through Corn- ittee, and during that stage several inlportant alterations have been made which make it fall even more lightly. In the tl'st place, all agricultural increment is now oenipted. In the second, no occupying ,olvner of a" small holding" in the country I. of a small freehold in the town will pay .13113'" increment tax. A "small holding" will ,e a holding of the total value of £5OO, and a :3°47n freehold will mean a freehold under i-415 in London, under £26 in the big and under £l6 in rural districts. tiluch for the small holder, but there are ller concession/ for the ordinary land- :ll4l' ; the chief of those is that 10 per cent. all increment is to be freed from increment vslltY• In other words, the tax will not fall increments which are merely a fair nerturn for invested money. This abatement 10 Per cent, will act as a graduation on the 111,,cenilent tax. The owner of increment up £2OO will be let off £2O, or the owner of .4'ererlent up to £2,000 will be let off £lOO, t4cl -so on. Lastly, there are two small con- of some importance. There is the case twl'il°se who have bought land during the last ntY years at a higher price than that th".lch it commands to-day. In those cases the °riginal site value will be substituted for site value of to-day. Then there is the 'ssible case of land that achieves increment</p>
        <p>and then loses it—sold on 'a rise this year, perhaps, and sold again on a loss ten years hence. That land may rise again in value, and the increment may be restored. Is that increment to be taxed? No it is to be franked as having paid duty, and increment is only to be charged when it rises above the level of the previous tide. Finally, on reversion duty, as on increment duty,all agricultural land has been exempted: and for all the duties the State will pay for the valuation. So much for the concessions made up to the present date. Doubtless, the concessions on the Undeveloped Land Duty will be just as generous. Such being the state of affairs, can there be any justification for the complaint of harshness in the passing of these taxes? On the contrary, we are inclined to agree with the PRIME MINISTER when he says that Mr. LLOYD-GEORGE retains a mind almost too open to the plead- ings of the other side. It is only when they make demands that he is adamant.</p>
        <p>We hope the _last</p>
        <p>THE ROYAL chapter has now been VISIT.—THE reached in the story of MAYOR CRITICISED. the Royal visit to Warrington, for the longer it is prolonged and the less does it seem worth the telling. Our readers will be sufficiently familiar With what has taken place since the eventful day to obviate the necessity for much recapitulation of detail here. Suffice it to say that the circumstances leading up to the present of a diamond necklace to Princess Victoria were such as to cause grave dissatis- faction not only in the Council* but in the town itself. Warringtonians were all agreed that such a signal recognition of Her Royal Highness's birthday befitted the occasion. What they did not like was the hurried and unbusinesslike manner of the selection of the gift and its presentation. A committee was appointed at the Council meeting of July sth to select the present, but that committee was ignored and no explanation given after the necklace had been presented in the name of the town without the committee or the Council ever having seen or approved it. We said last week that the soreness thus caused might easily have been removed by a little tactful consideration on the part of the Mayor. Had he taken the members into his confidence even at the eleventh hour, he might have avoided the unpleasantness which culminated on Thursday evening in a special Council meeting, and a unanimous resolution tantamount to.a vote of censure on His Worship. Even Royal visits do not afford an excuse for ignoring the Standing Orders and the individual rights of Council members. Thursday's meeting was inevitable after the requisition to the Mayor had been disregarded and we can only express our satisfaction that the memberslssembled under such unusual And painful circumstances conducted them- selves with dignity and a due regard for their position as representatives of the people of Warrington. The protest they made was fully warranted by the situation, and we hope that its undeniable weight and serious- ness will not be lost sight of by the Mayor in that period of calm reflection which usually follows a rupture of peaceful relationships.</p>
        <p>T h e Inter-Depart- HALF-TIMERS TO mental Committee on BE ABOLISHED. the Half-time System has just issued its report. The Committee is unanimous in its condemnation of the system and reQommends its abolition as from January Ist, 1911. Like most topics a good deal can be said on both sides. It may be considered hard for dependent parents to be deprived of the assistance of their children when such assist- ance is badly needed. On the other hand should not the children's future be the dominant consideration? Parents should not b 6 allowed to handicap their children's chances for the future by exploiting them in their. youth. The report shows that the bulk of parents whosechildren are working half-time are in receipt of good wages and the selfishness or thoughtlessness of such parents is respon- sible for throwing a heavy burden on young shoulders. The system is shown to impair physical and mental fitness and it tells dis- astrously on the organization and work of the schools. A child who has to be at work at six o'clock in the morning and comes tired to school in the afternoon, cannot keep step with a neighbour's child who begins the day with school at nine. Economically also the system is bad, because it lessens the amount of adult employment, and it is doubtful if it is economical from a manufacturer's point of view. In Lancashire there are still many of the workers who approve the system, but prominent labour leaders who formerly sup- ported it are now against it, and there is a growing feeling both among the employers and the employed, that its abolition would conduce to the general welfare, and that there is no question that it is injurious to the children themselves. Very properly now-a-days the tendency is to place the interests of the children first.</p>
        <p>In Committee of Supply THE RISK OF the Prime Minis ter INVASION, took the opportunity to make an important statement on the conclusions of the Imperial Defence Committee regarding the security of Great Britain against possible invasion. This is a problem which has twice been made the subject of prolonged and scrupulous investigation by the Committee—once at the instance of Mr. Balfour and once at the instance of the present Prime Minister. The deliberate and unanimous conclusion arrived at is that a force of very much less than 70,000 men could not possibly evade our fleet, so long as we maintain our supremacy at sea; while, if we lost, even for a short period, that supremacy, our downfall would be certain without invasion. In order to leave an ample margin the War Office is organizing our defence on a basis for dealing with a hypothetical invasion of 70,000 men, though.. it is not believed that 70,000 men could ever get over. There are two all-important consequences dependent upon the conclusion at which the National Dqfence Committee have arrived. One is that the amibitious military schemes which Lord Roberts favours would be a source of weakness to this country, by deflecting the necessary expenditure and attention from the Navy. The other is that since it is the Navy upon which we chiefly rely for repelling invasion, and entirely from preventing us from being starved into sub- mission, we cannot afford to run any risks in cutting our expenditure upon that branch too fine. Mr. McKenna was able to show, without any difficulty, in Tuesday's debate, that the Government is fully alive to the needs of our naval defence. It is true that six or seven more destroyers ought to have been in commission at the present moment, and would have been but for the strike on the North-East coast, but the Government had left sufficient margin for such con- tingencies and there was not the slightest risk.</p>
        <p>The attitude of the CLEAR THINKING critics of the War Office versus in regard to aerial RECKLESS navigation may be SPENDING. fairly described as— "Let us spend money like water,and hang the differential calculus." Mr. Haldane believes that scientific methods will be more successful in the end than reck- less profusion, althougU the process of " clear thinking:' and of scientifically mapping out the lines upon which an advance can most effectively be made may seem slower. The precedent of our sub- marines, in which the same policy has put us ahead of all other competitors justifies such an attitude. And Mr. Haldane might, without any lack of modesty, have pointed to his own Territorial scheme as an example of the superiority of science and economy over rule of thumb and extrava- gahce. Mr. Haldane has reversed the experience of his predecessors at the War Office. At the outset his scheme met with little but criticism and distrust ; to-day it meets with little but eulogy. Its success is to be seen in a concrete embodiment at Caerwys and on Salisbury Plain. And other nations are studying it carefully with a view to imitation.</p>
        <p>Germany,that favoured NEW GERMAN land which tariff TAXES. reformers are so fond of depicting as a model of what tariff reform can achieve, is just now experiencing the blessings (?) of Pro- tectionist finance in full measure. The problem of how to make ends meet has become so acute that in order to raise the funds necessary to replenish the national exchequer, the Government has resorted to a list of new taxes which will be severely felt in the German households. This is how the " Standard " correspondent wiring on Sunday describes the state of affairs :</p>
        <p>_ "The effect of the fresh taxes imposed in order to cover the anticipated annual deficit of £24,000,000, in the Government revenues was unpleasantly felt by the taxpayers ofGermany this morning. tip till the present moment the man in the street has regarded the question of fresh taxes more as a theoretical idea, and it is only now, when his pocket is assailed, that he realises' that he has to contribute tdPwards the increased naval programme and so-called social reforms. Last night the warehouses of Berlin were besieged with clamouring crowds purchasing matches, coffee, tea, cigars, etc., for to-day the new taxes are imposed. Last night the Berlin housewife could buy ten boxes of matches for ten pfennige (1d.),0r for fifteen pfennige (11d.) at the most. To-day they cost 31d. Coffee has risen 3d. per pound, with the result that many thrifty people have resorted to a substitute for coffee called " malt coffee." Matches are to be replaced by the tinder and flint apparatus, of bygone ages, and all kinds of devices are being resorted to in order to avoid a great increase in household expenses. White beer, one of the most popular drinks in the east of Berlin,was formerly purchased at the ordinary restaurants and beerhouses for id. per glass, and will now cost nearly half as much again. Matches have been purchased by hundreds of thousands of boxes from Sweden and Norway, and it is safe to say that, so far as Berlin is con- cerned, households are stocked for months to come. Much disappointment has been evinced in official circles at the devices which have been resorted to by the,•giard-hit taxpayer to evade fresh impositions. Scrupulous are has been exercised by Excise officials that no one in the whole of the Empire should avoid any of the fresh taxes. Those householders who, prudently laid in a large stock of goods which they antici- pated would be subject to taxation do not escape. House-to-house visitations take place, and every one possessing more than a stipulated amount of taxable goods is bound to pay a certain .duty according to the new scale. Cigarettes and cigars are tied round with so-called Lcibbinde or paper bands, denoting the amount 'of duty to be paid. Travellers entering Germany who have hitherto been able to avoid Customs duties on dutiable articles will in the future have to render tribute. All the Excise regulations are to be enforced with greater care than has hitherto been the case. it is now generally believed that the deficit of £25,000.000 will not be made good by any of the fresh taxes imposed, and that it will be absolutely necessary, before many months have elapsed, and after the Reichstag meets again, to find further ways and means of filling what appears to be a bottomless pit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>NTrEs AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NTrEs AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary debates on the Budget will begin again on Monday next, and the consideration of the. bill will proceed from day to day until it passes the House of Commons. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield: M.P., and Mrs. Cros- field, Who have been paying a visit to Mrs. John Crosfield at Colwyn Bay, on Wednes- day motored over to the Territorial Camp at Caerwys, there they were warmly received by the officers and men of the local battalion. To-day Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield will be at Warrington distributing the prizes at the final of the Works Bowling Competition at Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>* * * The Borough Surveyor has been requested to prepare a plan for the better seating of the members of the Council in the Council Chambe.r. The instruction is not very illuminating but we presume an enlargement of the Chamber is contemplated. The members cannot be disposed to any better advantage in the present confined and unsuit- able area. In its present state the Council Chamber is anything but a hygenic meeting place. * * *</p>
        <p>What has been the re-cord during the past fortnight of Parliamentary rest? One of the most remarkable uprisings that has ever taken place in this country outside a General Election. July is generally regarded as a "close season" for public meetings, but during this month hundreds of meetings have been held in every part of the country— meetings large andmeetings small—meetings in the towns and meetings in ,the country villages—on behalf of the Budget. There has rarely in our time been such a striking manifestation of popular enthusiasm. * * *</p>
        <p>What is there on the other side? The narrow, dogged opposition of a small class, assisted by all their myrmidons, professional and otherwise, to taxes which are asked from them in order to meet the very expenditure for which they have clamoured. This class was ready enough to ask for the Dread- noughts, but they are not very Auick to pay for them. " What has happened since to alter their tone ? " asked Mr. Lloyd-George at Limehpuse. "Simply that we have sent in the bill." It is an event -which causes surprising changes in the point of view. * * *</p>
        <p>There is no disguising the fact, even in the enemy's camp, that the Budget had seriously damaged the Tariff Reform cause all over the country. People are wisely turning from illusory tariffs to taxes on unearned increments for a solution to the problems of unemployment arid trade depression. Even "The Daily Mail" has lost some of its faith in Tariff Reform, as evidenced by the follow- ing confession which appeared in its columns the other day: Among those who watch most closely the fluctuations of public sentiment there is a pretty</p>
        <p>general agreement that during the last few months the position of the Government has been strengthened, while the cause of Tariff Reforin has stood stationary. • * *</p>
        <p>From the minutes of the Street Improve- ment and Lighting Committee we see that Messrs. H. Milling and Co., have received permission to retain the sky sign upon their premises in Horsemarket-street for one year on payment of .ElO. This sign was we believe put up in ignorance of the Borough Bye-laws at considerable expense, and has only remained for the best part of this year by the courtesy of the Corporation. It is an excellent sign, but it is not an ornament to Bridge-street or Horsemarket-street. The idea of a general adoption of sky signs by enterprising tradesmen at once suggests the necessity for such a bye-law as happily exists. • * *</p>
        <p>All the railways have now issued their accounts for the half-year. In general the results are good. The London and North- Western Company pays the same dividend, but seems to have done about £64,000 better on the half-year. The Great Western has about £30:000 more to dispose of than twelve months ago. • The London, Brighton, and South Coast Company pa's a dividend a quarter per cent. higher; and the London, Chatham, and Dover gives its shareholders half per cent. more. These brighter results are largely due to economies, but the increased traffics on the " heavy " lines tell a tale of increasing trade which is even more convincing than that of the Board of Trade returns. If the railway figures are reliable we are already mounting the further side of the trade depression.</p>
        <p>Since the last Council meeting the Health Committee has again reconsidered applica- tions for licences under the Poisons and Pharmacy Act from the two local tradesmen whose names have been prominent in the matter, and has again refused to grant the licences. We have had occasion to point out before that there is more senti- ment than logic OIT the side of these gentle- men, and we• are glad the Committee are standing firm. It cannot be too often affirmed that the purchase of poisons should be made difficult and not easy for the public. Councillor Roberts still refuses to abide by the decision of the Committee, and on Tuesday will move a special resolution that the licences be granted. If the Council has any faith at all in the Health Committee and its professional advisers, it will refuse to be swayed by special pleading, however eloquent.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Show. • The twentieth annual show of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society will be held in Walton Park on Thursday, August 19th. Entries positively close on Tuesday next. The Queen's Own Yorkshire Dragoons Band will be in attendance.</p>
        <p>Open-Air Politics. The Progressive Labour League will resume its open-air campaign on Monday evening when another meeting will be held near Arpley Cannons. Mr. Robert Lewis, of the Lancashire and Cheshire Liberal Federation, will be the principal speaker.</p>
        <p>Athletic Successes. At the Rainford Sports, on Tuesday, J: Hands, of Warrington, won the first prize in the mile novice cycle (open) race, while H. Holbrook, also of Warring- ton, was third in the mile flat race (open). At the Victoria Athletic's Club Sports, Stoke, on Tuesday, C. Berry, of Warrington, carried off the first prize in the hal t-mile" cycle race. Music in the Park. The following is the programme which the Salvation Artily Band will play in the Park on Thursday next at 7.15 pm. under the conductorship of Deputy-Band- master G. B. Rowe :—March, " Perseverance" (Coles) ; selection, " Allelu;a " (Hawkes) ; selection, "Songs of joy" (Slater) ; selection, "Swiss Melodies" (Slater) ; march, " Shipley " (Tibb) ; selection, "Songs of praise" (Hawkes) ; selection, "Echoes from Calvary" Hill; "God save the King."</p>
        <p>BABY CARRIAGES AND Go-cAnTs.—Clearance All prices reduced.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. A Rev. H B. Meyer to Visit Warrington. _ _</p>
        <p>Rev. F. B. Meyo-r, 8.A., is expected to reach London on his return from his tour in the Far East about September 20th. Arrangements have been made by the Speaker's Department of the National Free Church Council for Mr. Meyer to visit Warrington on November 2nd and address meetings in connection with the Warrington Free Church Council. This tour will alsoinclude a visit by Mr. Meyer to Bangor on November 24th. At each town Mr. Meyer will preach in the afternoon, hold an over-tea conference and address a public meeting at night.</p>
        <p>Warrington Women's Circle. On Thursday afternoon 140 of the members were entertained at l'enketh House with the characteristic hospitality of Mr. and Mrs. James Dodds. The weather was perfect and games were arranged in the garden,and groups taken. After a bountiful tea under the trees,Mrs. Clatworthy and Mrs. Wood on behalf of the members presented Miss Dodds with an oak hall- stand in view of her approaching marriage. Mrs. Moore and Miss Paul thanked Mrs. Dodds for the afternoon's pleasure. Mrs. Dodds, Mrs. Parker, and Mrs. Letheren addressed a few words to the guests on leaving. • CYcLks.—End of season. Clearance sale. Great reductions.—J. S. GARLAND, Bridge se., Warring- ton.—Advt. From Stockton Heath to Eastham.</p>
        <p>The Eastham Hotel and Ferry Co., Ltd., propose to institute a passenger service between Stockton Heath Swing Bridge and Eastham, via the Ship Canal. On Thursday they held a trial trip with the paddle steamer" Sapphire," which left the Bridge ,at 2.30 and reached Eastham about 4.15. There was a merry Tarty of about 200 guests on hoard, including the Mayoress of Warrington, Mr. Mills, a Director of the Company, Captain Hill, and Captain Waring. The beautiful weather contributed to the enjoyment of a most successful trip. On arrival at Eastham the party amused themselves in the gardens and had tea, ultimately returning to Warrington by rail from Liverpool. The steamer will make its first public trip on Thursday next, leaving Stockton Heath at 2.39, return fare 2s. Gd.</p>
        <p>Town Planning. e A National Congress on Town Planning was opened on Wednesday at Port Sunlight. Professor Adshead, of Liverpool University, read a paper on the planning of suburbs, and Professor Berlepsch Valendas, of Munich, dealt with town planning in acient and modern times.—During the discussion which followed, Mr. Alderman Bennett (Warrington) said the papers to which they had listened were properly intended for an assembly of philosophers rather than the aveYage municipal elector. The Congress had found them deeply interesting, but how were they to get these ideas into the heads of the ratepayers? Two points that had been referred to he wished to pick out. The first was the way in which towns had been mutilated, and whatever beauties they possessed destroyed, by the abqminable way in which railways had been allowed to enter them. The second we the desirability in all municipal work of subordinating the eccentricities of indiViduals to the simpler tastes of the community.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Band at the White City Contest. Money prizes to the value of £4OO, a championship shield, and valuable musical instruments were com- peted for by a number of well-known brass bands at the :White City, Manchester, on Saturday afternoon. The test piece was a selection from Verdi's works.— The first prize, 30 in money and the White City 1909 Championship Shield, was won by the Goodshaw Band, the other winning bands being Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. (2nd) ; Gossages' Soap Works (3rd) ; Lindley (4th) ; Cleckheaton (sth) ; Foden's, Limited (6th) ; and Shaw (7th).---Mr. It. C. Stephens said the judges had asked him to state their opinion of the general playing of the day. The first three bands were very close, being divided by only one or two points, Later in. the evening the three bands who had won the principal prizes played in the open air under Mr. Stephen's lead.</p>
        <p>Local Will. The will of the late Mr. Peter Holt Lake, who carried on business as off-licence holder, in Knutsford- road, Warrington, has been proved at £3,006.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Stockton Heath Garden Society.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath Garden Society.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening about fifty inembers of this Society had the pleasure of visiting the gardens of Mr. H. Roberts and Mr. F. W. Monks at Hill Cliffe. The gardens which are charmingly situated are look- ing at their best, and the visit was much enjoyed. Shrewsbury Floral Fete. - - - -</p>
        <p>Shrewsbui7y Floral Fete, which is considered to be the most magnificent flower show held in the Kingdom will be held on Wednesday and Thursday, August 18th and 19th, when over i',1,200 will be offered in prizes. The bands of H.M. Co!dstream Guards, H.M. Irish Guards, and Shropshire Yeomanry Cavalry will be in attendance. Special excursions will be run from-all parts.</p>
        <p>-Death of Mrs. Arthur Isaac. Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Arthur Isaac, which took place at her resid- ence, 19, Wilson-Patten-street, yesterday morning, after an illness of only a week. The deceased lady. who was only 29 years of age, had a tooth extrticted last week, and septic poisoning ensued. This brought on pneumonia and pleurisy. A specialist was summoned from Manchester on Thursday, but he could not ofror any hopes for her recovery. Mrs Isaac was a Wesleyan and worshipped at Bold-street Church. Great sympathy is felt with the husband and one child in their bereavement. .</p>
        <p>Free Church Carden Party. On Saturday last the members and friends of the Warrington Free Church Council were entertained to a garden party at Penketh Lodge by Mr. C. E. Parker (President of the Council) And Mrs. Parker. About fifty ladies and gentlemen attended and enjoyed themselves with tennis, cricket, and rambling in the garden, where they had permission to help themselves, to the fruit. At 445 the party sat down to tea. During the day selections were admirably played by the Penketh Tannery Prize Brass Band, and at the close Mr and Mrs. Parker were cordially thanked for their hospitality.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. R, T. Daintith. We regret to announce the death of Mr. Richard Daintith, which took place at his residence, Sankey- street, on Thursday morning. Theldecelsed gentleman who was 78 years of age, had enjoyed good health all his life, his boast being that he had never been to a doctor for over 50 years. About a fortnight ago he suddenly declined in health, as was natural at sach an advanced age, and died on Thurs- day. He was a strong Conservative, and a Church- man and attended Holy Trinity Church. About 25 years ago he retired from his occupation as a pattern-maker at Messrs. Roger 'Endes, Scotland- road, and had passed his later years in quiet content- ment His favourite pastimes were gardening and walking. He leaves a family of four sons and three daughters. The funeral will take place at the Warrington Cemetery to=morrow at 2.15 p.m.</p>
        <p>Warrington Cycling Club. On account of the adverse weather the tour arranged for last week end to Buxton and Derbyshire was abandoned. On Wednesday evening the bowl- ing section of the club met the St. Helens C.C., at the Sankey Green in a match which resulted in a win for the Warrington C.C., by 60 in the single games and 20 in the doubles; the aggregate scores being Warrington C.C. 110, St. Helens C.C. 30. A concert for the benefit of the visitors followed, and completed a very enjoyable inter club fixture. A return of the summer weather favoured the run to Altrincham on Thursday and the road being in excellent condition there was ,a good muster of members. The club'sv arrangements for the coining week are to-days run•to Davenham ; bowling at. Sankey on Wednesday, and the run to Bollington on Thursday. TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BATCETS, SUIT CASES, etc. Prices to suit S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st, Wurrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The "Angelus." The Rev. R. E. Letheren, pastor, of Golborne-street Baptist Church, is giving a course of sermons on Millet's great picture "The Angelus." Last Sunday evening he preached an introductory sermon and made reference to the article on the picture by Mr. A. Mounfield in the" Examiner" of a few ,weeks ago. The subject of the address was " The life of the artist" which was divided into three parts, the period of preparation, giving an outline of his boyhood and youth and the influences upon him of his home training and. early surroundings • the period of apprentice- ship to art during which he was discovering his own powers, and the period of perfecting. Descriptions were given of some of his other pictures and characteristics of his work. To-morrow Mr. Letheren will preach on the following subjects :—Morning" A Glorious Sanctuary " • evening " The Gospel of the Common place "this being the second of the series of addresses on the Angelus.</p>
        <p>The Infirmary. A well attended, meeting of the Workingmen's Infirmary Committee was held at the Dispensary on Thursday evening. Alderman T. Burton presided. The Secretary (Mr. J. H. J. Hampson) submitted the statement of accounts in connection with the recent open-air musical festival and the same was approved. In rendering his financial statement he also reported that the amount standing to the credit of the committee to date was £299105. 2d.against £227 3s. for the corres- ponding period of last year. He, however, impressed upon the Committee the necessity of increasing the contributions as the extension of the institution had called for greatly increased cost of main- tenance and it was expected that• the deficit at the close of the year would amount to no less a sum than £3,200. The fund was not confined to the big firms who employed a large number of men, but the small firms who employed only one or two were equally welcome: Any contribution, however small would be welcomed? Experience showed that by a more general adoption of the system of weekly col- lections in the various workshops, greater satisfac- tion was given to the subscribers. He hoped that more firms would adopt the weekly system of collec- tions in aid of the Institution. It was decided to hold the annual Concert on October 2, and arrange- ments were left in the hands of the executive com- mittee. A vote of condolence was passed with the relatives of the late Mr. William Croskill, who was formerly one. of the members of the committee. Mr. Burton referred to the good work done by the Institu- tion and said he hoped the workingmen of Warring- ton would do their, very bests in its interests. GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., £8 Bs., and ..Cl2 12s. Others at higher prices. Fully guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,—J. R. WooD, 47. London rd., Stockton Heath. Advt. Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay. The teachers and choir had their annual picnic on Monday, the place selected being Llandudno and Colwyn Bay. The party, to the number of about 60, left Warrington by the 6.49 train, arriving at Llandudno shortly after 9 o'clock. The morning was spent itiostly in climbing up Great Ormets Head. At about 3.15 the party went by electric tram to Rhos, walking from there to "Bryn Eithin "the residence of Mrs. Crosfield, who had very kindly invited them up for tea. After tea, the time was very pleasantly spent in walking about the grounds, playing bowls, croquet, &amp;c. Mrs. A. H Crosfield (who arrived at the he during the afternoon, along with Mr. A. H. Crosfield) threw herself very heartily and very successfully into the task. of entertaining the guests. Before leaving, the choir sang a few hymns, and Mr. T. Dale (Sunday SchOol Superintendent) took the opportunity of expressing the heartiest thanks of the party for the kindly way in which they had been received. Mrs. Crosfield replied in a few well chosen words, saying how glad she was to meet the friends from the Mission. She was extremely interested in the work of the Mission, and was very glad indeed to hear that the efforts put forward were so successful. Mr. A. H. Crosfield made a few remarks, in the course of which he referred to the branch of the League of Help formed last year at the Mission. He was confident that the work on which they were engaged at Westniinster (committee work of which the general public heard very little) arranging a scheme of compulsory insurance against unemployment and sickness, would very materially reduce the necessity for such an organisation as the League of Help. He looked upon the Work of the Government in this direction, together with the scheme for Labour Exchanges, as being probably the most important that could possibly be undertaken, and be was glad to have the honour of having a share in that work. The proposals he had mentioned were the necesssry adjuncts to the Old Age Pensions and Workmen's Compensation Acts. At about eight o'clock a start was made for the station, the party leaving Colwyn Bay by the 9.15 train, War- rington not being reached until atout 12.30 (typical holiday travelling). Throughout the whole of the day the weather was beautiful, and the outing was voted as being one of the most enjoyable the party 1 ad ever held.</p>
        <p>Padgate Bowlers. An interesting bowling match took place on Thursday at Padgate between Padgate Green-lane and Padgate West End, on Dr. Bennett's green. Green-lane won five events out of six. The respective teams were: Green-lane, Messrs. P. Bennett, F. Maginn, F. Wright, and G. Pendlebury ; West End, Messrs. Dr. Bennett, L. Bennett, G. Walker, and S. Walker. The corres- ponding match last week resulted in a draw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH OF MR. JOHN WHITE.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF MR. JOHN WHITE.</p>
        <p>Warrington has lost another of its most highly respected townsmen by the death: of Mr. John White, who passed away with pAinful suddenness at the offices of the firm of which he was the head—Messrs. John White and Company, estate agents and surveyors, Bold-street—at 10.30 yesterday morning. Before leaving his home "Moore Hall, for Warrington, Mr. White complained of not feeling very well. Whilst on his way to the office in a cab he suddenly became seriously ill, and when he arrived there it was seen that he was in a dying condition_ Dr. Bowden was sent for but Mr. White expired in two or three minutes, heart failure being the cause of death. The news of the tragic occurrence created a profound sensation in the town, for Mr. White had been pursuing his work, both public and private, with his usual activity up to the last. So recently as last Monday he attended the show of the Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society at South- port, and was in his usual genial spirits. By all sections of the commuity his loss will be sincerely deplored, for his many sterling qualities endeared him to a large circle.</p>
        <p>,A.f.,.. ..'Mt:.:lt4,-,1'5•f:\'_',.:..,,7.4&gt;y*,:4!:',Q,,k,k,,. --7...,:::'.-,:'-' ',43'. '.•:',‘,'-''''' ~A-s:i.:°W.t.;4Mt.4Xit, ~,,,..,:-..,•::--2,. -.'„7.-1-,...i,. - '••••':,":i,!'t-i4ati4iA'S .:'.:,.‘r.4. ..,'..-•.',',,....-- -,:...t:n.f..•.,,,.....N\A: 5:,:,,A,.• .-,. : . -,5,%"' ..tt.:!'j. ~ --•,„.. - ~.: -4.1,4_1:':i.z..A... ' :`,--1'.4; 14t',,,' •",5*:,-.:;tpik • . -,-;.,,,,....,::.--4. ~ ,t ' . &gt;. ~,,. „,.‘?,,r''.' '''• '''''i4 ~'' SS ' is:: .4 .::: ', .?&lt;@, ~e.&gt; ' '-: W.:::•.,:' ',..;,. ' - t.S*.i.tBXt,i'. ~ ~,,V,"!'....iiiis' .... ,&lt;-.4,,...: '',.,' .',-,A4,..ev. • .:, ',-.....: ... .:...&gt;t. ~ ~..:_,... ..,.-.=.-.••••,..,--.-.. ..,A ~4.;-._g:;•t:f,'-'. '''''f'•.M.,4l-,0,';'.:.:,,,....47.4,..•,..t.,,,z0ii,..5v . c</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>?hag, 'ME LAN, MIL SOHN. WHITE, 3.?</head>
        <p>?hag, 'ME LAN, MIL SOHN. WHITE, 3.?</p>
        <p>2?irtc3,</p>
        <p>Mr. White was born at Aston, near Preston Brook; in the year 1839, his father at that time being agent to Sir Arthur Aston. The whole of his life had been spent in Warrington or the immediate neighbourhood, for in 1840 the late Mr. Henry White opened an office in Warrington as an estate agent and surveyor, and Mr. John White early began to take part in the busi- ness. In 1860 he became a partner, the firm being known as Henry White and Son. Its present title is John White and Company, agents to the late Lord Wimmarleigh's estate, and the estates of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Colonel Ireland Blackburne, Mr. T. H. Lyon, Admiral Parry and others. As a young man Mr. White associated himself with the local Volunteer Corps, and was successively private,sergeant, quarter- master, ensign, lieutenant and captain of No. 1 company, having been gazetted as captain in the year 1867. When in May, 1862, the 9th Lancashire Rifle Volunteers were presented with their colours, Ensigns F. Greenall and John White had the honour of receiving those symbols at the- hands of Mrs. Quekett, wife of the late Rector of Warrington, in the presence of 4,000 people, Mr. White gave a great deal of his time to public work and had rendered conscientious and greatly valued services in many capacities. He was one of the first three gentlemen to represent War- rington on the Lancashire 'County Council, Mr. F. Monks and Mr. F. R. Roberts being the other two. He was appointed a borough magistrate in 1879, a magistrate for Lancashire in 1891, and for Cheshire in 1896, and he had held the office of chairman el the Visiting Justices for Haydock Asylum. He was a zealous worker on behalf of the Warrington Infirmary and was treasurer of the building fund which was subscribed for the erection of the building. For many years he discharged the duties of hon. treasurer with cOnspicuous Success. Mr. White was keenly devoted to the interests of agricul- ture and for over 40 years ltid been connected with the Council of the Royal Lancashire Agriculture Society. For over ten years he had been chairman of the Council, while four years ago he was made an hon. vice-president, and,in recognition of his services, he was presented at Preston with an address and a silver casket. He was also a warm supporter of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society. Mrs. White was a good musician and an accomplished performer on the organ. So far back as 1857 he held the offices of treasurer and organist of the Warrington Musical Society. He was the first secretary of the Hospital Saturday and Sunday Funds. In his early . . _</p>
        <p>days- he was an e-nthusiastic Cricketer and acted a-s hon. secretary and captain of the Warrington Club about the year 18G9, when the club plated in a field which now forms part of Bank Park. Mr. White and the late Mr. James Fairclough played for Lancashire in its first county match—and as they were first at the wicket they had the distinction of being the two first men to bat for Lancashire. Mr. White was a staunch Conservative and an old camp ligner, throwinr, himself into the Parliamentary contests in Warrington with great vigour. For twenty .years he was treasurer of</p>
        <p>tie Conservative Association. He acted as vice- chairman for five years and as chairman for fourteen years. When he resigned the chairmanship in 1907 he was presented with a large and handsome silver rose bowl, while Mrs, White was the recipient of a beautiful diamond and pearl brooch.</p>
        <p>Mr. White% was a devoted son of the- Church of England, and nothing delighted him more than to assist in the advancement of its work. He had much to do with the erection of St. Ann's and St. Luke's Churches, and was hon. organist and choirmaster of the former for several years. For a long time he had officiated as chairman of the Warrington branch of the English Church Union, and of the South-West Lanca- shire District Union. He had also held the offices of churchwarden and lay delegate of Christ Church, Latchford, and Daresbury Parish Church. Up to the time of his death he was a member of the ißuilding Committee of the Liverpool Cathedral and a member of the Committee of the Clergy Daughters' Institution. In 1864 he married, Mary, daughter of the Rev. W. R. Melville, Rector o' Matlock. He took a great interest in amateur theatricals and was at one time an acting member of the Warringtgn Dramatic Society and .the Latchford Amateur Dramatic Society. As will be seen from the above brief sketch, Mr. White had led an exceed- ingly busy life‘and his death will create a gap which will be very difficult to fill. Deep sympathy is felt with the widow in her tragic bereavement.</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL. The funeral will probably take place on Tueschi morning at Daresbury Church,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>MARRIED WOMEN TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>MARRIED WOMEN TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>The Costs on the Corporation.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Genenal Purposes Committee on Tuesday the Town Clerk reported that the solicitors acting for the plaintiffs in the cases of Stansfield v. the Corporaton and Grounds v. the Corporation were pre— pared to stay both actions on payment of 50 guineas towards their costs which the Town Clerk stated were over 200 guineas. Resolved, that the sum of 50 guineas be paid towards the costs.</p>
        <p>POINT IN CHEQUE LAW. Sarah Ann Salloway, a widow at Birmingham Assizes on Wednesday, claimed £5OO from the estate of Edward &amp;noway, a relative, who lodged with her, on the strength of a cheque given her by him. The cheque was presented but not cashed, pending certification of the signature. Meanwhile the drawer died, and the question was whether there was power of recovery from the executors. Mr. Justice Grantham found for the plaintiff, and ordered the executors to prg the money claimed with interest. Stay of execution was granted.</p>
        <p>ENGLISH FRUIT ONLY.—GOOSEBERRIES 10d. per doz. ; RED CURRANTS, Is. CA* per doz. • GREENGAGES, 3s. per doz. ; BLACK— CURRANTS; 4s. per doz. McDERADarr, "Cheapest Man Alive." • MARKET &amp; BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.— Before the Mayor (in the chair), and Messrs. A. J. Jolley, H. Thornton, G. Pilling and T. Locker. BENEFIT OF TELE DOUBT.—Rebecca Chadwick, middle-aged woman, of 41, LongTord-s: reet, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in an entry near the Barley Mow Inn on Saturday evening, and also with stealing from a room at the B.rley Mow, a bag con- taining 11h. of tomatoes of the value of 41d. The Chief Constable stated that Benjamin Bbtden, labourer, living at 57, Nicholson-street, went into the Inn on Saturday night with a friend and they had some refreshments. The prisoner came into the same room and on her rleparttue a bag containing tomatoes was missed inquiries were made by Detective Metcalf and the prisoner was suspected of the robbery. She returned. to the Barley Mow and the prosecutor and the officer -went there and accused the woman of the robbery. She had a bag in her hand and when asked what it contained replied "Sweets." It was opened and found to contain the tomatoes. The prisoner then threw the tomatoes in the face of the officer. She was taken into custody scd at the Police Station two other tomatoes were found upon her, making up the whole of the former contents of the lartg.—Prisoner now said she picked the tomators up in mistake.—For having been drunk and disorderly she was fined ss. and costs. With reg trd to the second offence, the Mayor said the Bench had a doubt as to whether she intended to steal, tad there would, therefore, be no conviction against her. DESTROYED HIS CLOTHING.—Thomas Adamson, R casual inmate of the vagrant ward of the workhouse, was charged with baying on Saturday evening destroyed his clothing. He admitted the offence, but raid the clothes were not fit to wear. He was sent to gaol for . a month with hard labour.</p>
        <p>MESDA.Y.-13pfore Messrs. 11. RobettF. (chairman) and</p>
        <p>C. Jackson-Holmes. FACTORY PRoSECUTION.—Mcssrs. H. D. Poehin and Co., Ltd., Liverpool-road, were prosecuted for atowing a fly4aeel to run unsecurely fenced. The works raanager appeared for the firna.—Mr. John Herbert W.lrren, inspector of factories, prosecuted, and said be v'sited the defendant's works and found a fly wheel riled for drivicg the crushing plant unfenced. He also f und 11, e other smaller ones unsecurely fenced.—Tbe m.uager stated th-4 he had been manager over the p es( at works for 34 years, and they bad never been p ocetded against for anything of that kind before,— .-t fine of 10s. and costs (El.) was imposed. MOTOR WIInoCT A Wardle, a merchant., residing at the Norton Arms Hotel, was summoned for not having a head light attached to his motor car on the 27th July.—A constable stated that about 10 minutes past 11 on Thursday evening he was on duty at the Wilderspool cressiega when be saw a motor car come down the Chester-road in the direction of the bridge. He noticed there was no head light on the car but he obtained the number. On being called upon to stop the driver put up his band and shouted ' It's all right." Subsequently defendant told him that the lamp had gone out at the end of the new road and thought that it was not necessary to relight it.—Mr. T.S.Steel, who defended, apologised for the defendant's absence saying that he bad an importint business appointment. He had instructed him (Mr. Steel) to give an explanation of the case. He had been for a motor drive to Whitley and on his return as he was passing through Daresbnry be lighted his lamps. After going a short distance his right hand lamp went out. Mr.' Wardle got out of the car and put the left hand lamp in the place and when at tie end of Chester-road that lamp went out also. He was going very slowly and having only a few 3hrds to go he did not think it worth -while to re-light the lamp.—Defendant was ordered to pay the costs of the case (3s. fid.). No conviction was recorded. . .</p>
        <p>LIKE A MADAIAN.—WiIIiaM Dean was charged with being drunk and diso.derly in Winwick-strcet the 'previous day and &amp;so with assaulting P.C. Thomas Gihscn. Prisoner. was ejected from a public house and created a disturbance. On the way to the Police Station be knocked P.C. Gibson and Detective Clough down, gave Gibson several blows on the head and licked him on the knee. They had to get the assist- RECO of P.C. 1-lollsnd before they could put the " snaps " cn him. Prisoner tehavtd like a madman all the way eown to the Police Station.—Detective 'Clough in corrobc rating said he had not sem a man so ugh as prisoner for some time.—A fine of 10s. and -costs or 14 days imprisonment was imposed. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. C. White.</p>
        <p>DrscHancEn.—William Bradshaw was charged en 'remand with stealing a pair of men's woollen pants from some person unknown.—The Chief Constable stated that. the case was before the Court a few days ago, and prisoner was remanded in order to trace the ownership of the pants. The prisoner had made a -statement regarding them and it had been communi- cated to Wrelhan and the surrounding district. but no information had been forthcoming. He therefore had to alternative but to ask that the prisoner be dis- charged.—This was accordingly done. A MILITARY ABSENTEE.—WiIIiam Branyard was charged with being an absentee from the South Lanca- shire Reel:cent stationed at Orford llarracks.—A constable stated that he was on duty at 11.35 p.m. in Academy-street when be saw the prisoner with a great •overcoat and wearing a pair of shabby boots. Witness asked him if he bad deserted from the army and he said he had keen an absentee since Saturday. He further said that if be had had any money he would have cleared out of the town. Witness took him into custody and at the Police Station prisoner again admitted being an absentee. Prisoner was handed over to the malting escort, and the officer recommended for the 'usual reward.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. John Fa irclough (chair- . man), C. White, A. J. Jolley, .J. Rymer-Young and Dr. Naden. HEAVY PENALTIES FOR COAL STEA LlNG.—Kate Davies, 21, Bank-street; Mary Ann Burke, 16, Dolman's- yard, Mary Alice Ares, Brown's-yard, Mary Preston, -3, Bronn's-yard ; and Mary Ellen Burke (11), daughter of Mary Ann Burke, and Catherine Ward (13), of 10, Dolman's yard, were charged with stealing from a siding at Arpley a quantity of coal valued at 35., the property of the London and North-Western Railnay.—All •defendants pleaded not guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that about 11 o'clock the previous day a Mr. Richardson working at the St. Helens Cable Company -saw the four elder defendants coming along with their -sacks flunk across their backs. Having been informed about the coat inued coal stealing from trucks he thought they had become by it improperly, and detained them and sent for the police.—Sergeant Meacock, on arriving en the scene, questioned the defendants as to where they had obtained the coal. Two of them said they had foiled it" planted" under some grass, and the other two said they bad found it covered by an old cloak. ,"The officer took the defendant to the spot and could find no trace of any coal there and on examining the trucks in the siding close by they found signs of coal having been recently broken. The coal the defendant's had, very .closely resembled that in the trucks. On proceeding from the police station, Sergeant Meacock saw the girls, who en seeing the officer, they immediately ran</p>
        <p>away. They were however caught, and on being questioned, they said they bad found the coal. There w..s no doubt that the coal had been taken from the railway. Detective Clarke, who was in company with Detective Meacock spoke to arresting the defendants.— Joseph Edgar. Richardson living at 20 Warburton-street, said he had heard that the coal had been missed from the waggons in the aiding. He saw two women (two of the defendants) coming from the dilection of the siding with bags on their back. Five minutes afterwards he saw two more in the fields close by. He detained the four and rent for the police.—Detective Serg(ant Meacock gave evidence of arrest. —Detective Clarke. in answer to the chairman said they (the police) were constantly 'sending people away from the siding.— The Chief Constable stated that during the past two weeks there had been no less than 3 tons of coal taken from a waggon in that particular riding. Continuing he said the nilway company had no desire to press the matter but they did want to put a stop to it—lt was not the coal stealing they thought of I ut the con- siderable danger run by those people on the metals. The railwaycompany had had to draft a special detective down in order to obviate the continuance of the pract ice.— The Chairman said the magistrates were determined to put a stop to it.—Davies: "It is never too late to mend.'—Davies was ordered to pay 20s. and costs or 1 mouth's imprisonment and the other thre) 10s. and costs or 14 days. The two children were discharged With a caution.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Smethurst (chairman), Alderman J. Evans, and Mess:s. A. J. Jolley, H. Thornton, C. Jackson Holmes, and J. Hepherd. NEIGHBOURS' "TIFFS."—Mary Burros, of Erwooci- street, was summoned by Bridget Donlan, of Portland- street, for assaulting and beating her on July 31st.—The complainant stated that on Saturday last the defendant rushia into her house and dragged her into the :card while she had a child in her arms. During the scuffle defendant bit complainant's fingers very badly.— Evidence having been given in support of complainant's case, defendant said she was standing at her door when Mrs. Donlan passed by. Afterwards she heard screams, and on going out found Mr. and Mrs. Donlan fighting in their backyard. On seeing defendant Mr. Donlan took a running kick at her, and was drawing back to give another when a man came up and started fighting him. 41rs. Donlan then got hold of her hair, and she (defend- ant) had to strike her in self-defence.--Defendant was fined 10s.</p>
        <p>• ...i - rf----i-ir- t . , - -, _ - ilt@ii 1,- in e :,.. , g _ , :,.._ ... ~... .... ,_ ;-:::: , : •,u IziTs .....,. .., ....... • .2 :.' - ' ---._ ",.....0.7..•',4r-j.:'.1.;:•,..7t;7••••;..k..,,,,- , . _ _ •• 4:. - ' .... . ett:-..•.--:- •'" 's'3-t_-,e. -- -es , '-'77.....= = • e-;." Z</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Warrington Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Warrington Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mssrs. F. A. Frust (chairman), C. White, W. P. /Wands and H. Roberts. ASLEEP IN CH ARGE.—Frederick Cooper, a Wa rrington man employed by Messrs. Buckley's, contractors, &amp;c., was charged with heivg asleep while in charge of two horses and a frirniture van in Liverpool-road on the 16th of last month. He was also charged with not haying a lighted lamp attached to the van. He pleaded guilty.—The Chairman in fining defendant 10s. said it was a very serious offence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Bevisey-road Wesley Guild.</head>
        <p>Bevisey-road Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>August bank holiday Monday presented a good opportunity for the Bewsey-road Wesley Guild to enjoy an Outing. The weather was dull when 3-1 mem- bers and fiiends of the Guild left Bank Quay Station at 1 o'clock for Frodsham, but fortunately the rain kept off. Arriving at Frodaham a few minutes later the party sat off to walkalongside the hills to Elidsby; which was reached just before 4 o'clock. A good tea was obtained at a farm on top of the hills, and after- wards rations games were indulged in. As usual one of the party had a camera and a photograph of the happy guilders was taken. Before' leaving at S o'clock fur _guilders the members had a walk along the top of the hilts, the weather being very pleasant at the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a moderate attendance at this meeting on Sunday last, presided over by the Secretary, who returned thanks on behalf of the friends of the late Mr. Crcsskill, also flora Mr. Gresty for the vote of sympathy sent to him in his accident. Mr. W. Cairns gave the address upon " God's displeasure upon the world and its rine." He referred to the Divine com- mand to Noah to build an ark for the salvation of all who would be sived from the great flood, and raid that Christ was our great ark of safety. In Him alone was Salvation proclaimed, freely and fully. Mr. D. McEachern recited " A dream in the Covenantors days" which was greatly appreciated. Mr. Joseph Owen and Mr. O. Smi!ey also took part in the meeting. Mr. E. Iddon was at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Rev. H. Dunnleo Denounces the Czar.</head>
        <p>Rev. H. Dunnleo Denounces the Czar.</p>
        <p>Preaching in the Kensington Chapel to a large con- gregation on Sunday evening. the Rev. Herbert Dunnico made reference to the visit of the Czar to English waters. This country with its high traditions must never forget that it owed a duty to civilisation, to humanity, and to its own self-respect, and the Czar must know that his visit to these shores, if tolerated for diplomatic reasons, was repugnant to the feelings of the Brit sh nation. _A more weak Or illogical defence than that put before the House of Commons by Sir Edward Grey it bad never been his lot to read since Mr. Balfour posed as a temperance reformer and forced his licensing bill upon the country. From a diplomatic point of view the action of the Government was had: from an ethical roint of view wicked. The good name of England was being besmirched by an alliance with one whose bands were strained with the blood of people whose interests he bad solemnly pledged himself to protect. While they must never forget that there was a strong link binding the-peoples of all countries together, he would not venture to suggest active intervention. He strongly protested against our influence arid finance being placed at his disposal. The receet deputation, comprising members of the Russian DUllla which hal recently visited our city, was no more representative of the Ru,sian people than the House of Lords was representa- tive of the English people. The Czar's name was a symbol of murder and tyranny, and as such he was to be noted by a liberty loving nation like oars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKS ASSESSMENTS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS ASSESSMENTS.</p>
        <p>More Appeals.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Vt'arr:ngten Union Assessment Committee was held at the Workhouse yesterday, Mr. .1. Billiuge presiding. There were also present :—Messrs A.Brooks,G.Mather,W. Houghton, W. Gill, J. Evans, A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw, A BOttomley (elerk),R.R. Meaeoek(ialuer),W.H. Woodeock (assistant overseer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>HENRY KENYON AND CO.</head>
        <p>HENRY KENYON AND CO.</p>
        <p>Alderman Tinnion appealed on behalf of Messrs. Henry Kenyon and Co., chemical works, Arpley, against the assessment of £2OO gross £166 rateable. He con- tended that the assessment was excessive. The plant at a chemical works, as they knew, had to be continually renewed and if taken out, it was mostly scrap. The buildings of a chemical works were not such as they would spend a great deal of money upon. There was no wharf and only one means of access to the works, that road -being hemmed in on e.:ch side so that they could not extend the works. The land, in his opinion, was not worth more than .£5OO. He contended that the gross assessment should not be more than £l3O to £l4O. The old assessment was £1? net and 00 gross. In answer to the Chairman, Alderman Tinnion said the works were built in 1861.</p>
        <p>_ The Committee adjourned the case for conference between the valuer and cleik and the firm.</p>
        <p>LONGFORD WIRE CO., LTD,</p>
        <p>Mr. O. Lofth-o-ns-e-ippealed on behalf of the Longford Wire Co., Ltd., against the assessn egt of £2,140 cross £1,390 rateable. The original assessment was £1,071 gross, £846 rateable. He stated that a considerable part of the buildings was not in use at the present time, Dr if it was in use it was only partially so. The assessment had been raised at a very inopportune time, as trade had been bad of late. He suggested a reduction to £I,ICO rateable. The case was adjourned in order that the valuer might make another inspection of the works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WOMEN AS HARVESTERS. _ _</head>
        <p>WOMEN AS HARVESTERS. _ _</p>
        <p>America is having a bumper wheat 11-;r—v-est and the ordinary supply of labour has proved utterly insuffi- cient. The farmers have therefore gone to the town and offered as much AR 13s. a chiy with board, lodging, and washing. This offer attracted a number of college students, but the additional supply was not adequate. Finally the farmers offered 12. a day to women. The result has been that young women, school teachers, stenographers, college girls,-and women who have been working in factories at 255. per week, are nuw earning 12/. a day in the field, and farmers uty they work as well as men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>HOW TO CLEAR SCUM OFF A POND.</head>
        <p>HOW TO CLEAR SCUM OFF A POND.</p>
        <p>This can easily be killed by sp.aying the surface of the water with a solution 0., sulphate of copper (blue stone). The spores of the scum, or Blanket-weed, are destroyed, and the slime melts away. An American recipe gives one pound of sulphate of copper to one million gallons of water. One estimates the number of gallons of water hip pond by multiplying together the average length, breadth, and depth of the pond. The product gives the number of cubic feet of water, and as there are about Hi gallons of water in a cubic foot, the contents of the pond are approximately ascertained. —" Gerden'ng Illustrated,"</p>
        <p>Drawn by E. W. Kimble.</p>
        <p>called bacon, and the fat salted becomes lard. and the blood mixt with other things are called blackpudding. The pig makes a very quir noise when being fed. It is born in a place called a sty, and as soon as it is born it darts for the coal and soon is eating it. When dead the skin is cut for kettle drums for boys' brigades." . .</p>
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>The veterinary surgeon will be pleased to learn that 'when a horse is not well you should put liquid on them that makes a lot of blisters, and they are then strengthened about the legs." "Carriage horses are slender because a clumsy horse would not match a handsome carriage." "The horse is not a fierce animal, but if you get a kick from one you are not likely to' forget it." Sound logic. The animals's end is here recorded: "When the horse is dead the knacker comes and takes it away."</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>The housewife will be glad to hear that " Carterbury lamb is found on the Canter- bury Plains." The same writer, alluding to the cow, says "its skin is made into liver to make our boots with. Its tongue is sticky, and it can make a very loud noise with its horns." The goat appears to be "hoary and romes about the forest. It has two kinds of names, nane,' and 'billy." Its ears are short, and it is something like a cow because it gives milk."</p>
        <p>"REVISION." "Cross out some more, Aldrich, old pal, the boss is too busy to notice it !"</p>
        <p>The above clever cartoon from a well-known. American paper illustrates the peculiarities of the American Tariff revision. President Taft, as well as the other Republican Leaders, promised a revision downwards, and on the basis of this promise received the support of the American people. The tariff as it has come from the Senate lead by Senator Aldrich is a- revision upwards almost all along the line. The position confirms what Free Traders have again and again pointed out, that while it is very easy to put on a tariff, the interests created by it are too powerful and can frustrate the will of the people when they call for a reduction. The last Canadian Liberal Government was returned on promises made to affect a downward revision when they got into power. The interests were too strong and the result was an upward revision, and it looks as if this experi- ence is to be repeated in the case of their Southern neighbour. The controversy which has centred about the Tariff has been responsible for a deal of plain speaking. In its way, however, nothing; has been more direct and significant than the attitude of the Wall Street Journal, a paper which stands alone in the character of its cir- culation among the richest men in the United States. Writing of the Tariff this organ of the great centre of American finance observes :</p>
        <p>We have only to glance at the tariff discussion in the Senate to see hlw far we have fallen back, after the moral upheaval of a few years ago. The spec- tacle there is so base, so utterly without patriotism or principle, so far from the ideals of .either party, so crudely self-seeking, and so callously ready to sacrifice the interest of an entire nation for the benefit of a small part of it, the interest of a State fora small influential portion of that State, and even the interest of a Congressional district for a few contributors to a campaign fund, that it points to the establishment of a new tyranny: This moral rot in our commercial sys- tem, si indecently displayed in the United States Senate, is one of a number of signs which should cause us to think. Theqspectaele at present afforded by the United States Senate is an insult to every sell-respecting American. It exhibits statesmen supposed to be acting in a national capacity owned in soul and pocket-book by petty local interests, while one corporation after another shows that it can snap its fingers at the promises made by the Republican President and all his party before election, and accepted by the American people. That is vigorous plain speaking, rendered the more noteworthy by its source. It may serve to bring home to this country what is really. involved in" the making of a scientific tariff."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LANY.</head>
        <p>LANY.</p>
        <p>Anent our reference a few weeks ago to a teapot preserved at Lymm and said to have been the gift of John Wesley, Joseph G. Wright Esq., of Bournemouth, writes an interesting letter on the subject of" Wesley" crockery. Mr. Wright has made a close study of Methodist crockery and busts and may be taken as an authority on the subject. He says, "Theconclusions in my own mind are wholly against the tales of Wesley's present- ations of teapots. I do not believe any of those now met with and called 'Wesley teapots' were made in his life time. He was himself much opposed to the use of tea abandoning its use after August 1746 and repeatedly advising his preachers in later years not to take it. His views on this subject were very strong, and it is scarcely probable that he would encourage the use of it by making presents of teapots. I believe that all these older specimens of pots, jugs, cups, and such like were manufactured chiefly in the Potteries from, say, 1792 to 1810, and not earlier. The 1839 Centenary was a time in which many memorials in pottery and metal were pro- duced and not a few of those now usually met with were of that date." ** * *</p>
        <p>Those who examine children essays find their labours considerably lightened by gleams of beautifully unconscious humour: Among the -departments of the Royal Agricultural Show held a week or two ago at Southport was a children's essay campetition which has yielded an unusually good crop of "howlers ." Notwithstanding the advance in education children still have the most fantastic notions of animals. One little fellow, who has evidently suffered from an overdose of education, gravely states that "the horse belongs to the family of Equidee, a branch of quadru- peds distinguished by a single digit." A little girl in standard three imparts some useful information to milkmaids by her warning that " if milkmaids pull the cow's wrong paps the cow will hit them with its tail." Another competitor in the same standard starts by saying that he is going to tell you all he knows about the cow. "It is. kept," he points out, "in a shipping, and the man who kills it is called a butcher. You get mutton chops from the cow, and its teeth are put into the museum." The chewing of the cud has fired the imagination of many of the writers, and one brave competitor endeavours to describe the process by point- ing out that " the cow needs grass." Pre- suming that the beast has got the grass, he continues, "After a time, if you notish, it (he does not say whether he means the grass or the cow) will lie down and bring the grass into its mouth. This is called cewing cud." Con- sidering the process satisfactorily explained he goes on to say that' if you tese a cow you will get hurt. It will tose you in the air." The youngest had evidently stopped at a farm for a short time for he pathetically recounts how "one night there was a calf, and its mother had no more milk, and it (the calf) just opened its eyes and fell dead. In the morning we found it stone dead. It is wrong to tese cows, as they chace you." With a New Year-like resolution, he says that chasing the cows is his one fault, and that he is "going to try to give that up."</p>
        <p>"You can buy a donkey," another com- petitor states, "cheaper in Ireland than in England." This savours of another insult to the distressful country. The rabbit has been rather scantily treated.but one boy says that "it will leep to you when you open the door," and concludes that the rabbit has a short tail, and " all weas as a beet of white on it." . .</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>The pig has come in for many unkind remarks, the-unanimous opinion being that it is a dirty animal. Other facts concerning it are that "its flesh when it is salted is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Long School Holidays.—At the Leeds Health Congress. some praise was bestowed on the child nurses who are so much in evidence in the streets ,and parks now that the schools are closed. When a girl gets to the age of fifteen or sixteen,she begins to have interests of her own, but up to then she is commonly turned into a small nursemaid, to look after her little brothers and sisters. The boys have a better time with their games, and sugar boxes on wheels, and for the most part they escape having the cares and responsibilities of the charge of young children thrust upon them. Much is done by philanthropic effort to give poor chil- dren in the towns a brief summer holiday, but mostly they are at a loss to know what to do with themselves during the school vacation, which is generally regarded as being unnecessarily long. Sensible Garb for Rough Weather.—Many women nowada3s prefer the waterproofed tweed coat and skirt to the macintosh, or rain coat, and no doubt this notion makes for greater smartness in bad weather. All the light-weight tweeds—both plain and checked—may now be had specially prepared, so as to render them impervious to lame, and not the least difference can be discovered in their appearance. Tailors are building hats of fabric to match, quite simple creations, with a quill thrust through the ribbon band, and so garbed, tho open-air girl or woman has little to dread. Tailors and the Dressmakers.—lndifferent dress- makers have been passing through a troublesome time, the plain simple modes of the past few months having spelt torture to them. Frills and fussiness cover a multitude of faults, but the severe type of gown has to be perfect, or else it is a decided—a very decided failure. All but the greatest experts therefore will welcome the fuller and trimmed gown, into which no doubt soon, as much pains will be taken to cram yards, as has recently been t ken to exclude inches. Linen Tailor-Mades.—All the newest linen goats are being relished with a satin collar, black usually, but as this means the thoroughly experienced cleaner, instead of the less expensive laundress, or the very much less expensive tub, the idea is net for the girl or woman of limited pin money. Another noticeable point, with regard to these coats is, that they now fasten just below the waist, instead of across the bust, and yet another, that they are longer at the back than in the frout. Another Revival.—The fine jersey has appeared again to be worn over short pleated skirts. The girl or woman possessed of a really good figure, and minus the means to consult a good tailor, will rejoice at this revival for nothing shows off to greater advantage a perfect figure than the fine jersey. These particular garments may be bed just now in all sizes and colours. and at considerably lower prices than were asked formerly. The " Aprcn" Tabber.—lt is to be questioned whether the modish "apron " tahber is really an irup;ovement ta the up-to-date gown. This tubber closely resembles the old-world apron which our grandmothers donned upon the smallest provocation, having previously emb:oidered it in "crewels." But the tabber is more desirable of course, being very much more elaborately and artistically embroidered. Moreover, instead of being a detach dale feature, it is built of the same fabric as the gown. The Women's Exhibition at. Olympia.—Noreity in various forms is promised for the Exhibition of Women of All Nations, which is to be open during the month of September at Olympia in London, and will after- wards be transferred Co Manchester, Glasgow, and other places. The fashion and dress section is one that will naturally appeal to every woman visitor, and there will be daily demonstrations in all the arts, crafts, and industries, as are associated with women's work through- out the world, whilst in addition this section will deal exhaustively with every branch of dressmaking and millinery, with hairdressing rnd hygienic aids to health.</p>
        <p>Tee Domestic Economy section will include lectures and demonstratlcni on cooking and how to " feed the brute." That, we are told, is how to make him an angel, and baby, if not always an angel, is often king of the home, and is to have a section all to himself. Ills will be the greatest show of babies on record, and altogether nearly one thousand of all colours and nationalities are to be included in the exhibition,whilst the National Society of Day Nurseries is to arrange a cr6che which will be found in full working order daily. Divorce Facilities.—There is, broadly speaking, a wide difference of opinion between the Bishops and the Magistrates with respect to the proposed extension of divorce facilities to. the County Courts. The Bishops regard with anxiety the extension of these facilities to other classes, than those who already take advantage of them, they being exclusively the classes who can afford to pay for this relief. But the Magistrates view is that this suggested reform is the lesser of two evils, and one might safely say that they know from their daily experi- ence in the Courts, a good deal more about this subject than the 'Bishops do. It is a mockery of justice to extend the benefits of divorce to the well-to-do, and withhold them from the poor, for no other reason than that they are poor. In the latter case the womao tied to a drunken and dissolute husband, can get a separAtion order. That is all the relief she can afford, and teing rnarrrei and yet unmarried, the law leaves dangers in her path which she may escape if she had the oppor- tunity of obtaining the full benefits of divorce. Grace Widows.—A lady giving evidence it the law courts the other day, resented being called a grass widow, but it is doubtful whether she was right in regarding this as a mete vulgar expression. There was a time when divorces were granted only by authority of the Church, and a wife thus empowered to separate from her husband was commonly called a grace widow. If she re-married she was in occasional instances so described in the register, and it is probable that the term grass widow is only a corruption of grace widow. Wife's Share of ,the Family Income.—Social reform often means nothing more than the same thing under ft new name, and probably many of the sanguine expecta- tions of the results of the promised reform of the English Poor Law system, are doomed to disappoint- -meta. But there are practical ways of diminishing the causes which bring working-class people to the Work- house, and one of them would be to give the wife legal claim to a fair share of the family income and a similar right to any savings she puts by. In these matters we are behind many of the progressive nations, and even in Turkey, the wife has a legal claim to a share of her husband's resources. In Germany, if a wan is able to support his fasnily, and is proved to be dissipating his wages in vicious habits, he can he declared a minor, and a guardian appointed by the magistrate to whom his employer must pay hie a ages. In this country the man who could not obtain pal meut of his wages diverted in this way, to the support of his wife and family, would doubtless refuse to work, but in Germany the police would see aat he either did his work or suffered the consequences. It would of course be said what about the drunken wife? But safeguards would be easy to effect, and where there is one had wife there are to be found twenty drunken and dissolute bust ands.</p>
        <p>A MODISTIC SURPRISE.—Time was when a light airy fairy blouse intended for wear upon a winter afternoon would have startled us all, but nowadays we are thoroughly alcustomed to making no difference in after- noon blouses all the year round. We are also used to summer hats—worn at afternoon functions—in Decem- ber and no doubt we, most of us, flatter ourselves that any surprise in the matter of mixing up the seasons from a modiste point of view is not possible. How- ever those who patronise first class milliners, and were shown at their establishments felt bats in July, may have changed their opinion, for the felts are new ones, and not last winters goods posing as those of the winter to come. The majority of these felts show a narrow velvet band, and huge ckou of the same at the side, ani are worn with every variety of woollen tailor-made costumes. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ALLEGED ILLEGAL DISTRESS.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED ILLEGAL DISTRESS.</p>
        <p>County Court Action at Warrington. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before Deputy Judge A. Hyslop-Maxwell, Flora Ruben, wife of Noah Ruben picture framer, &amp;c., of Liverpool and formerly of 78,1xnutsford-road, Warrington, brought an action against Mrs. Green, Kuutsford-road,'and John Newton Vernon, estate agent, Mill-street, to recover damages for alleged illegal, wrongful and excessive distress. Mr. W. Madden, barrister (instructed by Mr. R. Barrow-Sicree, of Liverpool) appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. Greaves Lord, barrister (instructed by Messrs. Forshaw and Forshaw, Warrington) for the defendants.</p>
        <p>Plaintiff's case was that in consequence of litietion her husband, who had carried en the business of picture framer and glazier in Knutsford-road, h4carne bankrupt in 1906. His brother bought the business from the official receiver and employed plaintiff's husband at a wage of 255. per week. hater the brother went to America and plaintiff bought the busines. At that time the agent for the landlord was Mr. Joseph Titchett and the rent was paid to him. The rent was not paid regularly because there was a contra account. Some time in Februtry plaintiff intended to go to Germany and her husband stored some furniture. On the Monday before Good Friday—the time of the Jewish holidays—Mr. awl Mrs. Ruben went to Liver- pool to take part in the Feast of the Passover and the premises were left secured. A notice was placed in the shop u ind iw to the effect that business would be resumed on the following Thursday. On his return Mr. Ruben found that an entrance had been forced and a bailiff was in possession. It appeared that the house had been purchased by Mrs. Green and the bailiff was in for rent. Subsequently a sale took place, but it was not advertised in a proper way, the defendants seizing an engraving, worth about ss. which was in the shop, anl</p>
        <p>inicribing upon it. in thick black letters "Sale 00 Thursday, 11 o'clock." There were goods in the house to the value of £5O but they were disposed of at ridiculous prices. Mr. Madden contended that the premises had been violently broken into, that defendants had not been given any notice of the distress, and that no inventor, was gi% en of the sale. The proceedings were Napoleonic and harsh all through, and there was n° attempt to treat the plaintiff with decency and courtesP Nosh Ruben, plaintiff's husband, stated that the rent due was £4 10s., and that that sum was in the cupboard waiting for the agent to call for it. A parlour suite which cost fif.een guineas was s.ild for £2 19s. In an! wer to Mr. Lord, witness denied thst be removed some of the furniture at ten o'clock at night • about ten days before Good Friday. He was aware that the house was going to change hands, as a sale 11 auction was annourced,hut he did not know that Mrs' Green had bought it until the distress was levied. Ile did not tell Mts. Titchett that he was going Germany for an indefinite time. Mr. Green did no' ' call at the house for the rent a numl...er of times. A Flora Ruben, the plaintiff, gave similar evidence ail; raid no one Lad applied to her for the rent. She (110 not receive any notice of distress. The hearing had not been completed when the Court rore. The case was provisionally fixed for Sept 210,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL SPORTS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL SPORTS.</p>
        <p>• A Successful Gathering.- rk clouds overhung the Warrington Football en Monday last, yet not a drop of rain was felt by uw 4,000 people who witnessed the 24th annual Warringtol Football Club Sports. There was a record eatry.aeci some very been competition amongst the venous athletes for the handsome prizes offered by the Cluba Thase were valued at 1:80. The Borough Brass 133'' was in attendance during the day and rendered a Pro" gramme of popular music in spirited style. Alderrase Tinnion, the president Of the Club acted as one of the judges. At the close of the contests his clang° tindly distributed the prizes. The officials were :—Clerk of the course, Mr. Davies; starter, Mr. C. Bryan, Present; judges, Mesgre', D. Tinnion, J. &amp;Iles, T. Bowers, J. E. Warren, Rua I. Locker ; committee, Mess.i. W. Hewitt, F. T. Bark, T. Barnes, jun., T. Bunch, E. Osborne, W. Chalmers, w' Nevins, J. Duttm, I. Hacke:t, F. Tome-, E. Gilbert, J. W. Evans, A. Jones, J. Winstanley, F. Heeson, Hem y Hope •, handicapper, Mr. H. N. Ellis; treasurer, Mr. T. Pemberten ; hon. secretary, Mr. .".." Ashton; official representatives N.C.1.7.. C. Piclinvanee" Warrington: H. Booth, Warrington, Mr. J. E. Warren a'so officiated as time-keeper. SCHOOL Boys RACE.—The first race was for school bOYe; and 28 availed themselves of the opportunity affordel them in the 150 yards handicap. There were five heats se' the winners of the heats were: R. H. Watsan, 26 (Cairo; street), W. Brady. 27 ‘(St. Mary's) •, S. Catterall, (Silver-street) ; F. Waywell, 9 (Lleathside) and w' Keen, 31 (Naticnal). It was rather strange that the winners of heats were the youngest of the runnere' After a fine race, which was won in 18 4.5 seconds, the result was as follows : 1, F. Waywell ; 2, %V. Keen, 3, S. Cntterall and 4, W. Brady. 100 YARDS OPEN.—Tho sprint of 100 yards was ng much looked forward to by the competitors SS by If spectators.- No leas than 59 entered in this ennipet non and nearly all of them turned our. The &amp;stair: tenth heats were the quickest, the length being coverea in 9 4-5 seconds, whilst the final wes also done in Of same time. The winners of the bests were ; I,IS' Pestles 8:1 (Winnington Park) ; 2, A. Siddall OW' 2 rington A.C); 3, J. Lea 101- (Prescot A.C,) ; 4, F • Deakin 61 (Sefton H.) ; 5, P. E. Lear 9 (Pendleton) ; 6,3. Leather 81 (Wart ingt on A.F.C.) ; 7, J. Lambert 10 (Broughton H.) ; 8, A. Wilcock 9 (Warrington 11-1{) 9, W. E. Jackson 6 (Salford H.) and 10, C. W. Flawking 81 (Chester H.) The second round was won by F. Lear, W. Pestles, J. Lambert and J. Leather. The'''. won in a good race, the 100 yards being covered in felt seconds. The result of the final was as follows " F. E. Lear • 2, J. Lambert; 3, W. Postles—thre° strangers. Time 9 4-5 seconds. J. ONE MILE Cm:E.—The one mile bicycle novice hanov cap proved a great attraction,but there was a great d lifer enoe in the times, the quickest heat being 3re:n. 2st, for the novices and for the ordinary handicap 2-40 However, on the whole it was good riding and tho ~ ‘,4 winners of the four heats were T. Hatton, 30 (Y`-'I11".• A.C.) ; W. J. Lake, 30 (Warr:ngton) • S. L. Lee, ag (Lamberhead Gre,n A.C.); and A. Booth, 25 (Runwall C.C.). R. Glover. 35 (Widnes), was alsa included in the final Owing to being second in the fastest heat, viz: The fiaal took 3min. 13sec., thee() successful being /, Glover; 2, T. Hatton; 3, W. J. Lake. The race peculiarly interesting owing to the fact that hall wheel only separated the successful men. 880 YARDS HANDICAP.—Thirty-nine Out of 451 entrants turned out in the HO yards handicap. Severe competitors dropped off after going two rounds, as tbei race was rather speedy. It only took ltnin. 555i,c,, there was only a few yards difference between thei winners. The results were 1, W.J. Wildman 64 (Sslfe.cu A.C.) ; 2, G. %V. Hunt. 67 (Crosfield's %CO ; 3 S--P"' Hayward, 46. (Chester H.).) 220 YARDS HANDICAP.—The 220 yards handicen a favourite one. No less than 60 entered for this eveo,;... There were eight heats, and the winners were Thompson, 16 yards (Farnworth) ; P. 3. Deakin, (Sefton II.) ; J. Lambert, 18 (Broughton IL) ; Cooke, 151 (Broughton H.) ; F. E. "Lear' 16 (pendie; ton) ; A. Siddall, 16 (Werrington A.O ),"W. Pestles, 11 (Wituirev.ton Park), and J. Casey. 12 (Liverpool 11. A.C.). The second heat was the speediest, the 220 yar",7 being accomplished in 22 3-ssecs. The final tool'. 22 2-ssecs , and the result was :-1, T. Thompson ; 2, .1. Lambert, and 3, F. E. Lear. •</p>
        <p>, _ MILE CYCLE.—The one-mile bicyc!e handicap PO" vided some excellent sport for the onlookers. 'NM; one enteted and there were five heats. The winners (‘),I. the heats were:—.W. Hunt, 190 (Runwell C.C.) ;10'a Clayton, 145 (Pembroke C.C.) ; T. P. Ellison, I°4' (Pembroke FL): W. J. Lake, 180 (Warrington) and "• G. Clare, 175 (Leigh A.C). Considerable disappoint: ment was created when C. 13:rra (0/' after pulling up splendid retired when only 2 laps t° go. The final only lasted 2-11 1-ssec,., and the winners were :-1, T. P. Ellison; 2, J. Hands; 3, W. ClaYwu. Only half a wheel separated the winners. 600 YARDS OnsTacLE RACE.—As usual the 600 Ynr(ll obstacle race was very amusing. Thirty entered there were three heats. Instead of hay.ng to run walk—in sacks this year, the competitors had to finish up with a bucket on their beads. The winners of the heats were Harrison, 'P. Graham, T. Connor, Ji Oakes, I. Stewart and W. Litherland. The final resulte, as follows :-1, L Conner; 2, J. Stewart ; 3, Harrisoar. The race was run at a fast pace, and at finish Harrison had to he attended to, for he NO' " played out." Two MILES WALK.—The presence of A. .- r - Rowland, the New Zealand champion in the two raileS walking handicap was very popular. Twelve copretiters strafe-1. E. E. Jones. of Richmond 11., 210 yards Wl9 disqualified after going three laps for alleged trotting' tie would not retire and came in second, but no Pri4e was awarded him. Rovrland's 50, and NV. G. Yates, 65: (Salford /1.) were well matched. Yates kept in roil': for a lap or two then Rowlands reversed it and so went on right op to the last lap when Rowlands M a lead of a few yards and kept it. J. T. Mullhall, (Warrington) walked well—and properly—and Pine first prize, KO yards sonarating him and Rorilands *OO got the second prize. Yates a well deserved third. , 410 YARDS HANDICAP.—There were 36 who enterea for the 440 yards Irndicap, and they were divided int; four beats Those successful in the heats were Davies, 31 (Preston H.); J. Hands,33 (Crosfield's F. Fitzsimmons, 34 (Salford 11.) ; J. 0. Twist, (Liverpool H. and A.C.) : E. Ince, 28 (Sefton II.); Burgess, 2,3 (Davenham F.C.) ; H. J. Lyon, 36 (Raveil" head A.C.) ; and L. Stub!" 30 (Salford H.) Tho - fire' heat was the longest and took 51 1-ssecs., whilst 01 last one vas the shortest and took 50secs. The fin,ae. was run in fine time only taking 49 2-5 seconds. T"ci result was as follows :-1, E. Inca; 2, F. Davies; au 3, L. Stahlts. Two MILES CYCLE.—Of the 25 who entered ill tl3' two miles bicycle htindicap, W. Almond, 11t) (PreserlL A.G.), T. R. Whitfield, 200 (Sutton II.), C. Berry, (Warrington A.C.), W. Hunt, 295 (Runwell C.C.), T. Ellison, 215 (Pembroke H.), and H. Wood, 30 (Chester), won the heats. The firet heat was accomplished. i- Grain. !sec.: the Sccond, Omin. 29sec.; and the Ultra, Gm in. 24 2-ssec. The final was somewhat of a surprise. The race lasted 6mio. 10sec. After two laps the cyclists were together, and on the last lap it was almost a cell; tainty that Berry would win the first prize, but it wit' not to be; for W. Almond coming round the curvEt: passed Berry who could not recover his position, lin.' bad to be content with second, missing the first onl7 I)" a length. T. R. Whitfield followed a few yards behind. ONE MILE FIANDICAP.—The one mile handicap dral 39 entreat's. It will be sem that it was very g 00“.., running by the time it took to cover the gronnd, 4min. 20 3-ssecs. W. A. Pritchard, 78 (Chester it•i° carried off first prize. being 100 yards ahead of Holbrook. 92 (Warrington A.C.), G. NV. Hunt, 11° (Crosfleld's A.C.) gained a good third. ad Afterwards a large crowd gathered around the gat-- stand, where Miss TINNION distributed the prizes. .;„9 Mr. H. ASTITtiN moved a vote of thanks to Tinnion for her services. He said that although tu- competitors always tried to do their best en occasions like those, there were times when circumstances wertt; against them. Had it not been for the very tirsettle _ weather they had been having lately he felt Euro theY would have bad a larger attendance. (Applause). 0 Alderman TINNION, replying, said be hoped the% present would rally round the Warrington Foetal Club, fhey had tried to get a good programme, they had endeavoured to carry it out in a taw spur manlike manner. The racer that afternoon had beet° well and truly run. He thanked them very much behalf of his daughter.</p>
      </div>
