      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>TSAR.</head>
        <p>THE TSAR.</p>
        <p>ni:The Tsar arrived in the Solent on Monday v—_,Lernoon. He was met off Spithead by King r°lvard, who had sailed from Cowes in the Yal Yacht Victoria and Albert, Extra. ordinary police precautions were taken to tuard the Tsar during his visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TSARITSA.</head>
        <p>THE TSARITSA.</p>
        <p>The Tsaritsa, who is with her Royal husband on board the Standart, is said to have been much gratified and impressed by the cordial welcc me they received, and the words of appreciation sent out by the Tsar were 'no doubt thoroughly 'encloysed by Her Royal Highness.</p>
        <p>4....ftramy • . • • . ••••.; •C•"'"V ; • . ••••• • ..• • , ;' • tr. lz • • $ . „.... • . • . - • - : - :05t1:" • • , ik • •" . • k</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SQUARE OF PEACE AT BARCELONA.</head>
        <p>THE SQUARE OF PEACE AT BARCELONA.</p>
        <p>Barcelona is the centre of Spain's Civil War. Much damage has been done to the town during the riots, the anti-war mob having burned the beautiful churches, cut telegraph lines, wrecked the tramways, and fought with the soldiers and the civic guard. The Government has almost stamped out the disorders in the town itself, but the disturbq ances still continue in the province of Catalonia. Our illustration shows the famous Square of Peace, round which, inappropriately enough, the most desperate fighting took place. Thq statue is the famous monument to Christopher Columbus. -)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DEATH OF LORD [ILFORD'S HEIR,</head>
        <p>DEATH OF LORD [ILFORD'S HEIR,</p>
        <p>While Undergoing an Operation.</p>
        <p>Lo Athrlford's only son and heir, the Hon. Thomas tvits('rtolt Powys, died in London on Tuesday night. He %I, thirteen years of age. He was operated on for affection, and died under an anresthetic. inquest was held on Wednesday at Westminster. kiejon Lilford said that his son had good healtl, and never been placed under an antesthetic before.— tht Harold B. Gardiner, who, with a surgeon, conducted Nvitb'Peration said that the lad took' the anwsthetio biadt the slightest trouble. At the third movement Nib' 03' the operator the patient gave a slight retching ctt'ernent, which was immediately succeeded by a tlttation of breathing. The operation had gone on for sline,lY two minutes. The patient never made the Oxrtest improvement,-either respiratory or muscularly. %/gen was administered; in fact, every known method At restoring life was applied, but there was no response. lityhsthe post-mortem witness observed that the '11474 gland and spleen were double the normal 'size. R-eak `4-as the status lymphaticns, and this condition breh,ed the respiratory centres. • Clair Thompson, throat and ncse specialist, ho hat the operation wns absolutely necessary, and not think the operation had anything to do with t 111 r 04' obert Trevor, pathologist, who made the post- •el examination, said death was due to failure of I,!ration while the boy was suffering from a condition AornPhatisoi or status lymphaticus. l'erdict of" Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>NEW ST. HELENS ALDERMAN. hsAt ed meeting of the St. Helens Town Council on tille,-Ilesday, Mr. A. J. Foote was elected alderman in lee-esslon to the late Alderman Brown. Mr. Foote has 4.1.1 a member of the Council since 1889, and he is 'lriZan of the Finance Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MY SEVENTIETH BIRTHDAY.</head>
        <p>MY SEVENTIETH BIRTHDAY.</p>
        <p>There comes each year but once the day When first we saw the light, That oft in ehildhobd's happy hours Was welcomed with delight.</p>
        <p>When- men are three score years and ten Their life must soon be o'er, They cannot to the future look As they have done before.</p>
        <p>Like autumn leaves, our work is done, And we must•scou decay, • But ‘vhat has been will still remain When life has passed away. Then if beyond the shade of death The work of life we take, Maybe the dreams that follow it Are what on earth we make.</p>
        <p>Why do we struggle to the end For what the world can give? Lest besetting sins o'ercome While yet on earth we live.</p>
        <p>'Tis wiser for us to retire • When far advanced in life Than vain and empty honours Of selfishness and strife.</p>
        <p>Kind nature is for ever fair And friendship still is sweet And many things are beautiful That in old age we meet.</p>
        <p>Mord blest than conqueror's triumph, Than shout of victory Unto the aged warrior Unsullied peace must be.</p>
        <p>Ribbleton, July Hai, 1000,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FWARRINGTON CHAPTERS. WALKS AROUND WARRINGTON IN 1773,</head>
        <p>FWARRINGTON CHAPTERS. WALKS AROUND WARRINGTON IN 1773,</p>
        <p>A few weeks ago we quoted a description of Warrington given by Thomas Pennant 4 the learned Zoologist and Antiquary. Whilst the name of this cultured and-genial traveller is fresh in the memory of our readers we propose to give them some further glimpses of the surroundings of the town gleaned from his other jottings. Mr. Pennant fre- quently visited Warrington and at an inter- esting period of its history. His famous book on Zoology was printed at Market Gate at the press of William Eyres and he stayed more than once at Orford Hall in the con- genial company of his ' friend and fellow- scientist Mr. Blackburne.</p>
        <p>We imagine that his memoranda are known to few, possibly to none of our readers, and they certainly merit reprinting if only for the interest of being taken back to the period when Latchford was being rescued from the swamps and its land made so'valuablelas to fetch three pounds an acre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LA.TCHFORD AND GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>LA.TCHFORD AND GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>"During my stay at Orford I made an excursion across Warrington Bridge into Cheshire. At the foot, in a suburb called Latchford, is now building a good street and church dependent on Gropen-hall. This beginning might possibly have grown into a new town, had it not been checked by the evil times.</p>
        <p>"I passed over Gropen-hall and Latchford heaths, recently enclosed, and now made worth £3 the Cheshire acre. In the right is Gropen-hall Church, dedicated to St. Wilfrid, the rectory is the gift of Rev. Edmund Taylor, with patron and incumbent; but the advowson is upon sale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>THELWALL.</head>
        <p>THELWALL.</p>
        <p>"A little farther is the hamlet of Thelwall, noted in Saxon days for the town built by Edward the elder in 920 Cas recorded in the Saxon Chronicle 110), and fortified with a precinct made of stakes, from which it took its name; Thell signifying a stake, and Wall its present meaning. This he garrisoned, and committed to the custody of his knights, as a security to his new conquests. "Near the house is a small ruinous Chapel, which, with many others in this diocese are said never to have been consecrated; were originally only domestic, and have often fallen into disuse. In this township are very considerable powder-mills.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>"The little town of Ein-ime is a relief to the dull unvaried flat from Warrington to this place, being seated on a pretty inequality of sand-stone, and commanding a picturesque view down a dell. The living is a rectory dedicated to St. Mary, and under the patron- age of Sir Peter Warburton and Egerton Leigh, Esq.—each presents -a minister, who serve alternately, Sunday by Sunday. This division has existed ever since the days of the Conqueror, when qilbert Venablq, had one half of the town, and Osborne, son of Tezzon the other. They also divided the patronage of the church, which, served by a Presbyter, 'existed in the time of the Confessor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARBURTON.</head>
        <p>WARBURTON.</p>
        <p>"From hence I descended to a flat con- genial with the former, and reached Warbur- ton, a village and chapel. From hence I rode along the steep sandy banks of the Mersey, and passed some agreeable hours with my worthy friend John Lyon, Esq., at his neat house, Mill Bank 'near Hollins Ferry, a horse passage Into the County of Lancaster.</p>
        <p>"Near the house are two mills ; one for the manufacture of paper, the other for slitting and rolling of iron.</p>
        <p>"The /vrersey is by no means a pleasing water, running usually far beneath its banks. It takes its rise in the very extremity of Cheshire, and is as its name imports, the march or the boundary between the kingdom of ,Northumberland and Mercia.</p>
        <p>"It flows useless for navigation till it reaches the Irwal, five miles below Man- chester, when both streams receive artificial depth by the help of nine locks between that town and Warrington. They were made navigable under the powers of an Act of Parliament obtained in 1720, when it was undertaken successfully by several adven- turers. The navigation is never interrupted by droughts, as it c 9 be supplied with water between lock and lock, by flushing or letting off back water reserved for that purpose. It carries vessels of 35 to 40 tons burden • and such ig the increase of manufattures at Man- chester, there are more employed than ever. "The fish of this river are salmon, smelts, a few trout, pike, perch, bream,. chub, dace, graining, gudgeons, lampries, lamperns and eels. BANK HALL.</p>
        <p>"After crossing the river I resumed my joiirney and returned through Warrington: at the western end of the,town passed by Bank, the seat of Thomas Patten, Esq., a new house raised entirely upon copper. The foundations are the slags from the adjacent furnaces, the property of that gentleman, cast in moulds into squares for the purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SANKEY CANAL.</head>
        <p>SANKEY CANAL.</p>
        <p>"About a mile and a half fa-Ither I crossed Sankey Brook Navigation; which, from its junction with the Mersey, extends near 12 miles into the country. Originally the only water was a brook, which at spring-tides admitted small vessels about half-a-mile from the mouth to a warehouse at Sankey Bridges, which is still in use. But far the greater number of vessels pass into the river by a new canal from the bridge, a mile and a half westward to Fiddlers Ferry, the common passage over the estuary into Cheshire. "The present useful canal was formed in consequence of an Act passed in 1755, empowering certain undertakers to make the Sankey stream navigable; or, to speak with more precision, to cut a canal near that inconsiderable rivulet, and to render it fit for all the purposes of inland navigation. This is the most ancient we have in our island that runs distinct from the natural beds of rivers.</p>
        <p>"The present state of this canal is follows: It runs entirely separate from Sankey Brook except in one place, where it crosses, and for a small space mixes with it. Its length, from Fiddlers' Ferry to a place where it separates into three branches, is 91 miles, and from thence it is carried to Penny Bridge and Gerrard's Bridge, and there terminates, making the whole distance from the Mersey 11',' miles. In that course there are eight single and two double locks and the fall is about 60 feet The chief article carried on it is coal, of which in the year 1771, 45,568 tons were conveyed to Liverpool; and to Warrington and .other places 44,152 tons. There are besides slates -and corn brought down; and deal, paving and lime stones (purloined from the Welsh coast), carried up. Near the northern end of the canal is seated the important manufacture of plate glass, lately introduced into this kingdom, and which rivals that of the famous work in the Rue de St. Antoine at Paris, Let us</p>
        <p>acknowledge that we owe the success to some capital persons seduced from thence; there seems to be among all nations a recipro- cation of this species of artifice. "I am the more full in my account of this canal, as it seems as yet unnoticed, in common with the general history of this important country. Liverpool alone has as yet had its historian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BEWSEY HALL.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY HALL.</p>
        <p>"About half-a-mile north of SankeY Bridges stand the remains of Bewsey Hall, the eeat of the ancient family of the Botelers. Robert, the first who assumed the name, took it from his office of Butler to Runulp, earl of Chester in 1120. He had large possession in this country, and his descendents were great benefactors to the town of Warrington. Sir Thomas, I believe the last of the name, was, with his lady, murdered in this house, by assasins, who in the night crossed the moat in leathern boats to perpetrate this villainy. The unfortunate pair lie represented magnifi- cently in alabaster in the parish church; and the sides of the tomb are finely orna- mented with various saintly figures. The seat passed after to the Irelands, the Athertons, and lastly to the Gwilliams, who now possess the estate; the moat and part of the house still remains.</p>
        <p>"In the house is a singular picture on board, of an assemblage of the Florentine wits and poets, from Guide Cavalcanti, who died in 1300 to Marsilius Ficinus, who died in 1499. Dante is placed sitting with a book in his uplifted hand, as if reading to Caval- canti, who stands behind. Petrarch stands leaning forward, as if applying to Dante; is dressed in a white cap, in a blue dress over his shoulders, and a white vest; behind him is Boccace represented bald, all these with laureated heads. Behind him are Angelus Politianus in a blue cap, and Ficinus in a red gown—a groups of illustrious not to be paralleled in the same space in scarcely any country." .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>rsTYS'''TEIIO</head>
        <p>rsTYS'''TEIIO</p>
        <p>By Mr. Henry Whitham. (Continued from last week),</p>
        <p>We lived the boycott down, and I began to fancy myself a man. On the 24th of September 1859 I was admitted a member of the Prince of Wales Lodge, 1.0.0.F.-M.U. I went through all the chairs, and I am proud to think my name now stands on the merit board of the Lodge as a P.P.G.M., of the Order. A very few, years went by ere I came across a lady who then lived with Miss Rylands at the Summer House at the corner of Tanners-lane and Bewsey-street and who ultimately became my wife. She was the eldest daughter of Mr. R. Hewitt who kept the Mulberry Tree Inn, Stockton Heath. About this time I became the happy owner of a microscope, and became a fairly good amateur mounter of slides. Along with a dear old friend, Mr. R. Holloway, a painter, I used to spend my Saturday afternoons search- ing the country round for insects and any other material for microscopic work. My instrument ultimately landed into the hands of Mr. G. Campbell. I wonder if he is still moving around in this rough old world. I early joined the labour organization of my trade, and gradually grew to be its most prominent local official. I was always fond of reading, and to-day each room in the house has a large well-filled bookcase in it, chiefly religious polemics and industrial. I studied the labour conditions of the world; made myself as little insular as possible, This attitude of mind brought and kept me in a constant state of conflict with the local spirit of my fellow-workmen. I made enemies, and during the whole of my some- what public career had always a large number around me who suspected my honesty, and thought me more of a master's man than the workman's friend. During these years I had thrown off the yoke I then thought and think to-day is holding my class in its grip. I mean Christianity, and in saying this I don't mean the. teachings of Jesus Christ. The first decided break in this direction came about at a Sunday afternoon class held at the Wycliffe Church, Bewsey- street. The late Mr. Walter Ashton was our teacher. The good man had undertaken a big job ; no less than the explanation of the Bible from back to back. He had a young son with him, who acted as if he would much rather have been outside the church. The boy irritated me as well as the father, and when I stopped the teacher to question him as to something he was giving' us, I was told I was there to be taught and not to teach. The class broke up at once. It was in the Co-operative Hall that the Parliamentary Debating Society first held its meetings. The society had its commence- ment at about the time when Mr. Bradlaugh was fighting for the rights of the Northamp- ton constituency, and for his own rights and for the just rights of Englishmen and free- thought. On the Liberal side we had as leader the Rev. Mr. Pilcher, as nice and as intellectual a gentleman as one could meet. The Conservative I cannot just name, but it was one of two men, either a Mr. Burgess or the Rev. Mr. Carleton. I, as M.P. for Northampton, had to bring in a measure on the oaths question. Possibly, I intro- duced matter I might well have left out. In referring to Jesus Christ I am afraid I handled the question of His birth in a rough and possibly coarse manner. Mr. Burgess it was who took exception to what I said, and rising left the room calling upon all who had any respect for their character to follow him. No one saw it in that light, and no one followed him. After a little private medita- tion in the porch, the gentleman came back. In this manner was the end of the first Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society brought about. Mea Culpa ! Another incident of the Co-operative Hall occurred when I applied for ite use to give Mr. Bradlaugh an opportunity to address a Warrington audience. A meeting of the members was called, and I made the applica- tion in the. required form. I was opposed by Mr. F. Monks who, in the course of his remarks, said "it was licence not liberty we wanted." Of course the influence of the father of Co-operation in Warrington made me take a back seat, go home a sadder, but not by any means a wiser man. A deal might be said of this incident, but let it go. At one time I placarded the town With the bold but unwise words "An atheist's opinion of the Bible" and told the good people that Councillor Slater, of Bury, would address a meeting on the Fair Ground on the subject. The Mayor, Mr. Webster, did not like the posters as he saw them on his way to church, and called upon the chief of police, Mr. Hunt, and said there must be no meeting. I did' not see ft that way, nor did Mr. Slater. We went to the Fair Ground, I taking two of my young daughters with ine. We two mounted chairs and I com- menced the opening speech but the large crowd never heard the Atheist's opinion of the Bible. The Primitive Methodists were on one side of me, the Catholic contingent on the other. The former did the hymn busi- ness, and the latter, the one they understood best, rough rowdyism. Along Queen-street and Scotland-road we were followed by a yelling, hooting crowd, my girls frightened</p>
        <p>and crying. I remember one man put his face close to mine and questioned me as to my 'fear of death. The palm of my hand collided with his cheek, and I was quit of him. Many a time since then have I felt the effects of the loving kindness of my Christian friends. I and a Mr. J. Symes were with Mr. Bradlaugh and his two daughters, Alice and Hypatia in Palace yard on the day when Mr. Bradlaugh was forcibly ejected from the House of Commons, this being the occasion when he most certainly received his death blow. Someone ought to blush for shame at the events of those days. ' It was in 1886 that the strike of the Cradley Heath chain makers occurred. They struck to make it possible for a woman to earn eight shillings and sixpence, and a man thirteen-shillings for sixty hours work-. I took their, chains and with them on my shoulders, I walked the streets, visited the "pubs," and from the Public Hall platform addressed a meeting on behalf of the poor strikers who were out thirteen weeks. These poor people are not much better off to-day. In defence of their rights as human beings they ought to join the Territorials. I laid myself open to prosecution by selling heaps of the Knowlton pamphlet for which Mr. Bradlaugh and Mrs. Besant were uselessly prosecuted. I think Mr. Rylands was at that time member for Burnley. I know I supplied him with copies of the pamphlet. I may here say I was one of the committee to first invite Mrs.. Besant to the Freethought platform. During the years when Sir Charles Dilke was making himself prominent as a Republi- can I started a club in Warrington. We met in a garret near the sky and at the corner of Orford-street and Buttermarket-street. My speeches must have disturbed the good Tories. A Tory paper issued from one of the Market Gate corners suggested sending me through the door that stood under the Bridewell steps. The Sankey-street paper that has, all through my time, been, shall I say, notorious for its neutrality in all things religious and political—suggested that my barking might be worse than my bite, proving beyond a doubt that it could be sane at times.</p>
        <p>I may leave out my work as a politician and worker with the Liberal party. I early began to think I had something to say to the readers of the "Warrington Examiner " and as Clifford Dix, Felix Holt and Colin Clout I have made all the use of this paper I could I have always felt that the press would be the greatest power on earth in the working man's hands if he would only use it. The editor is always -a good friend to the poor grammarian if he is satisfied as to honesty of purpose. I may say here, I never said anything that was personally abusive of anyone over a nom-de-plume. Stirring times were now at hand, the wire trade of War- rington and of the Kingdom seemed doomed. But I must go back a few years. The Wire- Drawers' Union had an unfortunate and an unavoidable feature in its make-up. The steel-drawers who made a strong and higher wage-earning part of the Union's strength, had pretty nearly a monopoly in'its line in the world's work. There was not, and could not be the bond that is so essential to true unity and full'lnutual interest. As a matter of fact there was not anything but " self " go'verned the actions of the steel drawer when the interests of the iron wire drawer were at stake. As an official of the Union the first time I ran up against this 'snag was at Sheffield. A lary Government order had been given to a Woolwich firm. This firm gave the "drawing down" part of it to various -other firms; among them being your own Whitecross firm, and W. Cookes, of Tinsley, Sheffield. The wire trade of the Kingdom at this time was very bad, and the employers were pleased to get almost any kind of orders to keep the mills going. We in Warrington saw nothing wrong in draw- ing wire at drawing down prices. It went .out of the gates an unfinished article,and to be drawn again at Woolwich. The Sheffield district saw the matter in a different light. All that left the mill grounds _was to be paid finished price. Of course Cookes could not keep the order and pay the price. A friend of mine and a Warringtonian, G. Campbell, was fore- man of the place. A strike was declared,a,nd I was sent for. After hearing the men's story and the employers, I declared the strike was wrong both from an economical point of view, and as contrary to rules. I saw Mr. Cooke, and he offered to throw the order up, and to sign under the firm's billhead our price list. I told the men this, and they urged that Mr. Cooke must bring the list, and make his own statement, and one man stepped out on the floor and expressing the wish that I was on the floor he brandished a knife around. At this time I quietly asked the police to keep an eye on him. I determined the strike must end and if the men would not go back to work I would fill the mill with Union men. I did this, and the move caused me to call in the auctioneer, sell, up and take my wife and family to horrid, and hateful Sheffield. The mills started with all new men, and this mill was the only one in Sheffield, with possibly the Albion as an exception, at which iron wire was drawn. It was an iron wire ques- tion, and steel drawers wanted to settle it. The first Lodge night we all attended with our dues. The men had agreed not to pay if my money was refused. It was refused and we all cleared out. One by one they , accepted the men!s money. They kept me out for two years, and then gave the a clean card, sending it to me by David Halstead. During all this unhappy work my wife, slowly dying, was also a great anxiety to me.' She died on the 28th June, 1872, and lies in Grimesthorpe Churchyard. I had then three young children, the eldest of whom was born in 1861. I was married again at ' Grimesthorpe Church to Annie Jordan, of Bilston, on January 18th, 1873. The church, Baptist, had a deal to say about being unequally yoked. The dear girl heeded them not, and I have this to say, I have married twice, and no man on this earth ever lived a happier life. I never at any time interfered with their religious opinions, nor with their children's. Falling out, ill temper and all that sort of thing never enters our home. I have had thirteen children, eight of whom are now living. Four of them are married and have happy homes. One boy over thirty and single, abstainer and non-smoker. Seven girls, three of whom live at home. I never saved £2O in my life, am a Socialist and envy no man I know. The later lines of the above sheet read very , much like conclusion, and I fancy the editor must be growing weary of my long- drawn-out story. I have said the British wire trade was getting bad ; it really was in a desperate state. Our weaving mills were supplied with German wire. Mr. Peter Rylands told me the firm could make more money by ordering in Germany and re-shipping to customers. Of course the men would not believe this, although we drawers were, year after year, not working even half time. At length the employers called a meeting of the men's representatives. I stood for the men *and Mr. Claude Morris for the Warrington employers. We met in numbers sufficient to appear formidable. -James Speight, of Manchester, and I were the spokesmen for the men. We talked and</p>
        <p>talked, with the result that at the end we received a suggestion in the shape of a new wage list. When •we were parting Mr. Johnson of Manchester came to me and putting his band on my shoulder, said, " Whitham you ought to have been a seller and not a drawer of wire." Excuse this bit of conceit. When we came to read the new list it was a shocker; but there was something else that shocked me much more than the new list, and that was the utter incapacity of our men to reason it all out. It was suggested that Speight and I should go to the various wire districts of Germany. We went, saw, and I at least was conquered. It often seems to me that me zi see as they wish, and not as to facts. I could only report as to facts. We saw men with the help of a boy, that is, one man and a boy taking any number of blocks, working long hours, the man making about 245. a week, the boy about 4s. This really looked as if the whole of our wages were doomed. We were not hurried, we had lots of time to think it out and make suggestions. Only one idea occurred to me, and it stayed with me, influencing- all my actions during the long strike we foolishly rushed into and that was to throw ourselves into the master's hands, and throw on them the onus of providing new machinery, or fresh and radical methods of working, always keeping in view the desir- ability of the individual wage keeping near what it was at that time. No use, the men could only see that moving of increased weights should only mean increased wage, and not a reduction. We had one more meeting with the masters but nothing came of it. When we applied for another meeting, we received a reply ,of which the following is a part. "That it be clearly understood that the reduced list, as a whole, is accepted by the men. That it be clearly understood that the only subject to be discussed at the meeting be any anomalies in price in the new list, or any particular prices that may be thought by the men to be specially unequal, or bear particularly hard upon them. That methods of working, speed of blocks, &amp;c., it is clearly understood are not to be discussed at that meeting. Yours—Claude J. Morris. Mr. H. Whitham, 170, Manchester Lane." This letter meant, of course, that there was nothing to discuss, and in the middle or latter end of April, 1884, the strike was in progress. In the " Examiner " of May 10th, 1884, appeared an editorial with the heading: " The Wiredrawers' Strike. Settlement of</p>
        <p>the dispute." _ So that trouble also had passed</p>
        <p>away. I think it best not to male any further remarks on the dispute. There was a deal of newspaper writing, and I had lots of hard blows given me. I think I saved the trade to England as a fairly good wage- earning trade, and to Warrington especially. There was a great deal of misery and poverty as a result of drink. I could mention lots of men's names, now I should fancy gone, who could find money for drink, but none for the Union, and who had to be paid strike pay. About three years after these stirring times I met Mr. A. Rathbone coming to my home with a message from Mr. Carson to the effect that an opportunity was offered me of going to San Francisco. I took the chance, I am pleased to say, but I have had some hard nuts to crack, in the difficult task of getting down to bed rock. I left or lost, I should say, my first situation because, first I would not don a uniform and parade with the Republican party, nor yet speak in favour of ProtectiNa, and again because I organized the men into a Union, and my name began to appear' in- the Press as chairman of the Organizing Committee. Then I 'started a rather lengthy correspondence in a Republican paper on Free Trade versus Protection" and foolishly signed my name and address. One way of answering me was to send abusive letters through the mail, and in one of these I first saw the skull and cross bones, the writer telling me I ought to have gone on to China. I left San Francisco in the end because the American Steel Trust shut the .wire mill up and again I collapsed, but time was on my side and all things come to those who wait. I went to Worcester, Massa- chusetts, and commenced drawing trolley wire. - On an average I earned about 245. a week. This in plain English spelled poverty, but it soon came to an end. The panic of 1894 drove about 2,000 of Washburn and Moon's men into the streets. Then I found my way among a lot of foreign anarchists. This sort of thing never suited me, and so I left the town,went to New York, and returned to California by way of Panama, leaving my wife and four children behind me. After reaching San Francisco I was a long time before I could get work. 1 went into the country ,f n d got work among the grapes and raisins. I began to think I was never going to see •ny wife and children again. I never befoiT3 was so depressed in spirits. I had told one of my Warrington friends the state I was in, and by accident an old acquaintance asking as to my condition and being told, at once sent me n. Two other old friends sent me an occasional dollar, which cheered me up and kept me from going 'down. In Warrington I had been teetotal, and had kept it until this time. Leaving the raisin sheds one night I was so utterly broken-hearted, so completely Idejected and lost that I called in a saloon and had a glass of beer. I never in all my troubles thought of think as a cure-all. I left the saloon and went to my lodgings to find a letter calling me to the city. As I sat next morning looking out of the carriage window at the beautiful place I was leaving, a gentleman sat oppbsite me and he made the remark "What a beautiful place this is." "Yes," I said in answer, "It is a beautiful place and a horrid hell hole." lat once had the whole seat to myself. I soon had a job in the city, and I held it until just recently. As I said above "time was on my side." My children grew up, and proved capable and industrious, and to-day among them they own quite a big lot of land,.and my wife tells me she owns a bit, besides what is in the Cemetery. In November, 907,1 had an attack of pneumonia, and those of my family who could be reached were summoned to what was thought would be my deathbed. The recovery has been slow and only partial, as I find the complaint remains with me more or less chronic. My legs also are very feeble. The shock I received at the earthquake almost paralyzed them, and indeed, had I not been fortunate in obtaining a job as store- keeper at one of our railroad depots my working days \ would have been over then. As a member of the "Prince of Wales" Lodge, I am now in receipt of its benefits. At the time of the earthquake I had 21 cases of books to remove. To remove these and my household effects, and to keep my eyes on the fire called for quick work, but I did it. I have twice been at close quarters with the grim reality; the first time during the voyage from Liverpool to New York, of which the story was told at the time in the "Examiner," and again with the sickness referred to above. At the present time, reading and a little writing enable me to yass my time in comfort and happiness, quietly waiting the time when I also shall cross the bar. Such is my story. H. WHITH A.Me. C., 59, Broderick-street, San Francisco, July 9th, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>THREE MONTHS IN INDIA, SYRIA, AND PALESTINE.</head>
        <p>THREE MONTHS IN INDIA, SYRIA, AND PALESTINE.</p>
        <p>By Truda H. Crosfield. (ARTICLE V.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ANCIENT JOPPA.</head>
        <p>THE ANCIENT JOPPA.</p>
        <p>At Port Said we left the big ss. "Persia" and sailed up the shores of Palestine in a little Italian vessel, which sailed by night and traded in harbour by day. Our first sight of the Holy Land was on nearing the port of Jaffa. Low sandy shores and sandstone rocks stretched either side of the little cramped town. It is here that tradition localises the legend of Perseus and Andromeda, but I saw no rocks such as Royal Academicians accustom us to expect a 3 a background. Jaffa itsqf is the smelliest town I have ever been in, and the roads are mere tracks with huge holes in them. We took a drive round to view the celebrated orange groves, just then in full flower. The overwhelming sweetness of their scent mingled strangely with the terrible smells from liquid black ditches on either side of our track! There is a Greek convent on the site of Tabitha's tomb, and an ancient house near the harbour is pointed out as that of Simon the tanner. A stone cistern and very old and twisted fig tree stand in its tiny court. The view cannot have changed much since St. Peter stood there and looked down on the sandy shore and rolling waves. These waves often make landing at Jaffa impossible, or very risky, for there is no harbour and one has to be rowed in in open boats. But we were fortunate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FLOWERS OF CARMEL.</head>
        <p>THE FLOWERS OF CARMEL.</p>
        <p>Next morning found us anchored a mile out to sea at the foot of. Mount Carmel. Haifa is the port here, a clean, red-and-white modern town. The hills sweep handsomely north- wards, and ten miles across the bay lies St. Jean d'Acre, the base from which tin Crusaders manceuvered. We had a d'Aightful day on Mount Carmel. A wind- ing road leads to the monastery at one end, and all along the road-side grew the most beautiful flowers. Large scarlet anemones were everywhere,varied by purple ones; yellow cytisus cyclameas, marigolds and wild mignonette made the air balmy with scent ; and everywhere grew bushes and bushes of white cistus which just happened to be in full bloom.. The top of Carmel at that end was covered with shrubs of the large white fluttering blossoms, for the soil is apparently too stony for,use. Further on, we met with olive groves and nice fir plantations, due to the work of a thrifty German colony. Our driver took us along peaceably till suddenly we were drawn up i front of a large house surrounded 1)y pines. "English lady live here: you must</p>
        <p>! . • 3• • • , . ' $•", • . . • - ,e"."... ''''' • • 41, , I ' * . , . , i a . . • i*;•;',•''','s,'- ' • ',,,. • • , 4, ~,, ~.. A . • s, ' '' :,•;:,4 . , ,• , 7.Q ~ . ni",!...., • • , ' • ,A ' ^? ' • '''''''''.!•4:l2;:4, ',l-Alf'''.-.4'. ' ' 1 1••: . 7 «if.l--7.14:': '‘.. ''./,' s. ;i '' •,,„',.'r „. 'l'''''''' ' - . ' -' ^ 4'7 ~,,.'".,.'.itlii:::;.:.-i.,,i:•r:. ,‘ ,' ' , ~ - ,s . ; ~, :4:,,,',,,'.,-^.';'''' ' : ; ' 0 ', ''.."•-• - , • , .• ' '''''' ''. ' ~.3. '''' ;: - f.l+ ' ~' ,^' ..03.- '' 4°7 "7", ,' . ~- . - • .. Pk 14, ' 'll ki - , •,,,` , .e**".'''' y ~ 4 • ~ ,' , ‘",:`, . • , '' i .'t ••; .I'. , ,at, ,3 .oti 4; ' '' `I , 1.. •••••""1 ,t', t ', ' .t„ , ..,,. ..• ,-• , , ' ~, tt,,,,• . /*'' ' ..... •-•', , . ~ 4 „ ..‘ .01'''''''''' ',W",,,r4' ~ , . *6' 0 , .. , •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>GREAT STONE AT BAALBEK. (72 FEET LONG AND 16 FEET HIGH).</head>
        <p>THE GREAT STONE AT BAALBEK. (72 FEET LONG AND 16 FEET HIGH).</p>
        <p>see her," said the old man, and quite refused /to go on. So, introduced by the driver, we were fortunate in making the acquaiutance of Miss F. E. Newton,a charming missionary lady. She works amongst the children of Haifa, and has her home on Mount Carmel where she invites overworked missionaries to spend their holidays and recruit amid the • cool sea breezes. PALESTINE'S POLITICS.</p>
        <p>Miss Newton had a thorough grasp of the political situation in Palestine. She says the Germans were paramount until the overthrow of the Sultan; but that the Turkish Liberals knew very well who had supported their reactionary ruler, and German prestige has gone down whilst English has risen under the new regime. She said, however, that the new government guarantees safety in the towns, but has its hands too full to take care of country districts ; and that the first result had been an enormous increase in gunshot wounds I As many as four a day would be brought into the mission hospitals. The wandering Arabs or Bedouins had under- stood that every man was now at liberty—to do what he liked! Each one promptly bought a gun, if he had not one already, and would then march through the country side, saying to the peasantry "give us that sheep," or goat, or anything else which took his fancy; and not hesitating to shoot if the object desired were not at once handed in. Miss Newton said that the real Turks (that is Tartars) were few. in number, and were mostly officials in high positions. They were good rulers, and the Wall of Damascus had just then been down to Jerusalem to quell the violent dis- putes between the synod and priests of the Syrian Greek Churches. This was a most complicated quarrel, involving place and privilege on the part of a few high dignitaries and starved Churches on the other ; and the Patriarch, an old and honest man, had not the real power to overrule his council and help the people. The confusion of races, creeds, speech and coinage is endless in Palestine. Missions also abound, and what- ever may be thought about conversions (of which there seem to be very few genuine cases) the educational and medical work done by the missionaries is enormous and most beneficial. There is no doubt that the educa- tion given to the last generation of Syrians by English, American and foreign missions has enabled the peaceful revolution to take place. The children attending the schools are .mostly Christians already, who would otherwise get no proper education; but everyone who comes is admitted to the hospitals, provided there is room, a,nd the peasantry love and trust their missionary doctors and nurses, though as followers of Islam they should look on their benefactors as despised Christians!</p>
        <p>Damascus, and say he was martyred for helping St. Paul to escape down the city wall. Next day we started for Baalbek and wound for some hours up and across Lebanon in the train. Chill weather came on, and by the time we had broken the back of the ascent, and were running down into the upland plain, which lies between Lebanon and Anti-Lebanon, slashing rain came on and sopped us as we changed trains at the Junction of Rayak.</p>
        <p>This is a marvellous and ancient plain. Here Abraham is said to have pastured his flocks when he left Mesopotamia, and older still, on a slope of Anti-Lebanon, Cain is said to have slain Abel, and the site of his altar is still pointed out! The two great snowlaid ranges run north and south of it, and white glittering Hermon closes the southern end. The plain itself is purple-brown with plough land where it is not vivid green with crops. The colouring of both Syria and Palestine is certainly glorious in the spring, and I can imagine artists going wild over it. The mulberry is-largely grown as food for silk- worms, and the bright damp green leaves refreshed the eye after the bare brown slopes of the mountain sides, where there was no water. On of the chief charms of the land- scape to my thinking, however, is that there is no smoke to thicken the beautiful clear air, and the countryside is not divided up into blocks and patches by the use of hedges and walls. There are none, and barely any roads, to break the sweep of the land.</p>
        <p>BAALBEK lies midway between the ranges towards the Northern end of this high plain. Its ruined temples are-said to be amongst the most gigantic in the world. Certainly for grandeur they equal anything in Rome, though for beauty they cannot approach the winged loveliness' of Greece; but they• lack the glorifying halo of historic interest. No great names known to us moved in these huge buildings; and so the stones lie mute. I will not describe the ruins except to say that their vast size takes one's breath 'away. Three cyclopcean blocks in the base of the sun temple's outer wall measured each about 60ft. in length 15ft. in height; and it is supposed, the same in width. My cousin and I here rode for the first time upon a camel! Or rather, we rode on two boxes turned upside down and covered with a gorgeous tattered carpet. The only camel in Baalbek was to convey us out to the stone quarry. It had, of course, knelt down for us to sit on the carpet-covered boxes. When it rose, about ten Arabs held on to our feet, our skirts, and hands, for there was really nothing for us to grasp—not even the hump, which was hidden between the boxes! The camel's walk is a series of long slouching jerks forward; we preferred the trot, although equilibrium was then more difficult to su§- tain. The stone which we went .out to the quarries to see is 72 feet long and 16 feet high, and is said to be the largest single hewn stone in the world. What builders cut it out and then left it there, one does not know; but there it has lain for 1,600 years,</p>
        <p>half a mile from the temple. The temples are built of the limestone abounding in Syria and Palestine, which goes red-brown with age; and against this the showers of white pear blossom looked beautiful. Pale green, silvery poplars are the Main features of the Baalbek gardens, as they call the cultivated land lying round the little town. It is saved from the prevailing brown barrenness, thanks to a large and clear spring of water which has never failed Baalbek. It was Helio- gabalus who caused Heliopolis, as he called Baalbek, to be built A.D. 218-222; and as the name indicates, it was for the worship of the sun. The wealth of carved frieze and cornice, niche and cupola simply amazed one; and the six great pillars of Jupiter's Temple left standing after the last earthquake are 75ft. high and 8 yards around, whilst their enta- blature was 17ft high alone! It was the 28th March when we travelled down again to Rayak Junction and started to cross Anti-Lebanon at a height of about 6,000 ft. In our compartment happened to be a young Syrian, who was also a young Turk in the good sense of the expression. We were, fortunate enough to hear from him an account of how the bloodless revolution came about. The chief honour rests with the Macedonian officers, but it was the tele- graphists of Salonika who put the touch- stone to the powder. This revolution came after 700 years of tyranny; the Army began it, the Telegraphists completed it. Liberal agitators had tried the Army for 30 years, and had at last won their way. One reason may have been because the bulk of the Army went unpaid, only the Sultan's favourites reciving their money. At Salonika two colonels, backed by 400 soldiers, came to the Governor, said the Army was Liberal, and demanded a constitution. The Governor was himself a liberal, but he telegraphed to the Sultan, "Macedonia in an awful state: fear cannot keep it under. Army become liberal must grant them Constitution; I stand alone." The telegraphists, overjoyed, wired this not only to the Sultan but all ' over the Empire! In Damascus, Beyreut, everywhere, joy reigned supreme. People thought the Constitution was already granted. But the Sultan made a last effort. He sent—Shemsik Pasha, a reactionary field- marshal, to Salonika, with troops, in order to overawe Macedonia. The Liberals con- sulted hastily. They had 400 names inscribed on a list ; these were patriots who had vowed to sacrifice their lived at any moment if asked to do so for the country's cause. Twelve names were drawn by lot, but all twelve were up country, and not quickly available. "You cannot wait to send for them," said a colonel ; "it must be done now; let me do it." They demurred, but he cried "I go; good-bye in case I never see you again," and left the Conspirators forthwith. He went to seek Shemsik Pasha, met him on the steps of the telegraph office, and shot him in the chest six times. Shemsik's, officers rushed up crying" Who has done this ?" "I have," said the colonel, coolly, "follow me." And they did This colonel is now living in Salonika. Unfor- tunately I have forgotten his name! The change in the Constitution has also caused wonderful changes in trite fortunes of many Turkish subjects. The Pashas corres- pond more or less to our Peers. A Liberal one, Tuaad Pasha, was exiled to Damascus and imprisoned there for seven or eight years, with a high railing round the house, so that he could only see the sky! Four days before the Constitution, orders came from Constantinople to increase the height of the railing as fruaad Pasha saw too much sky! The next thing was, the door of his house of imprisonment was opened. At first he refused to go forth, and then only when he knew in what capacity. The New Government replied not only as Pasha but as a Mushia—a man with authority even over Pashas. There are about seven Mushias in the whole. Empire ! The Sultan . was immensely wealthy, but then he paid 30,000 spies. Every officer in the Army was supposed to be one. Only Musselmen served in the army formerly but now every creed will serve. The Sultan sent for the Presi- dent of the New Constitution when he saw no more resistance availed, and said" My son, what a grand work you have done; come embrace me," and kissed him on both cheeks when he would have liked to kill him. Our informant also related how a coachman of the Sultan had just been ordered 7 days' imprisonment for assaulting a citizen. He refused to go and appealed to the Sultan. But the Procurator General said he would throw up his office unless the man was sent to prison—and he went.</p>
        <p>The young Turk's state of mind can only be described as uplifted ; but alas ! since then heavy clouds have again overspread the horizon. We also met an elderly Syrian who heartily wished that England would take over the whole Empire and would adminiAter it as she has done Egypt, and this wish I believe is pretty widespread. It makes one realize how highly the, freedom and good government of England are esteemed. We ran down a long winding valley literally filled with apple blossom till the air was per- fumed with it. I have never seen such blossom before. Then we rounded a bend and came on our first view of Damascus.</p>
        <p>DAMASCUS THE BEAUTIFUL.</p>
        <p>It is a large white city lying in the centre of miles of thick green orchards and gardens, and watered as everybody knows, by the rushing, blue-green river Abana, now called Barada. I don't suppose anybody has ever been to Damascus without quoting "Are not Abana and Pharpar, rivers of Damascus, better than all the rivers of Israel." Indeed it is extraordinary how real all the old Biblical texts become to one as one sees the shepherd leading his flock instead of driving it, and carrying the rod and staff, one to guide the flock, and the other, shaped like a long wooden club to fend off evil beasts, the plough- man ploughing with a yoke of oxen and the strings of camels padding patiently along with their heavy loads. Outside the glorious miles of orchard-gardens spreads the dry brown plain east wards losing itself on the horizon in the direction of Palmyra, whilst to north and south rise hills. It was somewhere amongst these southern hills that St. Paul's conversion took place. High on the slopes of Anti-Lebanon which the train has just orossed, Mahomet is said, whilst still a camel driver, to have first perceived the city and to have declared it was so like paradise that whilst he had work to do on earth, he dare not go into it for fear of never caring to leave it again. Accordingly he turned his back on the city; a monument marks the spot. The Damascans love to comOare their city to a pearl set in emeralds, and minarets, domes, and flat terraces on the roofs rise white and gleaming from amongst the sea of foliage. But in 1860 a most terrible massacre of the native Christiq,ns by the Mahommedans must have turned the whole city red. Six thousand Christians were slaughtered, and dead and dying were burnt together for the whole Christian quarter was set on fire. Indeed that any escaped at all was due to the presence of a man whose name should never be forgotten. This hero was Abd-el-Kadr, an Algerian Moor, who, when finally captured by the French, was</p>
        <p>allowed to live with some followers in Damascus. Hundreds of fugitives fled to his quarters and were sheltered by him and when thousands of Mahommedans, excited to ferocity, came storming and yelling to slay them, the old hero with only about 20 Moors at his back, rode out into the midst of the frenzied mass of murderers, and rising in his stirrups,bade them cease from their blood- thirsty work,for that they were fools if they thought Europe would not exact vengeance fQr such brutality. These words from a fellow Moslem and such a renowned leader, actually checked the frenzied thousands and saved the remnant of the Christians. But no due reparation was ever demanded, and it is little wonder if the Moslems to this day have remained insolent and overbearing. It makes one simply sicken to read of the wholesale slaughter and agony which .befel in that terrible .month of July 1860 ; and once again it was borne in upon one how right and necessary it was that those who are the guardians of civilization should be trained and disciplined men, able to defend it, and to -succour their brethren in' rough and dangerous lands. . . .</p>
        <p>It was after this terrific massacre that Mrs. Bowen Thompson was moved to estab- lish the B.S.M. (the British Syrian Mission) mainly to educate Syrian children in Damascus• and elsewhere. The good these mission schools have done must be untold. There are many societies at work, of which the best known are the C.M.S, (Church</p>
        <p>Missionary Society) the London Jews Mission, the Irish Presbyterion and Edin- burgh Medical Missions.</p>
        <p>DAMA SCAN STREETS.</p>
        <p>It was at one of these Mission schools, the Irish Presbytarian Girls' schwl in charge of Miss Dill and Miss Logan, that we were to spend a week. Our first impression of ,Damascus was that our carriage would never, never get through the streets. There was literally not six inches to spare either side of the wheels in some of the narrower lanes. The high, blank walls rose either side and shut us in, and as the population lives ever in suspense,windows are small and high up, and doors are rather barred gates. Miss Dill's khevasse (ei janitor) Abu Tewfik, a stout dignified man of forty, had met us at the station and now drove in one ramshackle victoria with the luggage, whilst we drove in a second. Once we met another carriage which had to back into a right-angled street, an impossible looking operation, and then we found ourselves driving down a covered-in lane, whose end looked like a funnel, though this must hg,ve been due to the straight perspective orrits blank walls. Houses were built over us, and we suddenly emerged into a still stranger thoroughfare,a twilit bazaar! These bazaars are the chief feature of Damascus; they are quite wide streets,floored with tan or soft earth, and covered with tarpaulin high overhead, so that the light is always subdued. On either side range the</p>
        <p>mittee of elderly Danish pastors, come to ' visit their own missions and to make anH extensive report theron. Miss Dill kindly invited them to meet us at tea. They were all of them giants with leoline heads of hair and the gentlest and most charming manners, but as they mostly spoke German, our inter- course was fragmentary and halting, as I regret to say Ido not know it well. One old Herr Pastor spoke so very low that, as there was a teaparty of 16 going on, all chattering at once, I could not quite catch what he said. When I heard it was his first visit to the Holy Land and he expected it to be his last, I thought" what extraordinary courage to come out as a missionary at the age of 80" (for he looked that) and replied " You are very bravo" for I thought he meant he would die in the Holy Land, whereas he was only paying a skimmer visit! The devotion of the Moslems is certainly outwardly very great. All good Moslems pray five times a day, and' prostrate themselves devoutly on their prayer carpets in the mosques. It is certainly a very living religion, and I am told that good Moslems are really rather a fine type of humanity. Influenced by their fatalism they show wonderful endurance and patience under suffering, wrong or uncertainty. Prayer, almsgiving, fasting, pilgrim- age and frequent ablutions are strictly enjoined by the prophet; prayer must be preceded by washing, as, from a person not clean, prayer is not acceptable; the dress also should be clean. Think how much this stands for! It is a mistake to believe that the Koran says women have no souls; on the contrary it says "Whosoever performs good works and believes, men as well as women, shall enter Paradise." But all the same, women are never to be seen in the mosques where the men are busy saving themselves, and they certainly seem of inferior account, and always go veiled in the streets. It is however the exception, we were told, for a Mohammedan to have more than one wife, and the women that we saw looked happy and well-cared for. The Turk is a good humoured and kindly being when not roused to ferocity. But when one says ' Turk" one does not quite know whether one is speaking of a Turk proper, that is a Tartar, or an Arab, a Greek, a Copt, a Druse, an Armenian, a Kurd, or a Jew! Syrians are all that, and the confusion of races is only equalled by the confusion of creeds and coinage'. Never have I come across such a coinage; a certain dumber of riehassis, for example, make half a bishlik, but two half bishliks do not make a whole one! When I heard that, my spirit fainted within me. We relied, of course,upon Abu Tewfik who accompanied us when we visited the Bazaars. ki THE rAZAARS.</p>
        <p>The different crafts are grouped in different streets as was common in mediazval England. Thus, there is the goldsmiths' bazaar, the shoe bazaar, whgre delightful cherry-coloured</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>ELIJAH'S WELL.</head>
        <p>ELIJAH'S WELL.</p>
        <p>We sat long talking with Miss Newton, and gazed down over the rich corn plains, the blue bay, and the far encircling hills. Then we were taken to see Elijah's well. A German .Herr Pastor accompanied us with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>whom we kept up a conversation as stumb- ling as our footsteps, for the top of Carmel is one mass of loose limestone fragments, and we had a steep and painful descent into a narrow gully where there is room for the bed of a dried torrent, patches of corn and fig trees, and one shady nook where a clear spring wells out into an ancient stone cistern. This is most probably one of the Prophet's retreats; of the scene of his competition with the priests of Baal, we will speak later. A herd of black-and-white goats came down the gully as we were there, and trotted over almost impossible perpendicular surfaces in the chalky rock; they are unequalled mountaineers. It took 40 minutes to climb back up the hot steep sides to the stony plain on top with its carpet of flowers. Hence we viewed the great plain of Esdraelon (formerly Jezreel) where Armageddon, they say, is destined to be fought, and in the dis- tance, Tabor, little Hermon, Gilboa, and the hills of Galilee. Here we also saw in the grass a quaint chamelion. We picked up the queer creature with its melancholy mouth and lens-like eyes and placed it on red soil; but the colour did not change beyond a tinge of brown dulling the lustre of the green. Then we regretfully said good-bye to Miss Newton, drove down through the .fragrant pines, and re-embarked, to awaken next morning in Beyreut harbour. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>This prosperous town is crowded with Missions, amongst which the great American College stands out conspicuous. My cousin had letters to t-Wo missionaries whom we visited, and we drove round the outskirts admiring the rich soil and flourishing-vines, olives, and wheat crops. Behind this rises Lebanon like a tremendous sloping rampart, 10,000 feet high and capped with snow. In the afternoon we drove out to the Dog River, 10 miles along the shore, and celebrated in classical history. All this land belonged to the Phoenicians, and they had set a stone dog idol at the mouth of this river, the which was said to bark whenever enemies sailed in sight. This dog can still be clearly seen thrown down upon its side in the sea; the waves were washing over it when we looked down from the road, but in the slack of each billow we could clearly make out the dog's shape. It is near here also that tradition locates the battle between St. George and the Dragon. Considering that St. George is our patron saint, it is dreadful to find how vague everyone seems about him. He is also supposed to have lived in Cappadocia, further north, but they show one of his tombs in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>GATE OF HEROD, JERUSALEM.</head>
        <p>GATE OF HEROD, JERUSALEM.</p>
        <p>shops and booths, and a moving crowd in varied garments is always passing each other and the carriages down the centre. Having traversed two or three of these bazaars we drove down several more narrow lanes and could only marvel at the nerve and judgment of our Arab driver who never so much as once touched a wall. It was a relief to see that Abu Tewfik's carriage had stopped at a big brass studded door under an archway, and to enter this a moment later and meet our kind hostess. Damascan hoilses are all built with a courtyard, usually marble-paved, in which is an ornate cistern filled with Barada water, and round which grow orange trees, oleanders and various other shrubs and flowers.. In this case,four houses had been made into one, and there was a garden as well as the court- yard. The girls number 170, and are of all ages. They reminded me of the Swiss-French girls with whom I was at school in Lausanne; their manners are quick and polished, they are lively and intelligent, and immensely keen on their position in class, their examina- tions, and their favourite mistresses. We spent a most pleasant week under this hospitable roof, and went round several times in the morning, hearing the classes repeat their English lesson. As the rest was. in Arabic however, it was lost upon us.</p>
        <p>slippers hang up with wooden pattens inlaid with mother-o'-pearl; the silk, the brass,the copper bazaars, and so forth. The pierced brass work, as exhibited in their hanging lamps, is most beautiful. The manners of the natives are as dignified also as their language I regret to say it,' but everyone agrees that the Mohammedan Syrians are a finer,stronger, and more honest race than the Syrian Christians. Their faces wear a serious air, their walk is slow and stately, they never stare or push, or talk loud, and one is told that plural marriages are uncommon amongst them. THE WALT'S WIVES</p>
        <p>Apropos of the latter, the present governor or Wali of Damascus has had a romantic story. He married young, and livwl happily for many years; but he had no children, and at last his relatives insisted on his taking a second wife. This was a young girl. Far from the elder wife being jealous, she looked upon the newcomer as a daughter, and they formed a most happy trio. Then the young wife died, and the older couple were incon- solable. The Wali used to go and mourn on her grave, till those troublesome relations again interfered, and said it looked absurd; whereupon his wife went for him. Before leaving Damascus, I must describe going to tea with the Abu Tewfik. (To be Continued).</p>
        <p>, • s • 4 4 .. . 1 . 1 .r., ~; f , •-. ,-, .., Si- •'. I. ~.- ;:'!"-, „14, , 1 k. _ ~ ‘• ~, , ~, , ~...... , t A, ..11-;-.1-i; Ar ..„..,..... . . . 's' • f.' • ?-,44 ' ' 4 , ' .* r'' - "' • , . . .. ......„.'. 4 1- ft. 7„. ~ , ...,...- • ,i,114A ...,. , • ,r, ;','", ~, , - - /.. ' , , ' •'... , --" .' ' --- ''. . 7: ' i '''' lt*:' , 1 ' • . , 41 ~7', , - • ..,,,,..ti t...e.,,, 1.. „.- ~. . ~,.. ~,.. ~. . . •••‘ „4, ~,,,*,,,,,... ~ . , . - ...,...,. •„, ~ ~ ~,,,..,..., , ....., ...•,..,.,...-....A. 4..,....„..6?„4, ~ : , ',.'"i•V'''.:•.!..A...,,5:..--Arii,A,:i•-•:-...,,: . • ..-, 4. • . ..,, 4''''. - `„-. ,„,, ! 4114,...,.....4 9:.,i,;--.,..,..,.., , .fii..c.,4..t„....,..::,,,,.:,.....,..,.:,...,,,,, ~. ~..... , ;,,,,.,...,:.,..,...„.....,.....,....,...::...... , 1,4ttf...........,..„...„: ..„..„...41.......,..r.„4:.......-....,,,..,....,.,... . , : ~. .. . ..... .„.„. ~....... .—..,. ~.. . . , . ~ ~ ' .. . . • , , , 1."-r,,,,,,,..,.. , -,...Ac,,,.,.-,,,,,- .•':-.1&gt;....:F: .... . . _ . ~,iletf ,•••• - - - --' •• ...• :-.,..,i45...-;3* .- ' • :. . ... •10 : -....'.: ' r •••• \ -4, •4 i',.., .&lt; ...,.... ~. , •., . . , • .. , ~ ~ . t , • , .. • .0.4:'.•:".•;. ' '; s:. . - . , , ~4e • •. i -it i&lt;:,', :....-,t. , . •,,s , • i ~ ' ,„. , . ' o. ”..... ...' • ,1 i ~.,,,. ~.;;1:.:*..:. :44.,,.., '...;,..'*,Y : :.k;.,' :. :: '''''''''.;•.r.; . ,1: ~4.1: II ' „,,Alk•,, ' ':-.'::::.'...:,,,..e.:;t,,,-.4:''',' :.::,4 •,:.',...;):;,‘,. -....- ~..-. .-4 1.....,,—., L,i''.:''''' ~•••••• ---'''' ' ... ... .. .. . ..., ..... . . . .. ._.. .. " ... ~ .. _ v • .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>FLOWERY ARABIC.</head>
        <p>FLOWERY ARABIC.</p>
        <p>Arabic is by universal consent one of the most difficult of languages; it is also extremely flowery. English workers tell one that after using Arabic circumlocutions, it seems quite strange to put things in our brief, harsh, direct English. For instance, if you are annoyed with a driver for blocking your way, you say to him. Oh my brother, permit that I may pass you,' or 'Oh my uncle, will you back your mule ; ' whilst if it is a man addressing a lady he calls her 'Oh my eye 1 ' As almost every sentence is interlarded with proverbs and apt sayings, I could' only marvel at the courage with which the ladies and men of the missions had attacked it. We learnt a few indispens- able words, such as " tiyeb," good; "bus," enough ; " daharak," "mind your back," i.e. "get out of the way," and " hakim," doctor; but ambition led us no further. Mr. and Mrs. MacFarland, of the Irish Presbyterian boys' school, were most kind, and the former took us several rides outside the city. He rode a handsome and fiery Arab, whilst we were on two white animals, 'rather shaky in the knees, as all the horses are out there, but so keen to go, that whips were a superfluity. We rode on pathways through orchards and crops; all watered by the swift green swirling streams of the Barada. Loose stones and haphazard paths and continuous cantering made it about as uncertain riding as I have ever done. But we enjoyed it better than anything else. We also had the pleasure of meeting a corn-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>EARLESTOWN GROCER FINED.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN GROCER FINED.</p>
        <p>William Henry Griffithe, a grocer, of Derby-street, Earlestown, was charged at the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wednesday with being drunk.—Evidence showed that at a quarter past twelve the previous after- noon defendant was drunk in front of his shop. He was creating a disturbance, and there was a large crowd of people around. He had to be locked up, but was liberated late the same night.—ln answer to Mr. A. Browne who defended, the constable stated that defend- ant hid had a long spell of teetotalism, during which he was a very smart man.—Mr. Browne said Griffiths had been a teetotaller for a few years. His wife had gone to Gloucester to some friends, and while she was there he had taken to drink. He (Mr. Browne) sincerely hoped that it would not occur again.—Defendant was fined ss.</p>
      </div>
