      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS,</p>
        <p>Risley. .INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH.—TI e annual tburch sermons were held in the above place of worship :3!1 Sunday %ben Mr. J. Travis of Widnes preached two irquent sermons. The attendance was not quite so tjirge as desired owing to the inclement weather, but tiGae s%ho braved the elements were- well rewarded for .jieir trouble. The choir rendered the anthems "Cast burden upon the Lord," the bass soloist being Mr. 4-uweteaLr I4ythgoe, and in the evening the anthem "Awake, 4,e5 the soprano soloist being Miss B. A. Bate. The i-ting was nicely rendered, and all went away with a lasp'ration from the servieq, t SIMInAy SCHOOL TnEAT.—The annual Sunday School „teat tin connection with the Independent Methodist trch was given on Monday. Tea was provided RA the school for the scholars free, adults being charged r• each. After tea sports were held in a field kindly flt by Mrs. Joseph' Potter. A gcod number of people „.43g present. Cricket, football and numerous other nee were indulged in and the day being fine every- thoroughly enjoyed themselves, About Line tattock friends and members of the church sang at the wlttbouse to show their appfeciation of the generosity Airs. J. Potter in lending the field. _ Thelwall.</p>
        <p>Trip 4,,NNUAL SPORTS.-This popular fixture was 4 'led out for the fourth occasion on Monday, Admiral 11°3_1eaTarry kindly lending his beautiful grounds for ste event. Walton Brass Band was in attendance, and Ze village children were provided with tea free. There :a&amp; sPorts for both young and old. The Maypole dance iiMPerformed in a very creditable manner and praise „ism'Ae te Mrs. Roebuck for the careful manner in which el:_wka.4 trained the child-er. Handsome prizes were t, "ilted to the successful competitors by Mrs. T. or! Williamson, to whom a vote of thanks was passed 4•(=, motion of Mr. Roebuck, seconded by Mr. %V. t_SYler. A vote of thanks was also accorded I`4._ the Admiril for the use of tha field. - Vollowing are the results of the senior sports :-100 liwtus flat race for men: 1, Forrester; 2, J. Kite ; 3, 3. 50 yards marble and spoon race for ladies : 1, 722ir .41322.' Little ; 2, Mrs. Booth ; 3,Miss Edith Vicars. 4, ?Yards flat race for men : 1, H. Harrison; 2, J. Kite; hu,,, Rife. 50 yards flat race for ladies : 2. Mrs. Fair- Miss Maggie Reece; 3, Mrs. Booth. 100 yards eselllarrow race fur men : 1, J. Kite and W. Dean; 2, sYton and Ravenscroft ; 3,3. Forrester and R. Kite, 50 Littd,s- threading needle race for ladies : 1, Clayton ; 2, ;3, Littler. 100 yards flat race for men: 1, 2, Lea ; 3, Clayton. Obstacle r.ice : 1, Kite; 2, sLala ; 3, Clegg. 50 yards sack race : I,'Forrester ; kit."4ll ; 3, P. Kite. 100 yards three-legged race: 3, ~.ancl Forrester ; 2, Ravenscroft and C. S.abotter ; lite and Hunt. 50 yards flat race for marred ladies: leo 14. Fairhurst ; 2, Mrs. Clayton ; 3, Mrs. Booth. 3'ards flat race for married men: 1, J. Kite; 2 fl; 3, Nicholson. Slow bicycle race: Mr. 1, Niel on. Extra race-Stout gentlemen v. girls of 13: r• A. Hunt; 0, Elsie Jarmen ; 3, Elsie Handey.</p>
