      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Hatton</head>
        <p>Hatton</p>
        <p>PICNIC.—On Thursday the members of the Mothers' Sewing Class had their annual excursion, the place chosen being Southport. After a most enjoyable day, the weather being exceptionally fine, they returned home about nine o'clock. The day's outing was due to the bindneEs of Mrs. Secker, of Daresbury.</p>
        <p>Lymm. Mn. EDWIN MosTon—We are sorry to state that Mr. Edwin Moston of Rush Green, went to the ficspital on Wednesday to undergo an operation. His many friends hope that be will soon be restored to health.</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL TRIP.— On Wednesday the above school held its annual trip to Morecambe. Leaving Lymm at 7.45 a.m., about 200 teachers and scholars availed themseves of the day's outing. The Rector was in charge of the whole arrangements. The party reached Morcambe at 9.90. The weather was fine but hazy and everyone seemed to make the best of the time at their disposal. Tea was provided at the Carlton Cafe, when everyone did justice to the good things provided. A most enjoy- able day was spent, especially by the young folks, L3mm being reached about 10.40 p.m.</p>
        <p>HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY'S SHOW.—The annual show of the Lymm and District Horticultural Society will be held on Saturday next in a field in Maltman's-road (by land permission a Mrs. Hartley). £2O is offered in prizes and in addition there will be a number of special awards. A musical programme Will be provided and juvenile sports will be held. The object of the society is to encourage a love of gardening, to render mutual assistance to develop and improve gardens and to obtain a knowledge of the best mode of cultivation of plants and vegetables by monthly discus- sions and lectures in the winter, and by visits to repre- sentive gardens during the surnmcr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Woolston.</head>
        <p>Woolston.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED SUICIDE.—At the Warrington County Petty Sessiens, on Wednesday, William Thornton, of Runcorn, was charged with attempted suicide by cutting an artery in his wrist on the 15th July last at Woolston.—John Stout, a farmer residing at Welgrave Farm, said that on the morning of the 15th July he saw the prisoner near his house. He went out to him and found that he had cut his arm open badly. There was a knife in his possessicn. Witness took him to a neighbouring cottage to have his wound attended to and sent for the police. A constable said that at two o'clock on the 15th of last month be received informa- that prisoner had attempted suicide. On arriving on the scene he found prisoner with a very large wound about 2.ins. long on his wrist. Witness asked prisoner why he had done it and he said that he was "told to do it." Witness took him, to the Infirmary. On the way prisoner became very abusive and struck witness several times on the heal. At the Infirmary hr was detained as an in-patient. On the 21st July witness charged prisoner with the offence who made no reply. In answer to a magistrate,witness said prisoner was perfectly sober at the time he made the attempt.—Prisoner's father said his son felt home on the 15th July. He had made other attempts to take hs life and had, been in an asylum at Tynemouth. After deliberation the Bench decided to adjourn the case until the next Sessions day and in the meantime prisontr will be medically exam!ned.</p>
      </div>
