      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>TOUND DROWNED. A ht 37,erdict of "Found drowned" was returned thlle inquest, in Llaydock, on Tuesday, e '°d9 of J. W. Jermvn aged thirty-two, whose I:otlksltas found floating in anal mar the Doublens 43 on Saturday morning. The evidcnce was that " wbs a native of Motherwell, Scotland, but he 44,41vorked for a contractor namcd Lovell in the Aveull4h Colliery for nice years. Ile was last sten 40414 ca Alcnday wEek, five days before his bcdy was is believed to have fallen in the canal 4 Walking along the towrath.</p>
        <p>LIVING MAN'S TOMB. ...AI ht, fldian Mutiny veteran, Mr. Garrett Ronayne, of tra •i talgalow, Earlestown, has had a massive red nt° ewt, •torn stone erected to his own memory at tiot, n-Jn•Makerfield Cemetery,with a suitable inscrip- there gts d, on, leaving enly a blat•k space for the date of ath and age. The inscription is as under : Here lies • An old Indian Mutiny veteran, GARRETT RONAYNE, who departed this life</p>
        <p>. . .</p>
        <p>Aged—years May he lest in Peace.</p>
        <p>A WOMAN'S THEFTS. At s :4i H e Warrington Petty Sessions, on Wednesday, h" nellv, of Earl-street, Earlestown, was charged v 41,1 stAaline two boys' jackets the property of Ann Stlell, alit') of Earl-at; eet.—Mrs. Caluwell stated that 'l,l4.;llsnand was a blacksmith. She left the jackets hehind the kitchen door on Thursday the 29th Ztere—b,/,/ind at eight o'clock the following morning they '"ssing. She informed the police. The prisoner 111,° right to take the cloth n.—Catherine Kennedy at said that on the 211,th of July she 40 a; t horde. The prisoner went to her house with thillJackets in a bag. The following day bbe again NI: round between half-past eight and tine o'clock. Sats6,lted witness to pledge the jackets for her. WitteeS '0 Jones's pawnshop in Market-street, Earlestown Ptt'vned the jickets for Is. 3d. Wittess gave LAloer the tickets and money.—Mr. Hughes, assistant kkr mrs. Jones's pawnshop, spoke to advancing ed n the j•ickets from Mrs. Kennedy.—P.C. Lenny e-. Lent on necessary information of the theft he 'l4 Ingniries and in consequence found that pi isoner tiso„g"e to Golborne. He telephoned there and, Was detained. Witness afterwards received Cuetedy.—A ivritten statement from prisoner %i;tad bY the magistrate's clerk asking them to deal ItynlY with her. The prisoner, who had many previous "3 itioc'eons against her for different offences, was sent for three months with hard labour.</p>
