      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A WIDNES POLICE "PUNCHER."</p>
        <p>James Broadhurst was sent to gaol for two months at Widncs on Moneay cn charges cf I eing drunk and disorderly and assaulting Policc-constrJ)le Waddington, He struck the constable on the nose, and kicked him severely on the leg. RUNCORN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting on Wednesday, Mr.F.Wisivall presiding, Dr. M`Dougall teported that during July there were thirteen deaths and forty-eight births, com- pared with twenty deaths tlnd fifty-one births in July last year. TLe Runcorn District Friendly Societies' Council invited the chairman and council and otPcials to atteni the annual church parade on SeptemLer 12th and the invitation was accPpted, LANCASHIRE EDUCATION COMMITTEE SCHOLARSHIPS. LOCAL AWARDS.</p>
        <p>Fifteen schoiarships for proficier.cy in Stcondary School subjects, of the value of RC() per year, tenable fora term not exceeding three 3 ears hare just teen arded by the Lancashire County Education Committee. The i.iccessful candidates include George Bart n. Widne?, Widres Municipal Seccndary ; Ethel Chad- wick, Widnes, Widnes Municipal Secondary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LICENCE TRANSFERS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>LICENCE TRANSFERS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at Runcorn, the magistrates transferred the following licences :—Mersey Vaults, from William Hout,hton to James Henry Goouban ; Derby Arms. from William T. Crosby to Hairy Reece; the Waterman's Arms, Western Poia, from Susan Langley to Frederick T. Jakeman : Queen's Hotel, High-street, from Joseph Richaids to Thomas Chorley ; Marine Anis, Percival- lane, from John Henry Edge to John Millington; Bcathouse Inn, Mersey-street, from the late Charles Pugh to Hannah NO ; Foresters' Arm-s, Canal-street, from the late Harry Warburton to Mary Jane Warburton; off-lictnce, 86, Surrey-street, from the late George Crowther to Sarah Crowther; Welsh Harp, Cooper- street, from Samuel Brown to Thomas Frain. RUNCORN AND RELIGIOUS MEETINGS. COUNCIL DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, at Runcorn Urban District Council the secretary (Air. Marshall) said the superintendent of police had informed him that an application had been made by the Runcorn Free Church Council asking if say objection would he taken to holding services in Devonshire-place cm Sunday evenings. Ihe Law Clerk (Mr. Lake) said the council had no power to consent to anybody blocking the highway by religious pervices or anything else. ' Mr. Dean said services in Devonshire-place would be a gren public inconvenience, and he suggested that ths Council grant the use of the Market-place. .Mr. Handley said public meetings had been held in Dovonshire-place for sixty years, and he held that the public had not lost their right of meeting there. How- ever, the market ground was a more suitable place, Mr. Imison objected to giving to any body or society a prior right of anything that IN as the property of the lhe Salvation Army might want the ground. Eventually it was decided to reply that the Council bad no power to grant the use of Devonshire-place. LIBERAL RALLY AT WIDNES. - SUPPORT FOR THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>An enthusiastic meeting was held on Monday evening, in Victoria-square, under the auspices of the Widnes Division Liberal Association. Mr. J. B. Aitken took the chair, and Mr. Fred Horne, the defeated candidate at the last Ludlow election, was the principal speaker. The Chairman said they were holding more meetings in the constituency to keep the people well-informed of what was going on. In his opinion there was only two subjects to speak about, Free! Trale and the Budget. Dealing with the lattt r, he said the Government were cot going to raise- Id. out of the hard-earned wages of the working classes. It was one of the finest Budgets ever produced. Mr. Fred. Horne said the last time he was in Widnes the Budget had just come out, t.nd very few of them realized what the Budget meant. Singe it had been before Parliament all commcnsense people believed in it. It was a peeple's Budget, and Parliament had decided that it, coped with the necessities of tke nation; and the people would resist any interference with it. In discussing the Budget they had to satisfy themselves</p>
        <p>that the taxation was just, right, and necessary, and he defied any right-thinking person to say anything to the contrary. From a Tory point of view the Government was extravagaut, because in DOG they bad promised to check expenditure reduce taxation, and go in for retrenchment as well as reform. It was quite true they were asking for a deal of money, but there was a great difference in the way it was spent. The expenditure was wise, and he detailed the various Government schemes which would swallow it up. Touching upon unemployment, he said that Mr. Winston Churchill's scheme of insurance would go a long way to solve this very pressing problem. The following resclt,tion mas carried unanimously : —" That this meeting records it entire agreement with the proposals of the Government; believes that, with- out increasing the ccst of the necessaries of life or hampering industries, they will secure for this and future years the money needed for the Navy and social organisation, will vindicate Free Trade, will relieve local burdens, and will promote land reform; and this meeting further pledges itrif to support the Govern- ment in carrying into law and in guarding the constitu- tional rights of the House of Commons," Hearty votes of thanks to the chairman and speaker cicsed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
