      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WOMEN'S bUFFRAGE AT BRIDGE FOOT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S bUFFRAGE AT BRIDGE FOOT.</p>
        <p>Those who attended the Women's Suffrage meeting at Bridge Foot on Thursday evening and expected some" fun," were disappointed, for no opposition was met by the speakers. The meeting was organised by the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies (Warrington Branch). The Rev. C. Harvey Cook pre- sided and supporting him on the platform were .Mr. James Ball,and Misses Rathbone (Liverpool), Broadbent and Johnson. The speakers appeared to be fully in earnest and their objects were put very clearly to the crowd of about 1,000 people who listened to them. The CHAIRMAN said that he had a different text that usual to preach upon that evening and it was "The women's questicn." Many of them held that the woman's place was at home, she had nothing at all to do with politics and if she came into -the political world she would be more of a nuisance than she was at present. He was prepared to welcome them to the power of the suffrage on the same footing as men have it now or should have it in the future. (Applause.) Some of the critics said that if they would only go in for adult suffrage then they would be with them' but so long as they went in for women's suffrage on the same footing as men then they would hold off. That was rather foolish, because it was not in the sphere of practical politics to have universal suffrage. It worked, perhaps very well in Australia, Denmark, and other places, but at home we were a slower people than those in the Colonies and oven some of the coun- tries in Europe. At present it would not work for them to demand adult suffrage, so that everybody on reaching the ege of 21 should have the right to vote. 'they wanted the women,nho had the qualrfication,to vote at Parliamentary elections. At present our basis of voting was a money one, and based on the rent. If a women had to pay rates and taxes it seemed to him only lust that she should have a voice in saying what should be done in the country and in Parliament. Continuing, the speaker said that women counted tremendously well in all political struggles. If the women were good as canvassers be thought that with the training that would come with the exercise of the power of the franchise they would become useful politicians. Ile concluded , by moving the following resolution : "That this meet- ing calls upon the Government to grant the Parliamentary franchise to women on the same terms as men or as it may be granted to men." • Miss HATHBONE who is an exceedingly eloquent speaker, in seconding the resoluticn began by telliug her audience that they did not belong to the militant suffragists. They belonged to the society who were going to get the vote by constitutional means. They were not in the habit of breaking up meetings, or making raids upon the House of Commons. Those methods may have berm necessary at the beginning in order to bring forcibly before the public what they were aiming for ; but she believed they were now doing more harm than good, because they were alienating many who would otherwise have been heartily on their side. (Hear, hear). But still the militant movement only showed that the women were prepared to go to prison and to bring upon themselves ridicule, ignoring a vituperation for the cause they. had at heart, it only showed that the cause was a gnat one and the self-sacrifice and the self-devotion of those who advocated women's suffrage by those methods ought to he respected even if they were misguided and ill. chosen. She asked her audience, how many would do what the suffragettes were doing for any cause they had at heart? Continuing she said that the vote would not bring about the millrnium. Votes for men bad not brought about the milleninm. The vote had done much for them, but it had not done everything. When men said: "What do women want with votes ?" she said " What do men want with votes?" They were not classed as women were by the law, wi h criminals, idiots, paupers and children. They desired the franchise as tax payers because they believed in the principle that taxation anti representation sliculd go together.Engl isbmen valued the franchise as a method of protecting themselves against unjust leaislation—legisla- tron which was injurious together industrial interests. English women demanded the vote for the same reason. There errs an enormous number of women engaged in wage earning occupations. There were no less than 67 out of every 100 who had at one time or oilier to earn their own living—such as the factory hands, the domestic servants, shopkeepers, and memlers of the learned professions, teachers, and so forth. Was it not clear that if the legislation repre- sented the interests of the workers, the workers should have some say in making that legislation, and some control of the !Parliament who made it? (Hear, hear.) They had been told by the ant i-suffragists thet the proper place for women was the home, and that women bad no concern in politics. If women's sphere was at home, was it not a fact that women had a right to take an interest in the legislation for the home? The legisla- tion at present was largely affecting domestic life, such as the education of children, the conditions under which Children might be employed, temperance, tariff reform which ,affected the cost of the neces- saries ef life. It was the women who had the spending of the family incomeand therefore it was she who engirt to 'say whether living was to be made dearer or not. Women maintained that there was no real Govcrnn ent "for the people and by the people" so long as the larger portion of the democracy—women —was unrepresented., To give women the vote, as a symbol, as a acknowledgement and a sign of full citizenship would do very great deal to enrich and purify national life. Unfortunately we had plenty of women who confined themselves to the home, the women who could take of nothing but the home, of her difficulty with the servants and of her domestic expenditure. Did they find that such homes were the happiest and hest ? When the man came home in the evening, they could not talk about the great ques- tion that engrossed the self-respecting men of the nation. Many men were driven out of the home because they could find ner real companionship in the borne. From the home they went to the club and to the public-house. Concluding she said that if women had the vote it would give them full citizenship, would raise the status of women and in many ways it would enrich the family Hie. Mr. JAMES BALL then addressed the meeting at some length. He maintained that the vote was justified if only on the question of justice. No questions having been asked Miss BROADBENT Proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman, Miss Rathbone and Mr. Ball. Miss jOHNSTO4 seconded and the meeting concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Warrington Angler's Success._</head>
        <p>Warrington Angler's Success._</p>
        <p>The remarkable sight of 400 anglers fishing for charity was witnessed at Northwich on Monday. They hailed from every part-of Cheshire, Manchester, Wigan, Warrington, Openshaw, Gortou, and Stockport. Twenty pounds in prizes were given, the proceeds being devoted to Northwich Infirmary. A silver cup given by the Brothers Ire' din was also cffered for the Mid- Cheshire anglers. The principal winners were: First prize (£5), T. Leigh, of Warrington, weight 11b. lloz. 13dra.; 2, F. Millington, Lostock ; 3. J. Woodhouse, Gorton; 4, T. Bisbrown, Nortliwich ; 5, J. Lightfoot, Winsford; 6, G. Bates, Nerthwieh; 7, T. Johnson, Wigan; 8, E. Perry, of Walton.</p>
        <p>Women who think</p>
        <p>they know</p>
        <p>CC Have you ever used Fels-Naptha soap ? "</p>
        <p>"Yes, I use it every week."</p>
        <p>" And do you use it the Fels-Naptha way ?"</p>
        <p>"No, I don't. The "%Try idea ! Telling me not to use hot water, and not to boil the clothes ! Do you think / don't know how to do my own washing? "</p>
        <p>Don't you think Fels-Naptha makers know how their soap should be used, better than you do? If they tell you that boiling water spoils it, do you think they haven't got a good reason for it ? You are simply standing in your own light by keeping on in your own back-breaking way. Try the Fels-Naptha way next wash- ing day, and if you don't like it, you'll get your money back. So there !</p>
