      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ENGINE-DRIVER'S FATAL SHORT CUT.</head>
        <p>ENGINE-DRIVER'S FATAL SHORT CUT.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Crewe on Monday, on Harry Hug, a London engine-driver. Deceased drove the American special to Crewe, and was waiting with his engine to return to Landon. tie got off the footplate to ascertain bow long he would be kept waiting, and the theory was advanced that he must have t ased underneath a goods train for a short cut, and was run over, being al most:decapitated. .A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>pit, id...,, Anima</p>
        <p>RITAIN'S WORKERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>DAUGHTER'S TRAGIC DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>Thomas Grundy' a joiner, aged fifty-nine, who had been employed by Dlessts. Greenall, Vbitley, and Co, for thirty-five years, was found dead in his house, 128, Stanhope-street, St. Elekns, on Saturday night. Tie had not been in good health for some time, but worked on Saturday as es,tal. He was alone in the house but was seen standing at the door emokfng at 9 p.m. on Saturday. Later, when his daughter returned home, she found him lying on his back in the kitchen quite dead. The coroner Las been communicated with. ST. HELENS TRAM FATALITY.,</p>
        <p>Edward Keogh, collier, a married man, living at 7, Ellison-square, Thatto Heath, died in the St. Helons Hospital on Tuesday from injuries received by being knr.cked down by a tramcar in Prescot-road, at the top of Croppers-hill, on Monday. It appears that the mil, who was about forty-three years of age, was crossing the road shout 10 30 p.m. when be was knocked down by g car and doubled up underneath. When be was got out and attended by Dr. Latham he was found to have an extensive fracture of the skull, and was quiie uncon- scious. He died in the hospital before his friends could be found and communicated with. - ST. HELENS PIT CAGE ACCIDENT. SIX MINERS INJURED.</p>
        <p>An alarming accident occurred on Tuesday morning when the colliers were going to work at the Ravenhead Pit, belonging to the St. Helens Colliery Company.—lt appears that the men were being sent down to the emergency shaft, owing to the ordinary machinery being stopped for repairs. About a quarter past six, seven men were being sent down in the cage, and when nearing the bottom they were alarmed to find it going a great deal faster than it ought to have done. Indeed, they thought the rope had broken. The cage struck the landing violently, and the men were thrown all about. Six out of the seven were injured. One man, who was in the middle, escaped without injury. The others were taken up to the top and conveyed home, where they were attended lAy Dr. Latham. Thomas Woosey, of Cropper's. hill, was taken home suffering from shock. Timoth7 Lane and the other four men were found to be suffering from sprained ankles and bruises to the leg and back. The men are considered to have escaped very luckily from- the accident. COMPENSATION CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before Deputy-Judge A. Hyslop-Maxwell, Mr. R. H. Rigby, solicitor;applied on behalf Jobn Ellis Hughes, Crow. lane, Earlestown for the recording of a memorsndum of agreement under the Workmen's Compensation Act. Mr.Adshead Elliott, who appeared for the respondents (the L. and N.-W. Railway Co.), objected that his Honour had no jurisdiction in the case. The agree- ment which it was proposed to record stipulated that the company should find applicant regular employment at wh jell he would be able to earn a minimum wage of 255. a week. If the agreement had been come to—and be was satisfied that there was no such agreement—it was not one that would come within the Workmen's Compensation Act. It was not an agreement which could be recorded under the provision of schedule 2 of clause 9, because throughout the Act of Parliament there was nothing to be found by which a workman was empowered to have an agreement aecorded which would guarantee him regular employment at a minimum rate of wages.—Mr. Rigby a.sked for an adjournment in order that a fresh agreement could he. drawn up after con- sultation.—His Honour said Mr. Rigby could bring a fresh application if he wished. All he could do at present was to dismiss the application.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ST. HELENS TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>The three sections of St. Helens Territoe&amp;s, to the number of close on 1,700 men, left home n Sunday to join the great brigade camp at Caerwys, North Wales. The sth South Lancashire Rifles, under the command of Colonel R. W. H. Thomas, were early astir, travelling in two trains to Holywell, where they formed up for an eight miles march into can p. The morning was wet and wretched, and the otAlook was far from promising, but the men were in good spirits, everybody trusting that summer would come along some time during the camp fortnight to make life endurable. The cyclist section started, out for Widnes and the Transporter to make the journey by rcad, despite the copse- less rain. The headquarter compinies of the West Lancashire Royal Army Medical Corps, under Colonel R. Jackson, assembled at the station at. noon, and joined the train which brought the Blackpool and Kendal companies on their way to camp. The Telegraph and Field Service Companies of the West Lancashire Royal Engineers left the town early in the afternoon with full equipment, under Colonel Fraser. 31ajor Opperibeim commands the Telegraph Section, Major Taberner the Ist Field Service Company, and Lieutenant-Colonel Say ce the 2nd Field Company. The waggons and equipment of the Engineers were taken di ect to Caerwys station, within a mile and e half of the camp, and on arrival the men had a very busy time unshipping horses, waggons, pontoons, and stores, which will be at the disposal of the brigade during the exten- sive movemints that have been planned. In every case the men showed a great amount of keenness, and the camp promises to be of the most interesting description.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. A LaWiestion.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. A LaWiestion.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Council was held on Wednesday afternoon in the Tiewsey Chambers, Mr, R. Forshaw presided and there were present Dr. Murray Messrs. J. I3ackhouse, W. Houghton, A. Brookes, P. Longton, G. Mather, J. C. Sutton (clerk) B. S. New- combe (surveyor),. H.H. Burnage (assistant surveyor) Dr. Sepliton (medical officer), and F. Kerfoot (sanitary inspector). SPITTAL BR!DOP. _ _</p>
        <p>Ile CLERK said he had received a letter from the Clerk of the County Council recommending the application be made for to put in force compulsory powers in regard to the purchase of such land as was required for the purpose of enlarging Spittal Bridge or building a new one. It was en important matter ,I,ecause it involved a very round-about process but it seemed to be the only course open to the Council. In reply to the letter, he (the clerk) proposed to write to the County Council stating that consider- able correspondence extending over many months had passed between the City Bridge, master and the surveyor of the D:strict Council re the re building and ideningiof Spittal Bridge to meet present and future needs. 'Io effect the latter some small portions of land opening on the highway were necessary mid diffi- culties had arisen out of what the council considered to be exorbitant demands made by the owners. Finally the Council had concluded the enforcement of statutory power to be necessary, and he was instructed formally to ask the County Council to exercise their authority in the behalf. In conclusion the cleik wish to know who was liable for the costs of the arbitration proceedings. Mr. HOUGHTON moved that the letter be sent to the clerk of the County Council. The CHAIRMAN wished-to know whether the Council were satisfied that the demands of the landowners were exorbitant. SEVERAL MEMBERS: Oh, yes! .</p>
        <p>, . The CHAIRMAN said be bai reasen to believe that the demands wore not exorbitant, and that they might come to a settlement vi ith them.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROOKS said that Mr. Houghton and be had seen the landowners personally and they were convinced that no settlement could be come to stall. He admitted that Arbitration proceedings would be coLtly but he thought that as they bad other- schemes in hand they ought to go on with the'proceedings as they should let the people know th It the Counc.l was not going to be squeezed. In view of other schemes they, Lad in hand they ought to be able to get land at a reasonable price. The CHAIRMAN said he was still of the opinion that tbe_matter could I e settled %ithout going to aroitration.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER wanted to know whi-A-thet:- the Chairman knew something. AS he made such remarks as those he thought the Council ought to know if he knew anything. The CHAIRMAN said he only knew that there had been negotiations and he believed that the price asked was not exorbitant, and he would also remind the Council that Arbitration proceedings must be expenaive. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON said the- Council-was forced Into the present position owing to the fact that there was no possibility of accepting the terms of the landowners.</p>
        <p>It was an utter impossitility to agree with them. Dr. McirmAy wanted to know how long they had been over the negotiations. Mr. Ho lIGHTON : Three years. Mr. BACKHOUSE safd the only way by which a settle- ment could he arrived at was to pay tie full price that the landowLers asked for. • w The CLERK asked how much had been offered to the landowners. Mr. MATHER said he had offered a shilling a yard. The CHAIRMAN : Do you think that enough? Mr. MATHER : I think it is far more than it is worth. Dr. MURRAY said that as the matter had been going on for three years why hadn't the Chairman brought that up before. The CHAIRMAN: I have not come into this matter before. Dr. MURRAY : But you have been a member of the Council all the time. The CHAIRMAN: Don't you think that two shillings a yard is a reasonable price? ' Mr. MATHER : Nothing of the sort. The CHAIRMAN: I think it is a moat reasonable amount. • Mr. BROOKS: £5OO an acre is not reasonable at all. He hoped that the price (2500) would I e considered when the Finance Bill came into operaticn. Mr. HOUGHTON thought that asking such a price as that would adversely affect the assessment of land in that district in case the Financial Bill came into operation. Mr. BROOKES seconded and the motion was passed. ROYAL VISIT. Particulars of Councils claim for outlay upon main road in respect of the recent royal visit amounting to £222 Is. 24. were examined and signed by the General Purposes Committee, and were ordered to be placed before the county auditor with vouchers.</p>
      </div>
