      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Warrington Lodging-Houses:</head>
        <p>The Warrington Lodging-Houses:</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I crave —a little of your space to make a lew remarks upon the above subject? Generally speaking, it is not a pleasant thing to speak or write about, but through no fault of my own, I have unfortu- nately had to put up with the accommodation thus provided since .February last, and may therefore claim to have some knowledge of the subject.. Some of the houses are conducted in a tolerably iiecent manner, and discipline is maintained, but here, right in the heart of Warrington,l find at least one house -which is not conducted in such a manner. Here the lodgers are allowed to do just as they choose; here too, both sexes are allowed to mix together indis- criminately with the usual result that excessive drinking; profane and filthy language, petty quarrels and free fights are the order of the day, (or night as the case may be.) The direct result is. that in -nine cases out of ten, it is the innocent party which has to suffer. Can nothing be done to alter this state of things ? Why cannot the Town Council see to it that.the houses which are licensed by them, are conducted in an orderly and decent manner? There are lodging-house Inspectors here the same as in every other place. Are their hands tied that they do not seem to be so strict as in other places? My conten- tion is, that the Town Council being the licensing party are either directly or indirectly responsible for seeing that the houses are conducted in an orderly manner. They should also see that no license is granted to any person, man or woman, who is not of sober habits, and fully carable of maintaining order. In some of the houses I have been told the occupier does not care a straw whether a lodger washes him- self once a day or once a year; this is the way vermin is spread from place to place. With sincere apologies for thus trespassing upon your valuable space, and thanking you in anticipa- tion.—Yours faithfully, • Warrington. Augast 3rd, 1908</p>
        <p>FIAT LUX</p>
        <p>(3- ers - I 't . I',l 115111' t .4.4 1•4 ,i,, r.....,.....-. eft - - t=-----k- --• i :T il IP to the ..: _-7- E)--. --1., ITO%) ' ' . . 4,..._\., ~.._,. ~-....</p>
