      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT. SPECIAL MEETING OF THE TOWN</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT. SPECIAL MEETING OF THE TOWN</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Mayor's Action Condemned.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Warrington Town Counci called at the instance of five members (Councillors Lord, Dr. Joseph, Lashford, Halliwell, and Chantler) was held at the Town Hall on Thursday evening to consider the minutes of the Council of the sth July, and to pass such resolutions thereon as might be deemed necessary. The minutes ' in question had reference to the final arrangements for the Royal visit to the town on July 6th and to the appointment of a committee to select a gift suitable for presentation to Princess Victoria on the occasion of her birthday. This committee was never called together, tut a diamond necklace was presented to her Royal Highness on the occasion of the Royal vist. In the absetce of information, fifteen members of. the Council presented a requisition asking the Mayor to call a special meeting at which the subject might be discussed. This his Worship declined to io and five of the signatories to the requisition took the matter into their own hands and convened the meeting which was held on Thursday evening. There were present :--Alderman Bolton, Councillors Wilkinson, Fulton-Smith, Locker, Hawthorn Dr. Joseph, Lord, Pet.berton, Skelland, Halliwell;Ashcroft, Cunnington, Lashford, Dickenson, Broadhurst, Chantlers Sankey; Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. T. H. Spinks (assistant town clerk), and Mr. John Fairhurst (borough treasurer). On the motion of Councillor PEMBERTON, seconded by Councillor Assicnorr, Alderman Bolton was elected to the chair. Councillor LOCKER then moved that the Council go into committee, but that the ieporters and public be allowed to be present. The TOWN CLERK: There is no need to go into committee then. The CHAIRMAN : In committee members can speak as often as they like. A VOICE: Free discussion. The TOWN CLERK: Is thßt your object? Councillor LOCKER: Yes. Councillor ASIICRoFT seconded. Councillor FULTON-SMITH : I don't understand the regulations, but I should like whatever we do to be in order. Alderman BOLTON: We are perfectly in order in resolving into committee. The only question is as to whether the Council will allow the Press to be present. The resolution was then agreed to ; Councillor Fulton-Smith being the only dissentient. The CHAIRMAN: I- don't know whether you wish the reporters to report all that is said. Several MEMBERS: We don't mind. The CHAIRMAN : Then it is a matter for the reporters to please themselves. Councillor LASHEORD then moved: "That this Council views with regret end alarm the action of the Mayor in ignoring certain lawful resolutions unani- mously passed at the special Cotncil meeting of July sth, 1909.' He said he must preface his remarks by stating that his loyalty to the King and Royal family i was second to none n this town or the country at large. It was not his intention to discuss the minutes in detail, but only in so far as they had reference to the Mayor's actions in ignoring certain of the minutes and thereby setting at defiance the wishes of the Council. As one of the youngest members of the Council, he considered his Worship's notion very- arbitrary, domineering and unjust, and he strongly pro- tested against it as a dangerous precedent which could not be tolerated. Councillor HALLIWELL seconded. Councillor PEMBERTON said this was the first time in the history of the Council that a special requisition signed by mernbcrs of the Council had been refused. He regretfully associated himself with the resolution moved by Councillor Lashford. They had in that Cor- poration certain standing crders which were equally binding on every member and official, whatever his position might be. No man in the Corporation was above the standing orders. Not only had they been broken in ore case but in several cases, and it was a danger to public busineas and to the ratepayers of Warrington when such a system was allowed to go on. Once it was inaugurated, it was beyond the wit of man to tell where it would stop. He felt with reference to that special meeting that most unfair comments had been made in the Press. The gentlemen who called the meeting in the first place did so coolly and calmly. Thdre was no rush or excite- ment and they were careful how they drafted the requisition. Some people had circulated in the town and. in the Press certain libellous and slanderous state- ments, and it must have been obvious to anycne who read the proceedings of :july sth that those statements were entirely wrong. They were lying statements without the semblance of truth, and he felt most indignant about them. At the previous meeting certain resolutions were passed without a dis- sentient voice. When straight forward honourable gentlemen were to be attacked, in some cases behind their backs and in others publicly, it was monstrous and scandalous. Further, the amount of intimidation brought to bear on members of the Council was another abomination. (Hear, hear.) It would be a sad time for the country when certain influential people were able to terrorise or to stop the members from doing what they considered right in the public interest. He was pleased to think that the thajority of the Council were there in theeaublio interest, to serve without fear or favour the public at large. They did poet care about individuals or influential people. They were there to do their duty and whatever threats were made he would continue to do that. While he had breath in his body he would proclaim what he considered right in the interests of the borough. They knew with what great difficulty this affair had been brought through. Like Councillor Lashford, be would not take second place to anybody in the town in his loyalty to the King and Queen; and anyone who questioned that did so with a purpose. Councillor Dr. Joserer said he would like it to be understood that he had no personal feeling against his Worship the Mayor. He bad joined tbat movement very reluctantly but he had a sense of duty that compelled him to do it, and on that account he bad taken on himself a very disagreeable duty. He, like the previous ipeakers, felt called upon to do what he never thought be would have to do in his life, and that was to avow his loyalty. He had thought his loyalty was above suspicion ; it always had been tip to now. He was second to no one in the Kingdom in his loyalty to the King, the Royal family, and the throne. Nothing that he and those associated with him in the movement had said or done could be interpreted by tbe greatest stretch of imagination as approaching dis- loyalty in the question. It was on the part of those people who had spread unfounded, inaccurate and mis- leading reports in the Press. He bad very grave doubts as to whether one of the reports, at all events, was not libellous. The history of their, grievance was this: At a special meeting on the sth Jirly certain resolutions— numbers four and five—were passed. Those resolutions</p>
        <p>were utterly ignored by the Mayor. Fifteen members sent in a requisition for a special meeting and that was ignored. They did not get an answer to it, but they saw through the Press, their only means of information that the Mayor did not intend to grant them that privilege—he (the speaker) called it a right. At the ordinary meeting of the Council he (Dr. Joseph) asked the Mayor as courteously as he could whether he put. purposed to make any statement or explanation. He got an answer, with the scarcely veiled sneer. " You will have a future occasion to discuss it to yourheart's content." He thought that was very bad treatment. especially as he had taken great care to conch his language in the mostcourteous manner possible, so that the Mayor could have no cause of offence. The next thing that happened in the history of the matter, was the report of the Reception Committee, which was sif)- mitted to the General Purposes Committee. He had great respect for the members of the Council, but he had not much respect for the General Purposes Com- mittee. He looked upon it as a place where no doubt some good work was done, but where there was a great deal of error and imperfect work. Funeral orations of various kinds were made and schemes were buried, sometimes to be revived and sometimes not. The report purported to be. signed by a certain number of gentlemen, but it was not signed by some of them and in that respect it was not correct. He put it to the Council as to whether they had been -treated properly in having their resolutions ignored by the Mayor. It seemed so him,tbat it struck at the very root of proper government, and made the difference between an absolute monarchy and a sound, just, constitutional government such as we possessed in this coUntry. The same rule applied to Parliamentary Government and if it were ignored they would have anarchy at once. The members who had called that meeting might be looked upon as anarchists, but if their conduct could be stigmatised as such, be was proud to be an anarchist. He felt his position as a Councillor,after this procedure on the part of the Mayor,„, was very much wanting in dignity, but if be were to remain silent and not protest against it, he should feel he had not only lost dignity but a great deal of his self-respect. The interests and privileges of all the members of the Council were involved and he failed see why so many Were absent from the meeting. He did not think they bad been treated as reasonable, sensible men, worthy of being representatives of their fellow-townsmen. They had been treated more like marionettes and autoiratons than anything else. If he (the speaker) were to consult his own ease and comfort he would remain out of the conflict in which he had little to gain and perhaps .sornethins to lose, but he never shirked anything yet, however disagreeable or dangerous, and he did not intend to begin. He would like to say, not with any ill-feeling but rather as smart- ing urc!er the indignity of it, that certain editorial notes might have been better if they had been used less partially. But even those notes bad not frightened him from standing up and expressing his opinion. It was not the first time that particular paper had been used against a particular side and he thought there was not that discrimination, which there ought to be as to whether the side that they went for was right. Councillor HAWTHORN said that as one of the mem- bers who signed the requisition to the Mayor in the first instance, he would like to arty that the main object he had in view was to speed the end of a dis- cussion which was going on in the town with regard to the Corporation expenditure in- connection with the Royal visit. Rumours were afloat at that time which had little foundation in fact and if the Mayor had acceded to their request, called a special meeting and fully discussed the matter, it would have put an end once and for all to a very unseemly discussion. The Mapes refusal to call a meeting has tended to prolong a disagreeable discussion and also to embitter it to some extent. If the Mayor had presented a full state- ment of accounts and given an explanation at the special meeting which was asked for two or three weeks ago, it would bava put an end to dissensions. The main objection to the Mayor's action was not so much to what was done on the occasion of the Royal visit as to the way in which he did it. In the absence of any statement from the Mayor, it was hardly fair to criticise him in his absence, but he might have adepted a less cavalier action with regard to several members of the Council. Councillor LORD said he was sorry that certain articles had appeared in the outside Press on different occasions and with striking headlines. He entirely exonerated anyone actively connected with the local Press from responsibility for them. What the object of the articles had been, unless it was to drag Warrington through the mud and to cast a slur upon Royalty, he did not know but he deprecated them, and in fact strong action had been taken with regard to one paper and they had bad an unqualified apology. He strongly objected to the action of the Mayor. It was not his (the speaker's) desire to criticise the Royal Visit or the gift to Princess Victoria—in fact he believed that he was the one who proposed that a suitable present should be given to her. They had na quarrel with the King or the Royal family. He did not think there was a single gentleman present but who war as loyal as anyone in the country. Their quarrel was with the action of the Mayor. He could not for the life of him understand why his worship had taken that action. He had ignored the request of fifteen mem- bers of the Council, and others would have signed the requisition if they bad been asked. He had refused to call a meeting and the result was that five members, who were entitled by law on to call a 'meeting insisted on one being held. He (the speaker) for one would not tolerate the Mayor's domineering attitude. He was ' there as a representative of the ratepayers, not as a tool, or a dummy to be made a fool of by the Mayor or anyone else and he strongly resented it. He was sorry to have to take up that posi'tion. He had been a personal friend of the Mayor and had worked for him and helped to put him in the position he now held in the town, but he could not tolerate his action in this I matter.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he was not present at the meet- ing on July sth so he could norsay whether the resolu- tions were carried out or not. He thought the voting of an amount of money to the Mayor as salary hid probably been the mistake at the bottom of the trouble. If the Council bad not done that, they would simply have paid the bills themselves and the whole thing would have been better. He considexed the Mayor had acted rather discourteously. Councillor WILKINSON, Who said he had broken his holPays in order to be present, pointed out that what they had been called together to discuss was the attitude of the Mayor, and nothing with reference to the Royal family. They wished to show the Mayor of 'Warrington and all future Mayors that they as mem- bers of the Council were not going to tolerate any viblation in the slightest degree of their standing orders. He (the speaker) bad been somewhat hurt at the attitude the Mayor had taken up. Had he had the tact to call the spec:al meeting the whole matter would have been at an end. The reports he had seen in various papers had also hurt him as an old Warringtonian, and had pricked him very keenly. Outsiders seemed to have got the idea that all their grievance had arisen because they had spent a bit of money. There were gentlemen round that table who would not have cared if it had cost twice the amount. It was not that they bad met to protest against, but to show their disapproval of the attitude of the Mayor, and to let it be an object lesson for future Mayors. Councillor LOCKER said they were all proud of the King and Queen and members of the Royal Family. As a member of the Reception Committee, it gave him (the speaker) the utmost pleasure to see that everything possible was done to show their Majesties.how loyal the people of Warrington were. There was no question of stinting the expenditure. On July sth they decided upon a certain course, no matter -what the cost might be ; they were proposed to go to any extent. They did not curtail the Mayors power in any shape or form. They only wished to be taken into his confidence and allowed their share in showing their good feeling towards the King and Queen. If the Mayor had taken that course imme- diately be found things had not been altogether right, the trouble would have been avoided. The speaker expressed his sorrow to see the articles referred to in certain newspapers, which tended to give outsiders the impression that the Council were not altogether proud of the amount the visit bad cost, whereas that was the last thought in their minds. Councillor BROADHURST said they were proud of the good old town of Warrington and of the 'Royal family, and they would not take second place to any town in the country in that respect. Councillor FULTON SMITH said he was in doubt as to whether the prccedure would not have been much more dignified if a resolution had been placed on the agenda for the ordinary monthly meeting instead of calling a special meeting. He failed yet to see where the griev- ance was, but of course he might be dense. He did not feel that they were going in the right legal way. Had a resolution been put on the ordinary agenda it could have been discussed in pliblic and everyone would have felt that they had taken up a dignified position. The following voted for the resolution Alderman Bolton, Councillors Locker, Skelland, Ailacroft, Dr. Joseph, Wilkinson, Lord, Halliwell, Dickenson, Chantler, Cunnington, Pemberton, Hawthorn and Lashford-14. No one voted against the resolution which was declared carried unanimously. The CHAIRMAN saichWarrington was as loyal as any town in England and the money spent on decorations gave pleasure to everyone. The only grievance they had was that they had not been taken into confidence and allowed to do their share. A lot of the business had been carried out by doing without them and then saying "If you den't like it you can leave it." The resolution was again put to the meeting as a Council and declared to be carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>KILLED BY A CRICKET BALL.</head>
        <p>KILLED BY A CRICKET BALL.</p>
        <p>While witching a game of cricket at Broomfield Park' North London, a lad named Thomas John Come (13) was struck on the head by the ball. He fell down unconscious, and died the next day. • At Thursday's inquest a verdict of "Accidental death" was returned A fountain-pengin the pocket of a rich New York merchant, Mr. W. B. Craig,. on Wednesday saved his life by deflecting the bullet amecl at him by a woman. The "Telegraph" correspondent says Mr. Craig was so cool that he did not take his cigarette from his mouth until be reached the office door.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard ana is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.— WHITE &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renahaw at., opposite top of Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 7, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 7, 1909.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH FINANCE.</p>
        <p>HOW THE RATEPAYERS" MONEY IS SPENT.</p>
        <p>The Borough Treasurer (Mr. John Fairhurst, F.S.A.A.), has just issued his annual abstract of the accounts of the Corporation of Warrington for the year ended 31st March, 1909. The volume comprises_ _ _ _</p>
        <p>165 pages and contains a mlss of useful information as to how the ratepayers' money is spent. THE BALANCE SHEET—WARRINGTON'S INDEBTEDNESS.</p>
        <p>Accompanying the accounts is an aggregate balance statement of the liabilities and assets of the Corpora- tion on the 31st March last. The total liabilities -are set forth as amounting to £1,285,540 2s. id. and the assets to £1,805,401 ss. 3d. leaving an excess of assets of £519,951 3s. 2d. The balance sheet shows the liabilities and assets in connection with • the following funds : _</p>
        <p>...--- . Property , Assets and LiHbilities. Outlay. £ s. d. £ s. d. Borongh Rate 75,245 7 1 143,208 15 2 Improvement Rate Sewers Rate Museum and Library Education Middleton Sdholarship Parr Hall 6Ol 7 6 9,643 3 8 ?lice Sup'rannuat i'n Fund 6 9 8 16,626 12 5 P'v'te Pprovem't Expenses 16,685 10 1 16,685 10 1 Gas Wori.s Water Works 404,996 15 4 460,858 " 6 0 Electricity - 91,204 5 3 107,570 15 2 Tramways Fire Engine Renewal 509 17 8 Luchford and Wildet spool Causeways 591 18 11 Walker's Gift 395 11 6</p>
        <p>1,285,510 2 1 1,805,491 5 3</p>
        <p>The position of the various Corporation under- takings and departments is fully set forth. During the year the markets again proved a profitable source of revenue, the total receipts being £5,438 17s. 2d., and the expenditure £2,176 18s. 10d., leaving a profit of £3,261 18s. 4d. The income of the hospital ilepartment totalled £587 3s. 7d., consisting chiefly of the amounts paid by other authori- ties for the maintenance of their patients in the institution. The expenditure amounted to £3,800 18s. sd. In addition the smallpox hospital involved an expenditure of 077 3s. sd. and in the Health Department there was an outlay of £2,293 17s 2d. and an income of £255 13s.</p>
        <p>In the Sanitary Works Department the expenses in connection with the Longford Manure Works amounted to £3,855. The intercepting depots cost £939 18s. 10d nightsoil collection department £7,693 3s. 10d., and scavenging department £3BO 19s. Bd. The sale of concentrated manure brought in £3,505 18s. sd. Highway work in Warrington during the year cost £17,660 Bs. 3d. and the receipts, largely for work done for other Corporation departments, totalled £11,891 Bs. id. _</p>
        <p>The amount paid to the Electricity Department for yeai's lighting of the streets was £5,211 7s. 7d., and to the Electricity Department for the lighting of main streets, £620. Purchase of land and compensa- tion in Barbauld street cost £3O; Lower Wash Lane,. £25;, O'Leary street, £9 13s. GI Trees, guards, stakes, &amp;c., and planting trees accounted for £3l `2s. 3d. In connection with the Bridge-street improvements £167 6s. 4d. was paid in valuers' fees; £713 7s, id. for the erection of shops and purchase of party wails; £1,049 14s. 7d. to the Highway Department for the demolition of property and paving. The expenditure in connection with the Museum and Library amounted to £1;993 15s. and the chief item (f income was £5B 16s. sd. paid in fines by borrowers. The department receives the proceeds of a I.ld. rate (11,523 1)s. - 6d.) and a" special grant of £126 Bs. 10d.for branch libraries,and it has ended the year with a balance in hand of £l4l 13s. 4d. Elementary education in the Borough has during the year cost £41,557 Os. 2d.' the largest item of expenditure being 129,501 18s for teachers' salaries. The amount raised from the Borough rate for elementary education.was £15,531 15s. Sd. and grants , from the Board of Education totalled £25,354 19s. 5d., School fees brought In £2,182 10s. 7d. On higher education the expenditure was £7507 Gs. 7d., includ- ing £1,613 17s. 10d. for salaries of teachers at the Secondary school and the Pupil Teacher's Centre, and £2,469 153. Od. for salaries of teachers at evening schools. The ratepayers contributed £2,597 15s. 7d. to this department, £9OB 19s. 4d. was received as the exchequor contribution account, and £3,126 15s. from the Board of Education. Fees realised £642 14s. 6d. Twelve months' profit on the Parr Hall amounted to £92 14s. Bd., the amount received for the hire of the hall being £549. The gas works accounts show that the gross profit for the year amounted to £21,679 10s. 80. From this must be deducted a year's annuities and inter eat amounting to 15,193175. 3d. and £3,098 Os. 3d. trans- ferred to sinking fund,whilesthe sum paid to the relief of the rates from the profits of the previous year was £11,500.</p>
        <p>The gross profit on the waterworks undertaking amounted to 119,595 19s. 24. Payments for interest and annuities absorbed £13,250 2s. Bd. and contribu- tion to sinking fund £4,693 is. 10d. The balance in hand at the end of the year was £4,034 Bs. lid. In connection with the Lawson Arbitration the following expenses were incurred :—Costs, fees, &amp;c., £224 Os. 11d. ; expenses of witnesses, ecc., £9O 18s.; printing, stationery, &amp;c., £9 10s. 2d' The total working expenses of the ejectricity department during the year amounted to £9,318 2s. 10d. A contribution of £3,110 17s. id. was made to the sinking fund and the loans repaid amounted to £3,321 16s. The net' profit of £942 12s. Bd, was carried to the reserve funds.</p>
        <p>The net profit- on the tramways amounted to 12,370, which has been carried to the reserve fund., The total revenue was £19,580, and the working expenses (contracts estimated) £11,455. The pasJse9n4g. ersThcearrfiaedr es received nltl,o i,s. d im,b3e2reds ,60n. Costs &amp;c. re Warrington's opposition to the Liver- pool Corporation Bill amounted to £370 18s. 4(1. The up-keep of the public bowling green at victoria Park and the wages of the caretaker amounted to £65 3s. id., and £53 Bs. was taken in receipts from players. The expenditure on the ba thwas £1,036 3s. 6d.,and the total income was £65 18s. 5d., including £552 9s. 4d., by receipts from bathers and £9O paid by the Education Department for use of baths and instruction of scholars. The Watch Departments expenditure totalled/8,048 us. 7d., and exceeded the receipts by £3,946 7e.. 9d. The largest item is for constables wages which' absorb £5,953 19s. 11(1,</p>
        <p>In the Cemetery Department the expenditure amounted to £1,190 Os. 3d., and the income to £1519 18s. 4d.</p>
        <p>Expenses in the Town. Hall department totalled £1,083 us. 7d„ and the income £309 38.10 d. In the Parks Department the receipts were £IIS 10s. Bd. and the expenditure amounted to £685 is. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "Why Men Love Women."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "Why Men Love Women."</p>
        <p>The holiday- attraction at this theatre has been Walter Howard's favourite romantic Ay " Why men love Women" and large audiences have received it with enthusiasm. Mr. Howard is a master ofmelodrama and "Why men love Women" has proved one of his greatest successes. The centre of action is India and the gorgeousness and colour for which that land is famed are brilliantly depicted in some of the finest scenes ever . presented on the Warrington stage. Miss Ellen Snow's company is . a thoroughly capable one and the performance goes . with a splendid swing from beginning to end, the excite- ment of the audience being worked up to a high, pitch. Miss Ellen Snow as Violet Livingstone is a winsome heroine and makes the most of an excellent part. She enlists the sympathy of the audience-from the, first moment, and her whole-hearted and forceful portrayal wins rounds of applause. Mr. Oliver Rogers is a dashing and impulsive Gerald Fielding, and he too proves a great favourite. Mr, W. P. Mansfield gives a convincing study of the unpopular role of Captain Staniloff, and Mr. Stirling Gordon deserves commendation for a really clever presentment of Chundra Singh, the native doctor. There are many welcome humorous touches. Mr. Archie Watts as the eccentric Orlando Figgins, creating great "merriment by his attempts to escaps from the attentions of his loving spouse. The orchestra, under ,the direction of Mr. Norman Latham, gives some light selections, with good taste. - NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME..</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. William Bowne's pow-e—riul drama "Man to Man " will be presented by Messrs. Edward Lloyd and Clifford Bowne's Comp any.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP.</p>
        <p>A Successful Picnic,</p>
        <p>The prettily-situated Warrington Children's Summer Camp at Prestatyn was the scene of events of unusual interest on Tuesday. It was a-happy inspiration whieh prompted the committee to arrange an annual picnic, in order that subscribers and friends might gain practical acquaintance with the excellent work of the camp, and the splendid success which attended the first outing of the kind on Tuesday, was gratifying and encouraging. Fortunately the weather was genial and enabled all who made the journey to spend a pleasant time at the picturesque Welsh resort. What rendered the occasion exceptionally interesting was the fact that the Mayor performed the ceremony of opening the new administrative offices which have been erected by Messrs. R. A. Naylor Ltd., - Warrington. For some years the committee have felt the need of a building which might be utilized for the resid- ence of the superintendent lady or gentleman in charge for the time being, for committee meetings, secretarial work, and if necessary, for the accommoda- tion of some of the officers. It is also intended that when the children are not in camp the new building may be placed at the disposal of convalescents requiring rest and quiet that being one of the primary objects of the development in this direction The party, which left Arpley station by the 12.20 (noon) train, included the Mayor and Mayoress (Alder- man and Mrs. ForshaW), Mr. and Mrs. R. Pierpoint, Alderman and Mrs, Smethurst, Mr. and Mrs. J. H. Smethurst, Mrs. LOngland, Mrs. Clegg, Mrs. Houghton, Mr. Beswick, Mr. and Mrs. Ransome, Mr. J. Harding, Iliss Harding, Mrs. Harris, Mrs. Hall, Mr. T. McWhirter, Mr. and Mrs. McWhirter, Miss J. McWhirter, Miss L. McWhirter, Mrs. Denham, Mr. and Mrs. F. H. Aukland, Mrs. Glover, Mrs. Locker, Mrs. Lindley, Mr. and Mrs. Breward, Mr. and Mrs. T. E. Gibson, Mrs. Boyle, Miss Baynes, Miss Cannell, Mr. F. J. Emson, Mrs. Mounfield, Mr. James Cummins, Mr.. and Mrs. J. Moore-Murray, Mrs. Lowe, Mrs. Harold Smith, and Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins (hon. secte- tary). On reaching- the camp the visitors at once sat down to a substantial luncheon. The hon. secretary had made admirable arrangements, in which he had had the hearty co-operation of many ladies and gentlemen. Gifts of flowers and other ornamenta- tions bedecked the tables and the pavilion was beautifully embellished with floral displays and festoons of varied colours and designs. At the entrance gate of the camp the boys to the number of over 100, were lined up and received the Mayor and his party with hearty cheers of welcome. After luncheon all betook themselves to the new building which was to be opened, the boys forming a large semi-circle while the visitors were accommodated on the verandah in front of the administrative block.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR, addressing the asssembly, said he would first thank the boys for the hearty reception which they had given the Mayoress and himself On coming into the camp. He was delighted to see them looking so healthy and so happy. In suitable terms his Worship commented upon the excellence of the' institution and the manifest advantage arising from its work in such a worthy direction, for it provided a holiday treat for the children and helped to develop their physique and general well-being, He extended his heartiest congratulations to the hon. secre- tary on having secured such a beautiful site, commanding such lovely views of the Welsh hills and being in close proximity to the sea. He trusted that the children of Warrington would ever feel that a deep debt of gratitude was due to Mr. Jenkins for his energetic and indefatigable efforts in the establishment of the camp. It was with great pleasure that he (the Mayor) had come down for the purpose of opening the new building and he trusted that in every way the scheme would prosper. His worship then unlocked the door and formally declared the administrative offices open, the assembly enthusi- astically applauding. Mr. ROBERT PIERPOINT, in proposing a vote of thanks to the Mayor, said he need not tell them how ready and willing his worship always was'to take part in the ceremonial duties of his office, and he could assure them that anyone who occupied that office in a town like Warrington,- could not allow the grass to grow under his feet. The Mayor had already wished prosperity to those administrative officers. The building provided accommodation for the superintend- ent and at times for the officers in charge of the children, the committee and the secretary, where they could pursue their work free from interruption and the noise which he sincerely hoped the boys would always make. (Applause). The camp had proved remarkably useful and was not only a place for the giving of health to the little girls and boys,but also provided accommodation for grown-up people who came there to get fresh air, health.and rest, and he understood that the intention of the committee; as time went on and the prosperity of the place increased was that it might actually become, a convalescent home when it was not used by the boys and girls. (Applause.) Ur. RAN:4O3IE, in seconding, said that as lion. treasurer he remembered to bring his receipt book with him, and if any of them wished to help the fund he would be very pleased to give them a receipt. The building had got to be paid for. They had no special building fund. It was a Warrington building erected by a Warrington firm and, used by Warrington people, and they hoped that Warrington people would assist in supporting the camp funds so that they might pay for the building with as little delay as possible. The new building enabled them to use more of the provision huts for the children, and they hoped to erect more huts next year. With their present kitchen accommodation, they could then cater fo'r a good many more than they had done in the past. It depended on the generosity of their friends to enable them to extend the work and give more poor children a holiday. The building had been designed and erected by Messrs. R. A. Naylor Ltd., who had given the committee kindly consideration and generous terms. He thanked the Mayor and Mayoress for being good enough to come down and open the building which would be one of great usefulness and comfort for those upon whose shoulders fell a large proportion of the practical working of the camp. He trusted that those renewed and extended efforts for greater usefulness would meet with the encourage- ment which the committee and officials hoped for. (Applause). The vote was enthusiastically passed and the open- ing ceremony concluded. The attractions of the afternoon included a cricket match between elevens captained by Mr. E. J. Mee and Mr. Ben Jolley. The result was a victory for Mr. Jolley's side by 30 runs. to 21. The party returned by the_7.3 train, all being delighted with the outing and thoroughly convinced of the usefulness and good management. of the War- rington Children's Summer Camp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Week's Doings.</head>
        <p>The Week's Doings.</p>
        <p>The first batch of boys left Warrington at 10.28 am., on Saturday last for a week% stay at the camp. The boys were in charge of Mr. and Mrs. E. P. Cox, who were assisted, by Messrs. B. Jolley, E. Jackson Mee, A. E. G. Cox, F. W. Povey, T. Groarke, and W. B. Jolley. Camp was 'reached at 12.50 p.m. and a good hot meal had been prepared by Mr. Thomason. Having done ample justice to the dinner, the boys were placed in their various huts and then spent the rest of the day on the shore wading and in the camp grounds. They were early astir on Sunday morning although the weather was anything but promising. Several showers fell in the morning, while from noon to six o'clock it poured incessantly. The children were, however, able to attend their respective places of worship in the morning. • On Monday there was a vast improvement in the weather, and the children visited the beautiful falls at Dyserth. The singing of popular songs lessened the tedious nature of the walk, and the happy appearance of the boys was frequently commented- upon by holiday makers along the roads. On Thursday morning a detachment went to Rhyl while the remainder stayed in camp saving them- selves for their Athletic festival which was to take place in the afternoon. In the afternoon these sports were held and aroused great . interest as there were nine events and a very pleasant afternoon was spent. Mrs. Lowe, of Warrington,kindly presented the prizes -Which were useful articles of clothing. •The following is a list of the 'prize winners :- 80 Yards Fla Race (boys under 11 years).-,---1, Chariton; 2,3forris ; 3, Whitehouse. -</p>
        <p>50 Yards Sack Race (boys over 11 years).--I,Harris; 2, Riley; 3, Brooks.</p>
        <p>100 Yards Flat Race (no age limtt).-1, Ryder; 2, Astall ' • 3, Martin. One Lap Obstacle Yace (no age limit).-4, Doyle; 2, Houghton; 3, Ryder. Lacing the shoe Race (no age limit).-1, Gregg; 2, Callon • 3 Gartland. - -</p>
        <p>Foul: L 4 Race (no age hmit),-1,-Ryder ; 2,Callon; 3, Litherland.</p>
        <p>Flag Squadron Race.-1, Hut No. 7; s 2, Hut No. 8</p>
        <p>Three-legged Race (no age Brooks and Shaw; 2, Morgan and Riley; 3, Lowe and Burland.</p>
        <p>. ..., . . The next day was a red letter„ day, 'the report of which will be found above.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning the lads were taken for a mountain climb. Going through the ,village of Prestatyn they ascended the mountain 'leading to Gwynaescor, and then returned to camp by way of Meliden. This is a very arduous and fatiguing walk, but the lads did not seem to be affected very much as they were so interested in the surrounding scenery. The afternoon was spent in wading in the sea. On Friday morning the boys went on their usual toy-buying expedition to the village. They left Prestatyn at 7.3 p.m„ reaching Bank Quay Station about 8.20 p.m., after having spent a most enjoyable week.</p>
        <p>The Superintendent wishes it to be known that the sports were held Owing to the generosity of Tuesday's visitors.</p>
        <p>One Warrington lady left instructions for one of the boys to be provided with new clothes and clogs. The boy reached Warrington in high glee last night in his new clothes.</p>
      </div>
