      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>BUDGET OPPOSITION COLLAPSES.</head>
        <p>BUDGET OPPOSITION COLLAPSES.</p>
        <p>THE TURN OF THE TIDE. --- Unionist Press Admissions.</p>
        <p>SIR HENRY NORMAN SAYS THE BATTLE HAS BEEN WON.</p>
        <p>A remarkable change has come over some of the most prominent of the Opposition journals this week in their attitude towards the Budget. On Wednesday a Political Note in the " Times" indicated a turn of the tide in favour of the Government's much-denounced proposals. The "Morning Post" declares that the fight on the land clauses is practically over, and advises the Unionist leaders to take stock of the position as it exists to-day. The" Daily Mail "has suddenly found that the Budget has quite a number of good points to commend it to the public, and that the agitation against it has fallen flat except in the City of London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>LORD NORTHCLIFFE IMPRESSED.</head>
        <p>LORD NORTHCLIFFE IMPRESSED.</p>
        <p>There may be some explanations (say—s--t-li-e-" West- minister Gazette") both of the " Times " admission that the tide has turned, and of the attitude of the " Daily Mail" in a note by" P. W. W." (Mr. P. W. Wilson, M.P.) in the" Daily News." He writes : " lam told that on Tuesday evening Lord North- cliffe visited the lobbies and conversed at length with prominent persons in the Conservative party, of which he is a notable asset. He is said to have expressed the opinion that the Budget is popular, that the Opposition is making a great mistake in denouncing it, and that the Tories would be wise to let the thing slip through at the earliest opportunity. The Budget Protest League has been a fantastic failure, but judging from the "Daily Mail," it has been Mr. Lloyd George's speech at Mile End that chiefly impressed Lord Northcliffe. One detects his handiwork in the strange paragraph with which the Political Notes of the "Times" was headed yesterday." In these discouraging circumstances (continues the "Westminster Gazette ") it is not surprising that the "Morning Post" should regret the absence of "the men who have been fighting the Finance Bill with so much pertinacity." It does not complain—" it would be most unjust to complain, of their absence; they more ,than deserve the rest they are taking before the struggles in Committee are resumed." But, and here is the really pc+thetic point, "these gentlemen would be tie first to discourage any idea of Eurrenier. " BATTLE ,WON. BUDGET LEAGUE SANGUINE.</p>
        <p>To ascertain the opinion of the Budget League upon this latest development, a correspondent of the "West- minster Gazette" sought Sir Henry Norman, M.P., the hon. secretary, who at once declared that, in his opinion, the Battle of the Budget was won. "You will remember," he said," that when the Budget Leaps wSstßrted I told _9ll in an iatArview it was Lunded in the confident belief that the Badge needed only to be understood to be enthusiastically supported. "We have since then covered the country with meet- ings of all classes. Eminent Cabinet Ministers, eloquen- members of Parliament, and hundreds upon hundreds of unofficial speakers have addressed them; gramophones have given forth speeches from the party leaders; and three million pamphlets and leaflets have been dis- tributed all over the land. „ -</p>
        <p>"In other worth, the Budget has been explained, and explained thoroughly, and now it is being enthusiastic- ally supported. "Mr. Winston Churchill's great speech in Edinburgh, with his masterly exposition of the Budget, the extra- ordinary meetings of the Prime Minister in the City of London, and his deeply impressive addresses—how impressive only those who heard them can tell; and Mr. Lloyd George's positively annihilating attack at Limehouse the other day, have marked a most impor- tant step in what is obxiously a winning campaign. "And now to-day, with the right-about-face' of the 'Daily Mail,' following the hoisting of the white flag by the Ths Times' yesterday, I repeat that the Battle of the Budget his been won.' "FLYING ENEMY ACROSS A STRICKEN FIELD."</p>
        <p>"May I venture also to remind you," Sir Henry went on," that when you called upon me exactly eight days ago I told you that the supporters of the Budget were pursuing a flying enemy across a stricken field. At the time you doubtless thought that was the language of enthusiasm, if not of exaggeration. To-day it is abundantly clear that it was a statement of fact. 'The advantages of the Budget are set out in such a masterly and popular form in the 'Daily Mail 'to-day i that the question n this office is whether we should not reprint the article from what has hitherto been our most bitter opponent as one of our own Budget Lelgue leaflets.' . •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-07_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>UNITED IRISH LEAGUE. Presentation to Father Walshe.</head>
        <p>UNITED IRISH LEAGUE. Presentation to Father Walshe.</p>
        <p>Tuesday last was a day ever to be remembered by the members of the United Irish League, and •the many friends of Father Walshe, late of the Sacred Heart Church, Warrington. On that day the rev, gentleman at a smoking concert in the League rooms, was the recipient of a trap. value 50 guineas, which had been subscribed by his friends and fellow members. Vather Walshe has only been in Warrington about a year, but his influence for good his been considerable. He has left Warrington to take up work at Cahir, co. Tipperary, and he will letive with the heartiest good wishes of all who know him.</p>
        <p>Dr. J. D. LISTON, the president of the branch took the chair end in the course of an appronriate speech was most eulogistic in his remarks about Father Walshe. He described him as a zealous priest and a true patriot of Ireland. Probably they in Warrington and certainly the members of the local branch owed more to the rev, gentleman than it was possible to repay. He bad come to thena at a very critical stage of their existence and by his good judgment and whole heartedness he had lifted their position to what it was to-day. He (the speaker) was only speaking the mind of every member when he said that his leaving would be keenly felt, but they would be inspired by what he had done for them and endeavout always to keep to thit high standard. He wished Father Walshe to accept the pre- sentation rsa tribute and offered him their best wishes in his new sphere of labour. He hoped that he would have good health and if he ever were sent on the English mission Warrington would hare his services. (Applause). Mess. Charles Roddy, J. Byrne andP. Conway (vice- president of the league) alsa spoke in felicitous terms of Father Walshe.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Fsthr WALSHE was visibly moved when he replied. He said considering the very short time he had been amongst the people of Warrington it cryne as a very great surprise to receive such a handsome present. He wes deeply grateful to them for their expressions and for the beautiful present they had made him. Nothing could have been more suitable for his work in the new sphere to which he was going. No matter how far be might be from them he would always remember their actidn, and their generous appreciation of his labours would be a sti inulant to further effort. He had been blamed for identifying himself with the national cause, but he would not be true to his country if he did not use all the influence he 'could to attain the end for which they were warkin,g. (Applause.) In conclusion the speaker entreated his hearers to be faithful to the officers and officials of the local organisation. To those</p>
        <p>ltl who belonged to the same creed as himself he tis them to be true to their Church and live °</p>
        <p>Christian lives. the The smoking concert was of a very high order ~00. questi_te, artistes distinguishing themselves by their un able ability. The Warrington Male Voice Glee P3,1-1:t, consisting of Messrs. J. Sefton, T. Newns, T. Wirigiir. W. Dutton, J. Mather, C. Sinclair, J. Hannan and eit Barnes rendered several items which received jp merited applause. The pieces rendered were not absence," How can I bear to leave thee," and maog little Shamrock." Messrs. W. Dutton and J.Sefton,so,o the duet "The battle axe." "Jolley good compan9L.s sung by Mr. Sefton. Mr. Donovan of Ditton :'";rb prime favourite and sang "Father 0' Flynn; re s' Donovans," and "The Irish Emigrant." Mr. Bork,e „pi dered " From the green Emerald Isle," and "In u'roo- movements" in his usual style. Councillor Toole tributed " The Boys of Wexford," and "The Welrint'atid the Green." The accompanists Messrs. Newnt Barnes proved their efficiency in that office. ALltes M. Crowe proposed a vote of thanks to the ar'"go and this was seconded by Mr. P. Conwal.,oo- - and Mr. Newns suitably respondei. t'Auto cillor Toole proposed and Mr. limes Fay sennil" the vote of thanks to the clisirman. At the, conclusion whole assembly sang" God save Ireland."</p>
